        <p>NATION, 1.,111,./tEll. For example, the Exchequer contribut tun thin year has fallen short of our estimate by £671 due to the falling off of the Imperial Greets and in the Edocation Accounts the frets are very striking. In 1901.5 the excheffiter contributed £27,142 rand the rates £10,409. In 1910-11 the eschequer contriblited £28,065 and the retest £18,600 the increase from the Exchequer being only £1,723 while the extra call upon the rates was £7,592. The urgency of this qui-Ado:1 has been recognised by the Government who have promised thnt they will appoint a small Committee to go as expeditionaly as possible into tt e main features of this problem in order to arrive at h decision on the ...ions points which have remained unsolved to a large extent simply becalms of the difficulty rind magni- tude of the subject. May I say that nlthough we are under the unfortunate necessity of bnving to levy rater of 74. 104. in the £, yet even at this figure, we are below the average by it, of all the County Boroughs of England and Wales fcr the year jitst ended and I urn glad to know that even thin heavy burden will not fall ondoly even upon the [ergo ratepeyera. for the lid. in L will only °pistil an extra charge of under £2OO per annum upon even the largest manntactorers within the florungb. Shops rented at £lOO will be called upon to Pay £2 23 GI. store and homes of say £3O rental an adtitien if 12s. 94. The cottage property seiner for cottages paying ss, per week will ',ay nn extra Sr. 6d. per 'lnnen:. We must not overlook the fact that it ARRINGTOX to A LOW Inborn() ...NI/ LOW ASSESSKO . . .</p>
        <p>the average ratertbie value per bred of population of the Conroy Borough being Et IS.. and in Warrington only .E 3 17e. 5d., and thor if it were not for the Educe- tic n rate which was placed upon as by l'arl iament and not by any choice of our own, alif rates which have averaged for the 45 years prior to the education char go 3s. 10d. In the would now be only 61. :kiln the E and for this we have carriel mitt vast improvements both to then ale streets and else. here and hove built hospitals, police station, lathe, ennitary depute, newer., and done other importnnt work, and hare provided several parks and open specnn in addition to eqnipping the town with gas work., water works, electricity works and tramway.. I think consieering we started with an old town, with narrow streets, end very primitive sanitary condition., we tints much to Le proud of. The important worts which lie in front, including the very neceesary prov Won of a handsome commodious bridge will, 1 here no doubt, receive the moat careful 00., sideration of the Council, and let rue hoper hit they may he curtied out without adding unduly to the burden of the ratepayers. In CvncluNion I would like to say that while I cannot deny that the chairmarship of your Finance Committee entails great responsihilty, and I arn not going to con- ceal the fact that it also means prolonged nod close attention, abaorhing many hours of time, yet the hotline. success and financial arrangements of the Corporation rest largely upon the loyalty sad capacity of yoliv principal utilcers. I roust as ociate with my annual statement on expression of gratitude and appriciallvu for the unflagging seal and the high capacity of Dlr. Fairhurst our Borough Treasurer, kir. Wucirlyock end their respective Wags. (Applause.)</p>
