      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>VOTES FOR WOMEN.</head>
        <p>VOTES FOR WOMEN.</p>
        <p>Town Council's Unanimous Resolution.</p>
        <p>Consider:o,le interest 1.11 taken in the resoluta., uf which Alderman Pierpoint lied Riven notice, at Tuesday evening's meeting of the 'Warrington Tow,. Council, in fan( or of the extension of the Parlia- mentary franchise to women ratepayera. Theta was a 14 ree attendance of the public, including nutnerns lady representatives of the Warrington Women's Suffrage Society. Expectations of a full dress debate were, however, not realized as the reaolution only came before the Counted at the close of u long and Irving meeting, ilmol it was therefore carried wlth a unit inoum of speechmaking. . .</p>
        <p>Alderman I' time moved "That this Council, ias the only 1,, dy representing the women ratepayers of the town, do petition his Majesty's Government to grant facilities at an early date for a Vending the l'arliamentary Franchise to women ratepa)era on the Runs of the Parliimentaty Franchi. (Women) Bill. HII I, which on the 12th of July, 1..t10, passed its second reading by a majoriry ,J one hundred and ten." The alderman said he thought that after the lirely proceedings that evening, the ladies of Warrigton would, toe to the conclusion that it would very muoli better for the business of the town, if there were a few lady members of the Council. (Laughter and app`suse.) Ile proposed the motitm that stood in his name, vitt lie was sure it would wet with the approval of all the members present. I They nccd not discusa.it.. (hyer, hear.)</p>
        <p>unckllor S11:1.1, who bad also given notice of Iris intention to move the resolution seconded, and said there were one or two facts he should have liked to bring forger], tut, as the members had remained so lorg already to record their votes in favour of the motion, Ike would not do so. He did not think there was one member who would dare to vote against the motir ft, RR it Missile!, aI RRFORRIbIe one. Councillor SIMI %VD, Councillor B nrr noir:. Alder- man BI.NSKIT and Councillor Pest 11Elt ins supported and the motion was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>CUESIIIRE.I.ICENSING CONIMITTEE. NINETEEN lIIOItE PUBLIC.IIOI2SES TO HE CLOSED.</p>
        <p>There wes a large ettendance of Cheehire magist ret•s at the Knuteftad Session. Iltmee on /Monday, when the sonnet tenon of the County Uncaring Committee, was rubmitted. The committee considered thirty-seven licences referred by the renewal authorities, and nine- teen horses were ortleted to ke cloud, compensation being granted. The houses included the Black florae, Nentnich remount of compensation crimpled. £1,45m ; Brunswick Inn, Runcorn ;290) ; Shamrock Tavern, Runcorn (E 105 Spring Vsttilt., Winner. lErtso) ; Swan Inn, Runcorn CSM ; and the hog and Gun, Lower liekingte n (E 1,500 Theemounts were accepted he the parties interested In ear h tare, viii, the reception of the Shamrock Tavern, Runcornot Lick tree leen referred to the Mend Revenue Commiestione..</p>
