      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>CARTER &amp; SON, Bridge St. Warrington.</head>
        <p>CARTER &amp; SON, Bridge St. Warrington.</p>
        <p>PRACTICAL POULTRY FARMING.</p>
        <p>(By Redcap )</p>
        <p>Arr.m.</p>
        <p>April shouldbe • very bury month in the poultry yard. You may continue hatching from the heavier breeds if the past montlin results are insufficient ; but you shoulil lose no time in getting the egg. down. There are plenty of broody hove to be got non ; indeed, I am afraid there are too many. Still, there is rn reason why you shculil not make very profitable use of theta. Put them down with a batch of eggs and sell 6, th bra and chicks ant rinted out a few weeks ago. The light or cm-sitting verieties, such as Minoicas, Anconas, Ipghornei, &amp;c., must be produced this month no that you will gal pullets naily to lav; before the winter sets in. Special attention should be paid to the male birds heading the brerdirg rens. If it is not vasible to run two, then the tno should he rested every trther day, giving him a special food diet containing much meat and an occiutional feed of bread and milk and ,mttned. Orenproducti ii is apt to noise debility, tin refine, a atimukting diet (volmt condiment) M essential in the case of the mule L;rd.</p>
        <p>Early broals should have apvcial attention paid to them this month. If they are fairly well mstured they should be separated without delay. The young cockerels should be taken away aid reseed quite separately from the pullets. As a remit both sexes will grow and thrive much more rapidly. A good food diet will 'mist them during feather growth. 'they should have a liberal surq ly of t reen food and an ecasional sprinkling of hemp wed mixed in the grain. The latter, no rich in oil, helps the youngsters considerably in growing the feather. We are having 8 me very cold winds Just at present and ihre pre, urn- tire should be made to avoid them. The backyarder can generally 80 arrange to prevent the itT, cts, but 'not so the cottager and smallholder. Let me gve n a useful ' wrinkle ' in the share of an improvised shelter. Take a few woodbinenu or mils and make, an it were the, ft amewm k via small pstiltry house or shed and then inter-twine the framework with long wheat straw or fine shrubs of aome description, such as grows on railway banks and avast. places. Such a construction makes a very useful shelter and will often be freqnented by the youngsters while these cold winds are no prevalent. Every c,trivance which tends towards the comt rt of the young stock is pri fitable. You are always recrm- penned later on by giving special care during the early days of chickenhood. Another useful hint when considering the chicken- rearing question is the supply of green food 'in the following manner. Take an ordinary seed-potatoe lox awl fill it with good soil level w ith the top and then fasten over this (tightly) a layer of fine mesh netting. When complete sow it with mustard and cress seeds. When groan to the In ight of an inch let the youngsters have free acorns to it. It would be none the worse if you had say three or four sock boxes FO that you could keep op a good supply right through the Kennon. Of c 0.., if you are fortunate enough in having a grave run this method can toe di.pensed with. But to others not 80 well blest it is a useful Lint.</p>
        <p>EV.EN.TI oN PM TER 111 S</p>
        <p>As the fl ck of chickens increases no does a tendency to disease increase ale, It will not be necessary for me to go into eleiails on the diseases if chickens as I ' have already done re, iu • previous article. Brit now that the nunibera are increasing it is needful t ler], an ever watchful eye on them. A little roan powder added to the drinking water now and then will prevent many diseases arising. Pros ide them with plenty of warm shelter, keep them scrupulously clean, and feed on sound wholesome fond and you will not get far wreng.</p>
        <p>During the week I have received an invitation to visit one of my backyard enthusiasm This I have done, and a very enjoyable suit it was, too. I found sty host busy making alterations with a view to making his stuck more comfortable. In a small area ahro- ,lntely doss,' in silk hoicks and mortar, sty friend keeps a grand little stud of Minorca!, , the first item to hand was an unfo,tunate one, for my ' friend had to report the death of a very fine Black Minorca cceketel. I held a poet-mortem on this Wel and I found the internal irgons in very mayi condition. He was EVidently overworked and had teen alarm tog from what is called " largo-crop " commonly known by fanciers as .• soft crap." This is usually due to improper feeding and the forst g,,ing sour in the crop. It not attended to in time the whole syst, m becomes poisoned and death follows.</p>
        <p>While we are dealing with an actual cane I may on well give you a remedy for the disease. Take the affected otre and hold it heal downtv.irls and work the contents of crop out through the mouth. hunt over In the Tatient at first ; after you have : removed a good 'ration of the slur food, give the bird a drink of water to which han been added a small iliontitrof Coady, Fluid. Ilepent tin prrcess when the bird has had a good rest and so continue until the crop is perfectly clear. After this trying ordeal the bird naturally wants a little nursing round. Place him inn nice warm pen and feed on bread and milk and my tit-bitn from the tali!e. You should, how- ever, continue to add a little of the Fluid in the drink- ing water.</p>
        <p>To return to the subj-ct of my ri.it I must say that my host can even now give some of the oiler hands a few hints. It is a pity that such enthusiastic poulry farmers are limited in their operations. Let 'ther readers take advice from friend and when in difficild ties let me know, when I shall only be too pleased tn help.</p>
        <p>..CLI VI rurmi[l.,</p>
        <p>The growth of poultry shows irs a decided temptation for budding far ohs* to try their liana. I base uften been asked which is the meet profitable Poultry for exhibition purposeu or utility poultry - Well, you may just as well ask, which is the inest probable, n " chip shop," or a " shaving saloon"? It all depends on how much you know about the business. There arc equal yolks attending both branch, a of tie poultry industry ; but we must deal exclusively with poultry for exhibition. Does it ray ?Inn, generolls speak- ing it does pay, and pay well. Rut first of all a got theoretical ground work is necessary. Theu you should get into touch with a successful fancier from</p>
        <p>'Old C abar CHICALA A DR.Y FEED FOR CHICKENS. AGENTS :</p>
        <p>CARTER &amp; SON Bridge St. Warrington.</p>
        <p>%VIP .1 yr u tv,,old learn much. When you feel sure that you have gained all the knowledge you can in this direction, make tip your mind what breed you intend taking up. Having thus far proceeded place yourself in the hands of • reliable breeder of the variety you have decided on. 'rake Ina advice and act upon it. A cetemon error with amateurs is going in for too many varieties until they have mastered one. Specializing i the en], sure ray to success. Having decided cn the breed stick to it until you have made • name. Then pro can develore and take tip (with some hope of success) mher vat eties.</p>
        <p>NI,I:I”TANI.rn VAIN El 1</p>
        <p>Tie Met way to th,roughly understand th, variety of ycur taste i. done by vt,iting a few good shows. More especially so if you take a fancier friend with ye Here you have an opportunity of seeing a collection uf the best birds and are enabled to compare notes as to poi and had point.. You get to know whit to situ at. Still further, you get into touch with breeders of the variety and much valuable inf., !nation (au be tl us gathered.</p>
        <p>Nearly every breed and vat jets of p:Allity Lave a club to protect their interests, these clubs is.ne an ideal of their respective breeds, allowing the points to be bred for, and also a scale of ',Gioia which count on the judges book at exhibil ion.. Other valuable information and help is given. and all (generally spesikiug) for a small annual subscription not exceed- ing :7is. Err annum My advice to all intending Fanciers is to j in a club. The clubs arrange for novice classes ; there prove beneficial to the piing fancier in bringing him to the front. More especially ould I urge the j .bring of a specialist club, because it would still further h. 1p the novice to thoroughly understand his variety. Before we get to more practical issues. let me warn you to seriously consider local conditions. For instance, 'our special fancy nine be to keep Minorca's, and the climatic and other coriditions be itufavourable. As a consequence it would be folly ti fight against anch conditions, especially when breeding for shw pm-roses. I realise the difficulty in forming a new love, late nevertheless Ithis should be done if you want to le successful.</p>
