      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>BUTTERMAKEET-STHEET.IMPROVEMENTS..</head>
        <p>BUTTERMAKEET-STHEET.IMPROVEMENTS..</p>
        <p>Ilssixsx-r, in propoeing the adoption of the minutes of the Street Improvem sit Committee, ssid he noticed with particularaatisfacte. the decision of that committee wises proceeding with the widening of Bottermarket-street, to re-erect the Old Fox lon in some other pert of the town. The committee were also acting wisely in endeavouring to get uniformity of roof line and coral 'ex in the reboilding of the street. Bridge-street war handwrote in many respects and ens adorned with buildings of which there was reason to be proud but, viewing it as a whole, one could not but feel there had beets. cot.siderable want of harmony is the general arrangements. Each building seemed to stand on its own footing. In Itutternisrket-street they wised to avoid that and the rot lines and cornices of the new 'Mope would be in accordance with the special designs submitted by Messrs. Wright, 0 rnett and Wright, the architects of the circus at Market Gale. If the Town Council itself bed peer to erect &amp;hope in the main thoroughfares it would avoid ninny difficulties and bring in a handsome return in the shape of rents.</p>
        <p>The 31 tt secooied and the minutes were approved.</p>
        <p>'- GAS V. ELECTRICITY. ALLEGED cr2T-TIIROAT " CoMPETITION BROSEEN CONTMITTEES.</p>
        <p>In moving the ed Futon of the minute, of the Gas Committ.e, Alderman I'IEIII'OINT made a long state- ment with reference to the financial position of the Concern. lie pointed out that this year the committee was devoting the whole of the profit., which were expected Ito realize about £lO,OOO, to the relief of the rate.' and, at the point of the sword, they bad also sgreed to draw upon their reserve food so as to bring their total contribution to the rates to £13,000. During tho past ten years, including the present grant, the undertaking bad mmtributed £107,420 15s. 4d. to the relief of rates. The course of action decidt I upon to meet the exigencies of the present financial situation would mean that the One Committee would lose the greater part of ta sinking fund, and it would be thee placed in • wont awkward position. It would not be able to carry out certain alterations to The plant which in a short time would he necemary. Ile thought there was much need for investigation d)t the competi- tion between the Etc ;tricity nod Gas Committees. lie understood that in order to keep the plant going in the day time the Gas Committee had been supplying gas to large consumere at fret 20 to 20 per cem below oust price, while he belie, el the Electricity Committee had been selling ' 'oer at 10 per cent, below coat. Thu rime, he believed, had come when the Council should inaist upon an investigation. As chairman of the Gax Committee he would welcome with the greatest cerdtnl- itya meeting stf representatives of the twocomrnittms and one or two independent members of the Coexcil. Cut-thrmat competition between manofacturera foolish and between Corporation uniertalings it we! silly in the extreme. Aldermen BOLTON seconded.</p>
        <p>.„ CoUncillor PANIBERTON considered Alderman Pier' paint's statement lad drankn Inmost alarming state of •ffaile. (Alderman Pierpoint "No, no.", In • private concern it would be regarded as absolutely ,rotten. However aeriour the rise in the rate, might he, the milking of any Curportion concern to get over the chili:oily would not help them in the lung rim It behoved the Corporation to make the moat of its under- takinga and derive the some amount of profit from them that • pri•ate firm worth]. Alderman TINNION laid Aldermen l'ierpoint bad put the matter in a more alarmiog light than the cirzum- atences warranted. He di 1 nit Yea.. undertaking anywhere that was on• more solid and soiled forrndation than the Werrington gas department. Tome All reserve of .C 10.1,0 which Could only 6. drab a upon In cotes of extrarns calamity, sucb.as breakrlowrs or el:plosions. dbn committee was selling some gas for power purposes during the day time at a lower price I than to the ordinary c "Isomer, hat the cost of that gas, delivered into the mine, without considering capital charge., left n margin of at least Gri. per 1,000 feet prAlt. The food which Alderman Pierpoint her ' described as a remove fund war not such in reality, but n special fond relied for specific purposee, the most important being the provision of plant for which thd hooal Government Huard would not allow them to borrow Coun.cillor If. ROBERT-, labile expressing the opinion that the Gas Committee hod adopted a safe policy in malting the increased grant, emphasiee I rho necessity for co-ordination between the manufacturing com- ,Mittees of the C0...nc1.</p>
        <p>1 Alderm. PIRRPOINT said ligh did not wish to create any alerm as to the stability of the concern, for the conindttee could Lave contributed more to the rates and still heve been la a position to meet the ordinary , demigods upon their funds. At. later stme of the meeting Aldo.. SIIKTHU nor replied to Alderman Pitrpoint. Ile wes not aware the-e was any intentional competition, be mid, and he welcomed with open alms the suggestion of a joint committee to inveatigete the whole matter. (Hear, hear.) He denied that the Electricity Committee was selling at a loss. The minutea of the comet:. tee were approved. On the minutes of the Electricity Cumtaitiee, Cone- ' cillor if ()HEWN said it was stated that the charge for electricity for the tramways undertaking am much in excess of the charge to individuil firms. Ile Bui:- nlitted that a public undertaking of that kind could not stipple electricity at a profit to firms requiring more tit. 100 h.p. iustallation. Cutincillor Pew newrov pointed out that while the Gas Department with a cipital of t 1 r,5,000 .. cop- teibutinc £13,500 to the rates, the Electricity Di ii hr t- weet, with a capital of £lOO,OOO was handing over only £5OO. Ile contended there was a need for the revision of prices charged to the coneumeri. Alderman Sol ETTI ill, pointed out that the grant front the Electricity Department would have been larger if they had not had to pay £OOO out of revenue for plant for which a loan could not be obtained. The minutes were passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>PROPOSED NEW SCHOOL IN LOVELY-LANE. Acrimonious Debate.</head>
        <p>THE PROPOSED NEW SCHOOL IN LOVELY-LANE. Acrimonious Debate.</p>
        <p>The Genetal Purposes Committee raceme:model that the plot of land in Lovely-any, containing seven sores, be purchased from Lord Newton for the purposes of a site fore public elementary school and a public rearta- .ion ground; and that Councillors Bellian,Petnherton, Roberts and Wilkinson be appointed n sub-committee to negotiate with Lord Neutm's agent for the purchase of the land, with power to enter into a contract. The MAYOR. moved and the Otiert V M AYOlt seconded the passing 01 the minutes. Councillor liositrono formally moved as an amend- ment that the minute relating to the purchsee of the land be referred hack, and the motion was second d by Councillor CUSNINGTON.</p>
        <p>...... Councillor flnrr THORN said some reason ought to be given for the amendment. The question was fought out by the General Purposes Committee and it was decided by s majority that the lan'l should he purchased. Ile hoped the Council would clod by the committee. COUOCiIIOr BROADHURST said Whitecross Ward bad not got an open space, and the land in question was most desirable. (Hear, hr or). Within lu peers it might be Imporsible to buy a suitable piece of land in that lac tlity. Councillor liolletcre suggested, and recent! members supported the suggestion, that the matter should nut be discussed in puolic, as the purchase of the site was still under negotiation.</p>
        <p>Councillor 'PI.:MUER:I4:N contended that the minute ought not to be allowed to go forward In its present forte e• it would give the sub-committee authority to purchase at any price. If ti at curt of minute were accepted, anything could be dune in the Corporation. Ile agreed that it we, perhans not wise to puolish the price at present, hot it shourd certainly have appeared on the privet, agenda. The DEPUTY Tow, CLERE said the committee was empowered to negotiate for the land and a certain price waa stipulated, hut it was considered unwise to print the figure while the negot rations were pearling. Councillor PEMBERToN :Aid that never in the history ol the purchase of property by that Corporation, had anybody been enabled by information given in that Crimea to buy property against the Corporation. Then why should there Le this secrecy? The minute as it stood was illegal. The deputation had already waited ripe I.ord Newton's agent and Catered bin a price without having the permission of the Commit. Councillor ROBERT, : It ban not. Councillor PsarnartToN r I sly it has. Permission is only being asked for to-night. We are actually asked to confirm the arrangemen lot an interview that has already taken place. I was a member of the deputation that waited on Lord Newton's agent and a certain offer was made for the laud, brit immediately we left the office I dissociated myself from that offer and said I would not ire re,ponsible for it. Von are now asked, by confirnaing tti minute, to give the committee a free hand. They cats oiler £lO,OOO for the land if they like. Norsense, absolute nOnsence, and you know it. . . . . .</p>
        <p>Cuocillorßrttt.Sn : Ile is misleading everybody. Alderman TissiiiN (to Coon illor Pemberton) : You are treking through your hat. sod you know it. CoklOcillOt PENIIIERioN : I appeal to the deputy Town Clerk that If this minute is pet. sd, without any mrouat being mentioned, it would authorise the Coin- otitts. to give £lO,OOO if they wished. The DEPUTV TO,NI CLEEN: :I should not like to ad•ise them to do it, knowing the instructions they here received. It is all in your own hands. You are Fore to Confirm or otherwirs this minute. There is no I L.-to done if you approve it. You will ratify what the sub ximinittee did, and it is perfectly legal and not out of crder. .</p>
        <p>C PEMIIEFLTON rattintained that when the deputation made the offer to Lord Newton, agent they were acting illegally. The agent was wrong in thinking that the committee had no altarrietive site in the neighbourhood. Councillor 11E..,111A1.1 said he took a very strong view on the temistion at the meeting of the Consent Parpot e Committee but was handsomely hence. lie had every confidence in the sub-committee. They all i knew the price that wets named and he did not think for a moment thut the deputation would attempt to go beyond it. .</p>
        <p>.7.1170Cii.101. ROEIVIIT, said he would like to quote the old adage" It's a dirty turd that fouls its own nest " sad he thought the san. tot found its acceptance or otherwise in the minds of the members of the Council and the general public presant that night. He also rose to give an absolute and flat contradiction to certain ' statements made by Councillor Pemberton.There was not a single word in either of the resolutions that WAS 'cornett or intim trat• in any parttcular. They were passed by the Ceneral Porposes Committee arid as set Out were true in substance lint in Not. The whole question of the suitability of the site was frankly and freely discussed for over en hour, and</p>
