      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>FINE WIN BY WIDNES AT ST.IIELENS.</head>
        <p>FINE WIN BY WIDNES AT ST.IIELENS.</p>
        <p>This returo League fixture attracted 10.000 •pectn- tors to Kuousley-road. Early on the visitors forced matters, nod Saintun cross-kicked to Lslly, who succeeded in scoring under the post.. June. easily improved. Fast end•to-end play foil. sod, and Flana- gan picking up in the Widnes quarters nicely served Bomb it.), who reu in at the corner. Tortill failed. , IVidnes commenced bustling tactics. and McCarthy taking advantage of st St. Helens mull ran over, for I Junes to do the needful. From s brilliant sound of passing by the Widnes backs, Swinton scored an unimproved try. Half.time—Wition 2 goals and 3 tries (13 point.), St. Helens I try (3 points). Pope started the second half for St. Helens. Widnes opened out with grand passing, and Johnson crossed, but Taylor failed to improte. Piny was vigorously contested to the finish, when Fltmgen scored in the last minute. Result —Widnes 2 goals •nd 4 trim (16 points), St. Helens 2 trim (I, point.). Teams— St. Helens —Turtill, back ; Warburton, Belches., J. Greenwood and Flamgan, three-quarter bscke ; Harold Greenwood and Clare, bell backs ; Butterfield, Crellin, Mavity, Heaton, Pope and Briers, forward.. Widnes—Jonee, back; Lilco, Lally, Taylor, and Swin- ton, thrm-quarter backs ; Noon and O'Hara, ball back. ; G. Aspey, S. Aspey, McCarthy, Lloyd, Rigby, and Johnson, forwards. Referee, Mr. J. C. Lumley, Leeds.</p>
        <p>LIXSZILD CO :IPOLIND. (ASISOKI, 8,111.1/S, Sqll iii, To Ito.) For Coughs sad Colds. Influenza eto.—Adrt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>Notes by " Ovalist."</head>
        <p>Notes by " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>Theie will be acre general than local interrat among Warrington football follower. to-day. The semi finals for the N.U. Cup are partietilary intern. ting engagements, and a'. Huddersfield and Salford there should I e a tine etruggle for the right to appear in the beat. The semi-finals are Rochdale Hornets v Broughton Ranger., at Salford. Batley v Wigan, at Huddersfield.</p>
        <p>Everything peints to the careers of Rochdale Hornets and Wigan, though after the surprise of the preceding round over-coutidence will be checked in the ranks of both players and ,pectators. Batley are prone to urnet calculation', in cuptis warfare, as wit- ness their cinching defeat of Salford at Weaste, and their record as Cup holders. But with a continuance of the present dry weather caneitions, I cannot see tltem checking the progress of the brilliant Wigan thirteen. The latter are playing a cleverer and stronger g. me row than ex, before nr d on form should certainly win the Cup. None of the 25,4 gar people who saw the masterly way in which they over- played Oldliam last weeir can have any doubts about their ability to conquer Batley, and whichever team may Its co-h'ina'isti with them.</p>
        <p>Ace :riling to latest reports the Wigan iihipper, Leytham, will be an absentee as the reitllt of the blow he recired at the conclusion of die game with Oldham. Ilenwick is such a capital reserve wing, howe•er, that there should be litt weakening, if any, of the : Colliers' attack. The forwar Is, on whom so tuncit ' will depend against a team of Batley's cm dines, inspire confidence. They aro chosen fir scrummaging power, arid nit for linearn. The latter will be safe in the hands of Jobs, y Thomas and Co.</p>
        <p>At Salb,rd there should be a chre and strenuous: struggle between Rochdale Hornets and the Rangers. The latter base made an advance of late, but one cannot help ihinkirg they hove been lucky to reach the semi-final. The hornets have much the better record, and should just rill the match ,IT by dint of no; ericr combination behind. Foruaid thee is nothing to choose, and even a lucky gral or two migLt npset, the prophets. I expett Rochdale to prevail, however, and teach the final Lr the first time.</p>
        <p>And now to to poor oh! Warrington. They are not exciting anybody by their rkinga at present, and their engagement with C'tventty to-day will seem like a parlour game in comrnriaon with the clip-ties. lint learn assure the local enthuidasts that they 'bay look forward to a ,mart game. Unless Coventry have been overt:l4n i.y end-of-arason stakne,s they should create a good impression no their first iSit to Wilderspool. In the Cup-tie AVIIII War- rington they played fast, open football, and in most restates they made a god show in the scoring line The light wing, Dakin and Woodburn, is particularly rpeedy and aggreesire, and once the defence has Lela peeler ted the Ilidlanders will inake a formidable side.</p>
        <p>' Warrington should win, of course, and as their remaining matches are Halifax and Iluilder.field at home (oil Good Friday and Easter Mot.day) they should wind up the siason in a subdued blaze of glory. That is, if the area from lartown do not prove rather too good ! They have plarril a great Maine since the New Year, and sir uld prole a big bank-holiday attraction.</p>
        <p>The Warrington tam ter to-day will be : Ileintrre, full back Bradshaw, Diekeneon. Taylor and Way- well, threequarters; Bre tka and (mercy, halftbseks ; Johnston, Shugart., Thomas, Boardman, Chester., aud Skelhotn, forwards.</p>
        <p>The league flitures for toßlay, with the results last Reason are as follows : Oldham l5 Leeds 0 Warrington — C sentry ElMw Vale 2 11i 'nee Honshu 5 Brantley Bradford York l7 Halifax Hull It uncut.° Leigh</p>
        <p>Widnes are facing their last serious obstacle in the race for fourth position, and should just about win at Ebbw Vak. If they fail. the Rovers of Hull taunt win at Bradford to capture the coveted place.</p>
