      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>One buppores that the primary tbject of a mmic hal! is to provide amusement and wonder for as many , as can obtain a comfortable seat. This ideal 111 especially achieved this week ml is strikingly exemplified by Harry Nelson and Captain Spaulding respectively. Ileeidre being a born humorist. Harry has got a good voice and he uses it to advantage. His song deriding the euffragettes is originally con- ceived and his interjections seem to be qui,: spontanecra. There is none of that " well prepared" element which so vies forces itself to the front among many artistes. Ho is sell mouth hearing and seeing : he is a novelty. As for Captain Spaulding we were struck with amazement when weaaw hint quench his thirst with retrol and satisfy Ids hunger with melted sealing wax. Besides the womlerful things he does the Captain possesses a fine and well exerci6ed faculty for dry humour which enlivens his performance immensely. he Wittington Trio present a kket,ll entitled "An Inquisitive Visitor," in width they evolve some ast.nishing acrobatic feats. As harmony singers, the Three Orleans surpaes anything of o like nature that has teen on the Warrington boards for many months, their singing being really delightful. They make the best use of capital voices. Lily end Agnes earn popularity as Lancashire duettiats, their contributions bring of a particularly meritorious character. As a comedy whistler and raconteur Burt Ward keeps the audience in a jolly mood, aryl Lilian Rose gives some capital representations of diverse conditions of English life. The pictures, as usual, are welcome, and the orchestra, under the leadership of Ifr. E. A. Widd p ren.lers exctllrnt u.u-ic.</p>
      </div>
