      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>Teachers' Association.</head>
        <p>Teachers' Association.</p>
        <p>.1( a recent meeting of the Warrington and District Amociation of Teachers, the following resolution was unanimously adopted That this meeting of Warrington teachers expresses a deep seem of indignation at the circular is.ued by Mr. Holmes, the late Chit( Itspector of Elementary School*, at the contemptuous, one therein adoptid to teachers from the elementary schools, and at the condemnation ~f such teachers as local inspectors without the produc- tion of any proof of their incapacity or inferiority • and that while thankful that the circular has he.. withdrawn, thie meeting respectfully urges the Board iof Education to render mpossible in the future the issue of any such secret document crntaining reflections upon 50,000 public servants. This meet- ing also expremes its conviction that no person is adequately qualified for the position of Inspector of Schools who bee not had some teaching and organising experience in the echo la." Copies of the resolotioe Ware been forwarded to Mr. W. Ituncienan (president 1 of the Board f Education), Si, J. H. Tonsil, MA, Ml'.. and Ur. Harold Smith, M.P.</p>
        <p>Fr Me,, are now selling their own &amp;Ater and taking " GOLDEN PIAUI " MAR, &amp;RINE home f,or their own DI e. f his is testimony for you.—Advt.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY. APRIL 8 AU.</p>
        <p>VAIN</p>
        <p>How far may Mr. BALFOUR be accepted</p>
        <p>IMAGININGS. as a qualified exponent of what the country meant when it pronounced against the House of Lords and in favour of the Govern- ment at the last General Election? The question may be answered according to taste. The LEADER OF THE OPPOSMON himself speaks on the subject with a confi- dence that would be admirable if there were any justification for it. The PRIME MINISTER told the House of Commons the other day that if electors determined anything at all at the last election it was that in this Parlia- ment they would once for all assert the supremacy of the House of Commons in all matters of finance. But Mr. BALFOUR knows better, and is not at all shy about say- ing so. When the election took place, he said, there was a feeling in the country that the House of Lords had gone beyond its proper constitutional function in referring the Budget of 1909 to the popular vote, and Clause 1. of the Parliament Bill was popularly supposed to prevent the House of Lords repeating the action of 1909. We may accept the statement provisionally for the sake of the confession that it ',lakes. It was Mr. BALFOUR who encouraged the House of Lords to reject the Budget. There would have been no such rejection if the Leader of the Opposition in the House of Commons had not also assumed the position of dictator to the House of Lords. He now admits that, in the opinion of the country pronounced through the General Election, the Peers went beyond their constitutional function. He accepts that view as being a correct one, because he thinks that by doing so he can limit the meaning of the popular pronouncement on the House of Lords question as a whole. The subject is not one on which he can expect or even hope to mislead the House of Commons. Gentlemen sitting on both sides of the House know quite well what the present majority was sent to Westminster to do, but there springs eternal in Mr. BALFOUR'S breast a brave confidence in the simplicity Of the country. He holds that the people out of doors can be made to believe what he wishes them to believe, and his overthrow in a suc- cession of three General Elections has not qualified this extraordinary confidence in his ability to make the nation believe something that is contrary to what it actually knows. Everybody is aware, for instance, that Mr. ASQUMI is stating the bare truth when he says that the present Parliament has been entrusted with the duty of making the House of Commons supreme in all matters of finance. Yet Mr. BALFOUR thinks it is worth while to pretend that all that was decided was that the Peers ought not to throw out the Budget. If the Leader of the Opposition had not lost his power of exciting surprise, this trivial manner of dealing with matters of grave consequence would be amazing. If Mr. BALFOUR and his friends had any competent knowledge of the mood in which the country is waiting for the passing of the Parliament Bill, they would, we believe, be anxious to facilitate rather than to delay that measure. We should hear very little of the 900 amend- ments. Members on the Conservative benches would not, as was done early in the week, attempt to waste time by speaking on portions of the Bill which are not before the House. The Conservative leader would not continue to argue in the mood of those School- men of the Middle Ages who gravely dis- cussed such questions as how many angels can dance on the point of a needle. The truth is, however, that the Opposition does not understand the national seriousness. It understands nothing of that kind which doeB not manifest itself violently. This is to be only regretted from the point of view of the Opposition itself,udiieh is just now as surely marching to a disgraceful defeat as it was when it was signalling to the Lords to throw out the Budget of 1909.</p>
        <p>The completion of the MR. W. H. LEVER purchabe of the Beech- AND LYIIII. wood and Oughtrington estates by Mr. W H. Lever on behalf of the great firm at Port Sunlight has naturally aroused a great deal of speculation and interest in this district. Mr. Lever is now the proud possessor of about half the township of Lymm and his purchase includes three fine Cheshire halls, two hostelries, and two railway stations. The possibilities are immense, but I Mr. Lever has already knocked on the head the idea of any of the commercial activi- ties of Port Sunlight being transferred to Lynim. It may be that the salt industry will be cultivated if the ground is favourable, but in the main Mr. Lever favours the idea of a garden city at Lymm. A spot with more natural advan- tages could hardly be found in the length and breadth of the land, and with the genius of a Lever as the stimulus, Lymm may become one of the show-places of England. But in whatever direction Mr. Lever's newest enterprise may tend, he is assured of the warmest co-operation by the inhabitants of a district who recollect with pride that his earliest commercial ventures found their home within its confines.</p>
        <p>len Inorganic Ceraoet, Binding, Pilling, to. withstands water. Bd. post f,...—Ad•t.</p>
        <p>There may have been THE RATES, some few optimistic ratepayers last week who thought that the newspapers were exaggerating when they forecasted an increase of 6d. in the borough rates. If so they were undeceived on Tuesday when Councillor Wilkinson presented his Budget speech and explained the need for a call amounting to 7s. 10d. in the pound as against 7s. 4d. last year. The Chancellor made a very lucid and interesting statement to an audience which was not, naturally, very enthusiastic. Like a wise man he •endeavoured to gild the pill which he felt compelled to administer, and some of his little sophistries would have done justice to a greater theme. For instance, he pointed out that sixpence 'in the pound did not mean more than £2OO per year for the largest manufacturer within the Borough— s mere bagatelle so to speak ! We hope the ." largest manufacturer" wi I be sufficiently philosophic and public spirited to look at the matter in the same rosy light. At the • other end of the scale the owner of • cottages rented at ss. will merely be called upon for another 2s. 6d. He will, doubtless, be equally resigned, and more especially if he can see his way to getting the impost back from the tenants. Coun- cillor Wilkinson was very bravely making the best of a decidedly bad job, and the fact remains that a jump of 6d. in the rates is viewed with much concern and not a little alarm by the general body of ratepayers who have long since realised that the privi- lege of working and living in a progressive borough is an expensive one.</p>
        <p>Th e ever-increasing CAUSES OF THE cost of education is the RISE, principal factor in the big rate increase, and accounts for nearly half the sixpence. In this connection Councillor Wilkinson very properly drew attention to the fact that the boroughs are paying more than their fair share of the cost of education. For too long the State, as represented by various Govern- ments, has shunned the burden, and we are • a long way from the day when the educating of the youth of the nation will become a national instead of a local charge. In six years the call on the education rate in War- rington has increased by £7,592 and now involves is. 7d. in the pound, while in the same period the Exchequer grants have only increased by £1,123. There is some hope to be derived from the fact that the Government recognises the difficulty and has promised to investigate. Another factor, which deserved more than a passing reference from our Chancellor, is that last year the rates failed by £750 to realise the estimated amount, a circumstance due to the recent reduction in assessments, particularly of licensed property. There will probably be a greater deficiency at the end of the current year,when the policy of the Assessment Com- mittee should be exposed in all its naked futility. Finally, and more serious even than existing conditions, there is the unpleasant fact to be faced that a further increase in the rates a year hence is probable. The increase of 6d. is a minimum which has only been arrived at by tapping the reserve funds of the Gas, Electricity and Tramways under- takings, and if the situation had been met boldly we should have been called upon to contribute at least Bd. more this year instead of 6d. Unless the revenues of the munici- pality increase considerably during the next twelve months, a further call is inevitable. It is perhaps superfluous of us to impress the need for economy on the part of every spending department; the responsi- bility is doubtless realised; but we feel that we are only expressing the conviction of the vast majority of ratepayers when we say that, for the time being, posterity must go hang and some attention be paid to the reason- able pecuniary ease of the present generation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>There was evidently some suspicion in the minds of the local police that the introduction of a Women's Suffrage resolution in the Council chamber might cloak some dastardly scheme by the militants. Colsequently there was a strong posse of plain clothes officers in the "crowd." The ladies, while quite ignorant of this strict surveillance, behaved in a most exemplary fashion. Up to now, the Warrington police have not dis- covered the difference between a suffragist and suffragette, and we can excuse them. Though very marked, the difference is not obvious to the eye.</p>
        <p>- The numbering of the people" in War- rington has been a huin-drum affair produc- tive of no unusual incidents, everybody— including the suffragists—being disposed to assist the 48 enumerators in carrying out their task as expeditiously and efficiently as possible. We have it from the Deputy Registrar (Mr. W. H. Harrison), who supervised the arrangements, that most of the forms were intelligently filled up, but of course some heads of families were a little perplexed to grasp exactly what was required. The "occupation or profession" column proved the greatest poser, homely terms, not officially recognized, occasiorally being used. The information is now being classified, but the grand total will not be known until next week-end.</p>
        <p>The suggestion made by Councillor Roberts at Tuesday's Council meeting that com- petitive examinations should be held for all junior positions in Corporation departments was welcomed by the Council and will, we have no doubt, meet with ready approval from the public. In these progressive days it is increasingly recognised that all boys— however humble their parentage—ought to have an equal opportunity of entering the public service, and anything that savours of favouritism is rightly deprecated. Merit alone should count and we are glad that I Warrington is to have the opportunity of leading the way for the municipalities of England in putting this democratic theory into practice. •o • -</p>
        <p>A few weeks ago Mention was made in thee notes of the necessity for some organisa- tion of the bonfires which are to be lighted on the night of the Coronation, so that they should all be burning at the same time. In this connection it is interesting to find what are the high places from which news may be transmitted from the Channel to the Border by signal fires. Macauley's fragment, " The Armada," describes how "From Eddystone to Berwick Bounds, from Lynn to Milford Bay," the sign was sent until "The red glare on Skiddaw roused the Burghers of Carlisle." The shortest chain that could be made would be on the West Coast, by bonfires lighted on Brown Willy in Cornwall, Dunkery Beacon on Exmoor, the Carmarthen Van, Ceder Idris, Snowdon, Scafell Pike and the Criffel. From Ben Lomond could probably be seen the Criffel and Ben Nevis, and from Ben Nevis a becon would shine far into the North. —"Country Life." Cold Days! Wet Days! Dry Days! All D. ! GOLDIN FLEECE " M•110•11.1NI Is always good alike. Joel like that potter."—Adrt.</p>
        <p>M. Renkin, the Belgian Colonial Minister. in a recent speech said : "We believe that Free Trade is an indispensable condition of Belgium's commercial prosperity. I am, and I remain. a Free Trader,"</p>
        <p>What was termed-on Tuesday "cut-throat mpetiticn," is alleged to exist between the Gas and Electricity Committees of the Cor- poration. Both are charged with selling power below cost price, and with "doing ore another" in the ways most approved by unscrupulous commercial competitors. It seems a queer position for servants of one master to occupy, and the result is likely to be a committee of inquiry, which one gentle- man concerned "welcomed with open arms" because "he would come out at the top of the tree." • •</p>
        <p>In his recent interesting lecture on his experiences as county coroner, Mr. Samuel Brighouse made the following reference to a local instance of heroism in the humbler walks of life _ . . .</p>
        <p>-....- In 1905 an he:oic strry was unfolded at an inquest at Wart ington on James Baxter, who was suffocated in • Corpmation sewer. John Stockton a carter, in pro, health, saw the crcwd round the manhole. He rescued two men, and though nearly °surer me, he fetched the deceased out. but too late. It, received the medal and vellum from the Royal Humane Society. and later he received the Stanhope ()Ad Medal from the Society for the bravest deed of the year. (Loud applause) That medal came to Lancashire in 1857.</p>
        <p>The "Winnipeg Trirtuae " waxes sarcastic at the efforts of the Tariff Reformers to work up an "annexation" scare. "The Tariff Reformers of Britain are hard up for a cry when they begin shouting that Canada is likely to be annexed by the United States. They're a stupid lot in their understanding I of the sentiment of the Canadian people. Are the people of Britain likely to become Germans because they allow German goods to enter freely into their markets? . . . If the Tories raise the loyalty cry and insult the masses of people who are crying out, not only for Reciprocity, but for tearing down the trade barriers raised against the Mother 'Country, they will deserve to be utterly I routed."</p>
        <p>There is nothing very inspiring about provincial Coronation festivities. An ebullition of perfervid loyalty fortunately enables us to see things in the brightest hues, else we might conceivably be bored by the programme for the day. Warrington will commence with the customary proces- sion of Council-men, magistrates, friendly societies, and the like all in their best black or regalia. The school children will be enter tamed in Victoria Park, and presented with a mug each. The old folks are to have a feast. It should boa thrilling day I Walking Day, 24 hours later, may seem somewhat tame in comparison. But the youngsters should be adequately. impressed! The Town Council distinguished itself on Tuesday, and in the presence of a deputation of ladies who claim equality with man and a right to vote. Since the meeting most of them consider themselves superior beings altogether, and wonder by what folly the Creator ever made mankind the stronger sex. For three and a half hours our city fathers " argled and bargled," what time the ladies looktlk on in amaze, or laughed immoderately. In the end the Council redeemed itself somewat by passing a women's suffrage resolution with complete unanimity. It was the only occa- sion on which they were not aggressively disputatious, and if it were not for a suspicion that the wearied members had arrived at a peace at any price frame of mind by 9.30 p.m. we should be inclined to conclude that the women's cause was won. But, nevertheless. a unanimous endorsement of "the cause" by the County Borough Corporation of War- rington will read well at Westminster</p>
        <p>The claims of the Ararrington Children's Summer Camp once more demand generous consideration as the arrangements for the coming season will shortly be placed in hand and the extent of the work will depend upon , the amount of voluntary support afforded. I There is no institution in our town for which I we can more whole-heartedly put in a plea, for the value of its work amongst the poor children cannot possibly be over-estimated. In an appeal just issued it is pointed out that during the past few years there has been a constant struggle to obtain additional accom- modation, and, now that this has to a great extent been provided, attention is being turned to utilising that accommodation to the hest advantage. Efforts will now be directed to increasing the number of children sent during the school holidays, and also to extended use of the camp as a convalescent home. This is necessarily influenced by the committee's obligation to discharge the out- standing debit balance of £74 18s. 2d. This being Coronatian year, it is hoped that the interest manifested by the King in the welfare of children may find a re-echoing sympathy in the hearts of all his subjects, and that the forthcoming season may be heralded with renewed and increased endeavour and success.</p>
      </div>
