      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>CORONATION. ARRANGEMENTS FOR LOCAL CELEBRATIONS.</head>
        <p>THE CORONATION. ARRANGEMENTS FOR LOCAL CELEBRATIONS.</p>
        <p>The Town Council having reduced to £5OO the ,inzn of !.:1,000 which it was proposed to vote to the Mayor by way of salary to debay the Coronation expenses 111 Wairington, the Coronation SubsCominittee have had a meeting and revised their estimates accordingly. We uoderetand that she programme for the day will be upon the following lines:— in the morning the Mayor, Corporation, citizens and members of the Friendly Societies will assemble at the Town Hall, and in the event of Lieut.-Camel Fairclough being able to arrange the Tro,ping of the King's Colour in Victoria l'ark, the procession will proceed to witness the ceremony, if not it will traverse the principal streets of the borough. In the lawmen it ii proposed to enteitain the whole of the children attending the elementary schools in the Borough in Victoria Park. Games and spotty will be held for the children, and each child will be provided with light refreshment and in addition will be presented with a Coronation mug as a souvenir of the day. Bands will also play io the two public parl, during the evening. fire proposed to entertain all the aged people in the Borough over 7.1 years of age. The form of this entertainment has n t ytt been settled. The Committee alen propose to erect a public drinking fountain in Victoria Park as a permanent memorial. .</p>
        <p>These arrangements, of course, we understand may be subject to alight •Iteration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>"Warrington Charities " Compeltlos.</head>
        <p>"Warrington Charities " Compeltlos.</p>
        <p>The Cheritiew Committee expect to hand over to our local charities between £9O and 1100, sac result of the competith n recently held, and beg to thank Lady Gretenell, Mrs W Peter Rylands, Mrs Henry 'Roberts and Captain G Il Croafield, for the valuable prizes given. Mr W Whatmough, of 12, Cartwright- street, won the gold watch, presented by Lady Greens)); Mrs. Crockett, of Brentwood, Sanirey, the marble timepiece, given by Mrs Ryland ; Mr G Nelson, of Park-place, won Captain Cro•field's prize ;"• dinner cruet"); and Mr S L Tunstall' of Grapprohall, the barometer, presented by Mrs Roberts. Tcr•srrimt,' a New Liquid Cement for Mending and linking. In tube. Id., 24., 3d.,l3d.—Advt.</p>
