      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>Royal Court Theatre.</head>
        <p>Royal Court Theatre.</p>
        <p>A novel attraction is announced for rext week when the Arne, ican drama " The still shunt," in which fire brigade work plays a piominont part, will be presented. " Iliebeth."</p>
        <p>The plan of reserved seats for the performance or "Macbeth" by Dr. Law, Mr. Harold Smieh, M.P., and a company of local amateurs on April 4 t , mill be opened at the ho flice at the Ftelal Court Theatre (to-day) Saturday at 111 n.m. We are asked to Mate that the rumour that some seats have already been booked is incorrect.</p>
        <p>Proceeds of Crestlsle's Opera. The honorary Treasurer gratefully acknowle 'gas havMg received from. Captain Crosfield the sum of .4:6.5 in aid of the fund, of the above ;revelation. such sum being one half cf the gross receipts of the recent performance of" Les _Cloches de Corneville" given by the members of the Croafield Ant ar or Operatic Society. A similar scknowledgment has been received from the Infirmary which has also bad a cheque for 165.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>British Women's Temperance Association.</head>
        <p>British Women's Temperance Association.</p>
        <p>A meeting was held on'lVednesday afternoon when Mrs. Gunnell presided, and gave an interesting address on s" Indian child n blow," which was much appreciated. Several of the members went by omnibus to Asbton-in-Makerfield to bear the Rev. R. J. Patterson. L LB., of Artnigh, Ireland, founder of the "Catch-my-Pal" movement. It was very interesting to listen to the racy coronet of the lecturer as be described the progresa of the movement.</p>
        <p>Warrington Photographic Society. As very o'ten bapptn•, the most i toresting and most serviceable meetings are those devoted to demonstrations or Ire urettes by members of the society, it is a pity they are not the best attended. Tuesday's menu was filled up with a discussion on the best was to develop a negative, whether by Time, by Tank, or by the .4,1 Teneative method. The second , was ably defended by the Secretary. Ur. Holloway, seconded by Mr. W. R .Jackson, and two good negatives were so developed on the spot. We old fogeys held by the old fashioned plan. A good many lively and characteristic stories were bandied around ; some of them probably chestnuts, but a very jovial evening was spent.</p>
        <p>MOTOR BICYCLES. SOIR agent for the celebrate Premier* Machine.. Trial. end 211 informatioe fred —J.S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge .t, Warriegtoe.-Ad • ' Assessment Committee.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Union Aesessment Committee was held at the Workhouse Yesterday, Mr. A. Brooks presiding The") were also prenent : Messrs 3 W Knowles, G Mather, A J Jolley, E L Rent, W (101,W C Brookee, A Bottomley (clerk), R R Meacock (valuer), and W H Woodcock (societal overseer). Mr Stephen, of Messrs. Banks. Fairelougli and Stephen, valuers, Leigh, appealed against the ass...rents of ten licensed houses at Newton-in-Makerfield. He pointed out that in slaking allowance for the new Finance Act dud's@ the com- mittee had annulled that the amount of those &amp;vies would be half the new prose, but as a matter of fact the Inland Revenue Commissioners had adhered to the old gross in calculating them He therefore asked tint in making the sesessment, the committee should take into account the licence duties actually paid. The commit:se declined to make any change. The Valuer submitted his re-valuation of , the whole of the off-licences in the Union. The 'supplemental valuation list for Warrington woe pre- sented and show the gram value of the borough to be 11372,445 and the rateable £290,121 10s. including— 'buildings, 1.288,572 ; land, £1,540 10e. The increase for the year was £1,578.</p>
        <p>St John Ambulance Brigade. Saturday afternoon as itnewwd the anr.ual competi- tion at the Central Police Buildings for the Warring- ton Ambulance Challenge shield. The teams competing were Central Station (Warrington), Golborne, Earlestown 11., Earlestown 1., Widnes and Cadishead. The two last named are novice teams, Fairlestown 1., who obtained fourth place in the recent London and North-Western Railway roar- peti ion at Belle 'toe, were the holders of the shield ihree testa were submitted ; viva voce, an individual practical test, and a stretcher test. Drs. Parker (Stockton Heath) and Jackson (St. llelens) rfficiated an judges and at the close of the competition announced the following reatilt Esrleetown I. Kai points), Golborne (219), Central Station (240). The maximum number of points was :fib/. The shield and prism were then handed to the winners fry the Mayoress (Miss Joseph) who together with the Mayor had been interested spectators of the proceed- ings. A vote of thanks was passed to the judges on the propodtinn of Mr. 11. T. Williams, seconded he Mr. A. E. Prier. Dr. Jack.on in reply congratulated:all the teams on their performances in the stretcher tent. Mr. A. E. Spann prop sed a vote of thanks to the Mayor and Mavorees. He was glad, he Raid, that the first citizen of the town had shown his practical interest ia ambulance work, arid felt sure his presence would have a stirmahoing effect on the members of the local corps. (Applause.) —Mr. Fell having second. ii and Captain Waring supported, the vote less accorded unanimously. The Mayor in reply said he considered the work of the varhus teams was worthy of the highest praise. Competitions of that character ought to receive every encouragement, for they tended to make the Brigade members more enthusiastic and more efficient. (Applause). GARDEN ROLLERS, at 27, 8 each. Lawn Mowers at 15). each, marvellous value—lnns Mowers around by y. Satisfaction guaranteed.—. S. srair.alaxther .1 i'lronmonger, Bridge at., IVarrington.—Advt Warrington Women's Circle. On Monday the annual Gymnastic Display and Concert was held is the Club Room, Bridge-street. The gye.naAic display was arranged toy Miss Bell (instructress) and the concert arranged by Mrs. Kenworthy. Theaccompanis's were Miss Parker and Ili•a Griffiths. Miss Be.sie Dutton oronpied the chair. The programme was as follows :—Part I, Swedish exercises, Dumbed!, Marthing. Part 2, pianoforle duet, Mi-ne, Chia hs and Starkie ; song, " 0, haste, ye birds " MN. Booth ; song, " The sailor's grave " Mr. Griffiths ; monologue, " Carle Podger bangs a picture" If Longhottran ; snag, " Angus Macdonald" Mra. Kenworthy ; duet, " Slaying " Mr.. Booth and Mr. Griffiths ; monologue, " The robber thief" Mr.. Longbottom ; song,. 'Tie Morn" Mrs. Booth; song, " Dear home songs " Miss Kate Hurter ; duet," Over , the hawthorn hedge" Meadames Booth. a.nd</p>
        <p>Kenreoriby ; song. " ijoise into the garde—n,. Maud" Mr. Griffiths. Part 3, steps fancy marching, • ...</p>
        <p>Indian club exercises. All the varioun's items were excellently rendered and enthuaiastically received. During the short interval at the and of the musical pact of the programme, Mi.. Dutton in a graceful little speech on behalf of the members of the Cir.!le, thanked Ilias Bell and Mies Parker for their help during the past sessions, and also Mrs. Kenworthy r bringing friends and giving such • delightful concert Miss Dutton a'so presented a writing case to Miss Bell as a " token of affectionate regard, and appreciation of her work, and with many regrets that Miss Bell is leaving Warrington," from the girls of: the gymnasium clam. Work done by the members of the millinery, dressmaking and fancy work clamso was displayed at one end of the club room, and won many expressions of admiration front the visitors. Think of it! Pun ad. you can have a pound of GOLDIM " Ms R 6 ARM; equal to Butter at 1,. W. It mem your money.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>MR. W. H. LEVER AND LYME</head>
        <p>MR. W. H. LEVER AND LYME</p>
        <p>DEWHURST ESTATE PURCHASED Big Developments Rumoured. A PROPOSED GARDEN CITY.</p>
        <p>Lymm, and indeed a very large part of Lancashire and Cheshire, has been all agog this week by the official intimation through the daily press that Mr. W. H. Lever of Port Sunlight had purchased the 13.. w burst estates of Beech wood aid Oughtrington. Our readers will recollect that in December last we announced that Mr. Lever had acquired these extensive properties, though in response to our inquiries Mr. Lever would not confirm or deny the report. We may, or may nzt. have been a little premature, but if</p>
        <p>the contract was not then"signed,sealed, and delivered," the bargain must have been well advanced. The official intimation is that Mr. W. H. Lever has on behalf of Lever Brothers, Limited, acquired through his agents, Messrs. Knight, Frank and Rutley, the Beechwood and Oughtrington estates, in Cheshire, which comprise the greater part of the town of Lymm. The property includes Oughtrington Hall, Beech wood Hall and Oughtrington House, and the famous Lymm lake, situated in the picturesque valley of the Dingle; also the village of Oughtrington, the Plough Hotel, numerous shops, chief rents, certain manorial rights, the advowson of Oughtring- ton, several houses and small holdings, cottage property, and the Jolley Thrasher Inn. The railway stations of Lymm and Heatley are on the estate and the Bridge- water Canal passes through the property. Mr. Lever has also bought the Lymm Waterworks as a going concern, with the pumping station and reservoirs. Lymm is</p>
        <p>1 1</p>
        <p>BEECHNVL OD HODS which overlooks t'.e Lake at Lymm from the south-east si '0</p>
        <p>Ardern. Lymm.</p>
        <p>• , .••• • •••• `,•r I • . _ 1</p>
        <p>Photo Mrdrinyon. MR. W. H. LEVER.</p>
        <p>• !ir I 1/41 , 7 tfl " ,-4461•••••- 64 kit ho r "t 1. la :11111111011 MOM • • I</p>
        <p>, , .. . , - • . - AI- V‘V f 1- -.: ' ..... *,• A, II I. • .11t1 1 _., 't ... . • ~,..msa.6ll* I '.. p . ::,.,' 44 , ~. , • --- •,1„. 'not(</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>OUGHTRINGTON HALL, which adjoins Oughtrington Church and Village.</head>
        <p>OUGHTRINGTON HALL, which adjoins Oughtrington Church and Village.</p>
        <p>one of the few towns in England where the water is supplied by a private company, and the rights exist under a special Act of _ . .</p>
        <p>Parliament dating from ina:</p>
        <p>The entire</p>
        <p>property covers - about 1,700 acres. It belonged to the Dewhurst Trustees. for whom Messrs. Osborn and Mercer acted. Tlva purchase price is not stated. POSSIBLE INDUSTRIAL 1 DEVELOPMENTS. It is slinderstood (says the "Manchester GuardiaA ") that one of the principal factors in the p rchase has been the discovery of salt in t district. Since the Lymm Salt va Works , re established a few years ago by aLiverp4ol company boring has taken place lat differint points alor.g the Bollin Valley near to Where the river joins the Manchester Ship Canal. Salt of an excellent quality has been foand.iii.abundance, and men are work- ing the • . - Ac</p>
        <p>something in the nature of a garden My on the lines of Port Sunlight will find a place in my plans for the future."</p>
        <p>In another interview Mr. Lever said the</p>
        <p>details of the purchase were quite correct. The scheme was yet in a fluid state and no details could he made public. The purchase, he explained, was not a personal one, but made on behalf the firm, and It was far too early to make any public announcement as to their intentions. " None of the commercial activities now carried on at Port Sunlight," he said, "wilt be transferred to Lymm. We have bought the Waterworks, the Lymm lake, and the halls, too. But we shall not make sosp there; NVe have ample room for development at Port Sunlight both for the extension of onr commercial undertakings and our housing scheme." "There is much more possibility and pro-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <p>meat canning. Salt is one of the very few things used in the manufacture of soap for which the Port Sunlight works are dependent on outside firms. THE DEWHURST ESTATE.</p>
        <p>The estate has been in the hands of the Dewhurst family for about half-a-century. It was purchased from a number of owners by the late Mr. George Charles Dewhurst, head of the cotton firm of G. and R. Dew- burst, of Manchester, Preston, and Black- burn. Mr. G. R. Dewhurst had three sons, Mr. George, Mr. John, and Mr. Robert • Dew hurst, but Mr. George and Mr. John, pre-deceased him. At his death he left his Scottish estate, Abruckill Castle, Perthshire, to his son Robert, and the Beechwood and Oughtrington estates in five parts to the four sons of Mr. George Dewhurst and to Mr. John Dewhurst's one son. Arrangements were made, it is understood, for the estate to be consolidated, but this led to some com- plications owing to the existence of a trust. These were added to by the death of Mr. ttleton Dewhurst—the eldest son of Mr. George Dewhurst—and the passage of his share to his son, who is at college. Under these cirumstances the family have decided to dispose of the estates. A PROPOSED GARDEN CITY. -</p>
        <p>The Lymm people are much satisfied with the new development, as it is recognised that it will add considerably to the prosperity of the place, and it will also bring increased traffic to the Ship Canal. It is confidently believed that nothing will be done adversely to affect the rustic charm of the district and its popularity as a residential area,especially on the Thelwall side. Lymm is regarded as one of the quaintest towns in England. It still possesses an old market cross and stocks, and it is a favourite pilgrimage for North of England cyclists. A deputation from the Lymm District Council have had an interview with Mr. Lever's agents, and it is expected that this big purchase may tend to develop Lvmm into a huge garden city, something after the style of Port Sunlight. STATEMENT BY PR. LEVER.</p>
        <p>In an interview with a press representative on Wednesday night Mr. W. Fl. Lever drew attention to the statements which have been made concerning his purchase of the Beech- wood and Oughtrington Estates, which comprise a large part of Lymm. " I am glad to have an opportunity," Mr. Lever said, "to correct some of the mis-state- ments that have been made concerning the Lymm purchase. It is stated that some time ago I conceived the idea of boring for my own salt at Heatley, and that, the experiments proving satisfactory, attempted to purchase, the lleatley portion of the estate from the Dewhurst Trustees. These statements are entirely wrong. I have never bored for salt, nor did I attempt to purchase the Ileatley portion of the estate. Of course I know there is salt on the estate. Nor is there any truth in the statement that it is the inten- tion of my son to take up his residence at Oughtrington Park. I know nothing of my alleged intention to take up three or four farms at Heatley for industrial purposes and other surmises in print of my intentions are equally wild. All I can definitely say is that Lever Brothers have not the slightest intention to establish a soapworks at Lymm- ,lt is extremely probable, however, that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>EDUCATION COMMITTEE. ELECTION OF CHAIRMAN AND DEPUTY-CHAIRMA N Petition for Feeding of School Children.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE. ELECTION OF CHAIRMAN AND DEPUTY-CHAIRMA N Petition for Feeding of School Children.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington EduceSion Committee Ives held at the Town Hall on slnteday evening. Alderman Bennett woe voted to the omo end there were also present )—Alderman A Bennett, Councillors T Riffles, W II Hellion, C Jeckson Holmes, H Roberts, W II J Jenkine, It Ilenshall, W J Wilson, T S Steel, Dr. McKee, J Pemberton, J Dickenson, Revs .1 Devi. end Father Neville Wilson, Mee Jackson Holmes, Messrs A el Jolley, H T Fairclourh, 0 Walker, Plinston) J Chrirnes, 11 head., C 11 Unewortb, J Upson, J Moore Murray (director), and R B Roberta (rsaistant director). ELECTION OF CHAIRMAN AND DEPUTY- CHAIRMAN. Mr. PLINSTEN moved that. Cooncillor H. Roberto be re-elected chairman of the committee. Ile bathe said, made a first clam chairman during the past year, having diwharged the duties most ably. Councillor Roberts did say last year that he thought his election, to the position masse effort to gag him—Height...l— ett however that might be, he had done the work admirably. Mr. FM1111.01,01 roconded. The worlc of the com- mittee, he said, hail gone on remerkably well during the past year, and he hoped it would do an well in the future. The proposition wee unanimously agreed to. Col noillor Iliert.P.s moved the reelection of Mr. Jolley as deptity-chairman. They all Anew the great Interest Mr. Jolley took in education in the borough— in fact there wee no more faithful servant of the com- mittee. Councillor .lACEstse: DoLNIEs 'seconded and this pro- posal wag also unenirnously approved. C,ouncill r ROBERTi-, in returning thanks, void Mr. Plinstoo slightly misunderstood him when bethought he (councillor Roberts) suggested last year that there was anything in the alines of a deliberate attempt to Reg him "I fancy " added Councillor Robert. "that such an attempt would be rather abortive—Heughter) —but at the same time! feel that in the position of chairman of this committee one has to endenvour to suppress one's own strong •iews upon particolor emis- sions in deference to the admitted right of the majority to decide the policy of the committee for the year. I have no hesitation in saying I believe I shall have again the courtwue consideration and cordial support of every member of the committee as I am proud to soy I feel I hey° had it during the past year. If any- thing goes wrong It will not be the fault of the com- mittee hut of your chairman. (laughter.) I sincerely hope, however, that none of you will hays ranee for complaint at the end of this year of office." Mr. Jot LEV also thanked the committee with the utmost einrority for that renewed mark of confidence THE FEEDING OF SCHOOL CHILDREN. PETITION FROM TRADkiS AND LABOUR COUNCIL. 'the DIRECTOR read a petition from the Wen ington Troika end labour Council protesting against the decision arrived at by the Committee re the provision of free meals for neceveitous school children and dernending that all children in whose homes the income was less than 2e. !II per heed per week, after rent was deducted, shordd he provided with free meals. There were twenty-three signatures to the petition. The Lirector suggested Wit the question nhould be refeired to the School Attendance Committee for recousideration. ANOTHER CHANCE FOR IRREGULAR ATTENDERS. When the retorn of attendance of member, of the committee during the past year was estimated, the Director pointed out that at the het meeting a resolu- tion was passed by which members who had not attended 25 percent. of the meetings of sub-committeee should be ineligible for reelection. That Standing Order wan nova in force. There had been no oppor- turity for the sub-committees to consider it and it woald therefore be fur the committee to decide whether it should take immediate effect or it s operation should he werended torn ye,. Gencillor VERRERTO, loin passed a. a Standing Order and it is not competent for this committee to yore it. The DIRFETOR t It in this committee which ulti- mately decides the exceptions. Alderman 111,5 brr, after announcing his intention to retire from two sub.committees, in consrquence of pressure of other duties, said he thought it would be unfair to make the new Steeling Order. retroepective. Members ought to be given the opportunity of amend. leg their ways under the new r fpn, e. 51r. KiNtitioN (the mover of the reoaluti an requiring a minimum of attendances) said his idea was that it shoold sutomatioally prevent any member of a committee who had not male 25 per cent, of attend- &amp;reels during the past twelve months Dona being eligible for re-election on that ese-comneittee. The CHAIRMAN It would be retrospective in that</p>
        <p>lento. Ti,. BEINSTON pointed out that several mem) ors of the authority had nut put in any attendances at the monthly committee meetings. Ile presumed that, according to the Standing Ordtr, thy aould now cease to he member. The Chairmen I don't think so. Toe Standing Order will operate rather curiously. If the clambers of the Town Council, who constitute the Education Authority, choose to re-appoint any gentleman who has not attended, I don't see what we can do. Mr. PLINviON Then %that good is the Standing Oder? The CHAIRMAN : It only appliel 10 the sub- committee., not, to the major committee, to far at members of the Town Council are concerned. The question is whether you desire the Standing Order to operate with regard to the constitution of the all,- committees to.night or not. It appears to me thet I notice not having been given to members of last year's committee., if there in any honest desire to amend their! ways, it would perhaps be advisable not to put in opera- tion the Standing Order to.pight. Councillor JnuKlN' l Vote ought to have been a lawyer. (Laughter.) After further discussion, it non decided, on the proposition of Mr. limp..., seconded by Alderman BENNETT, that the operation of the Standing Order be postponed for twelve month.. ' THE CHOICE OF EMPLOYMENT ACT. The Director reported, with reference to the Educe- lion (choice of employment)Act, Ittinthat the Hoard of Trade hid given notice that unless something wer done by local authorities tefore the end of this year, they would undertake to do the work. As it would hen long business to get the system into working order, it would be as w-ell if it could be referred ton sub-committee for consideration and retort. The powers conferred upon the authorities of County Floronghs Included To tuake arrangements for giving to boys end girl. under seventeen years of age, artsistance with respect to the choice of suitable employment, by the collect i .n and communication of information and the furnishing or advice. It the Education Committee did not take action, it meant that the Hoard of Trade would she charge of the children on leaving school and formulate their own scheme of finding them labour. Throughout the country it had been determined that that would he a great mistake and that the work should remain in the hands of the education authorities. Mr. RISS DON May I risk whether that deals with the juvenile advisory committees established in non' rection with labour exchanges The DlRElliolt Yea. Mr. Unction: Is not there a direrence of opinion abvut that 1 Haven't a great many towns decided thet the hest way is to work it through the labour</p>
        <p>• exchanges ? The Dlnvirron it—mill be worked through the labour exchanges ultimately, but the preliminary work is done in connection with education. Mr. UNSWoRtit suggested that the matter was so irocortact that a special sub-committee should be formed to deal with it. ColtScillOr PEMILFICroN in supporting the suggeation said he had grave doubts as to whether the Education committee bught to take the work up at all. Ile woutd let the Oovernmeot do it. The rates of Warrington were already ample and the Government ought to Bud the means of doing the work. Already the ratepayers of Warrington were having to pay unjustly for educa- tion. The Board of Trade could carry on the new scheme through the labour exchanges arid he did not see why the education committee should spend • single penny it. Mr. Croon said it wee not a question of any expendi- ture hesoada few shillings for postage, at the present time. lie supported the formation of a special cons- wittier. Already a large number of progreamve authori- ties had adopted that method and committeea bad been formed, consistiog of employers°, labour, repreisent a- fives of the labour borealis, education committees, town councils, leachers and all those who were inti- mately aseociated with child life. Very much good Noes being done and more was expected. The Board of Trade, throe th the labour bureau, was not omentially an agency for dealing with child labour and he felt strongly that the education committee ought to keep the crettol of ttat department in their own herds. (hear, hear). The question was referred to a sub-committee, con- sisting of Messrs. Kingdon. Upson, Walker, llneworth and Plinston. ATTEND YNCES 01' MANAGERS. The various rut:-committees having been appointed, Mr. Ibinaton, referring to the return of attendances of rep. waist ire managem, said he was pleased to fled that a fore er member Mr. T. P. Bradshaw) led the may, Laving visited the school of which he was nonage, twenty-seven times in the year. Ile set an exgreple to many of the managers. Several bad oely visited the schools once in the shots year. The Rev. J. Davies: The last spesker Is a manager of Christ Church school, but only put in one visit apart from the meetings. Mr. PLINSToN: I included myself when I said some of us might take a lesson. (Laughter). The CHAIRMAN lam afraid we did not all under- stand it in thst may. iLsughter. .ASSJCIATION OF EDUCATION COMMITTEES. The Chairman of the committee (Councillor 11.</p>
        <p>Roberts), the Chairman of the School Management I Committee (~on Stevenson. and the Director 1. Mr. J. , Moore Murray) were appointed representati yrs on the I Association of Education Committees.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>TRADE, EMPIRE AND WAR.</head>
        <p>TRADE, EMPIRE AND WAR.</p>
        <p>OUR BUSINESS EMPIRE IN SOUTH AFRICA. ,By Professor J. W. Graham.] A ItTll 'LE 11.</p>
        <p>The Empires of the world, however glorious in dominion, and conscientious in administration during their later and quieter years, have always been mere robbery to begin with; for they all represent a change , . of ownership of that which cannot be bought or sold the land, life and liberty of a nation or of a savage tribe. In primitive times such conquests may have made for progress, or they may oct. The conquests of the Buns and Turkadid not. We mostly hear, however, cilly the conquerors' nem-mot of the matter, aid we estimate our own Empire from our omen people's BCC nut of it. But in fart, DO motive more al°. ions than trade and investment has led us on to Empire. We are traders filet and go on to be civilisers some time later. The latest, and therefJre piesurnably the most enlightened, of our empire-building adventures shows the methods of sordid empire in a single clear case, which I believe to be a type, containing all the usual I eatures. . . .</p>
        <p>We began with mining for gold and diamonds, will large investments of capital, and with native labour, servile and spit down by the chiefs. Ind not actually enslaved. There was thus no opening made for a permanent white population, but only for great fortunes by cosmopolitan, generally Jew iall,capitalisis under the English flag, sllli the II mtinh t ispayers' protection. There was local difficu'ty with a stiff and rather stupid colony of Boer farmers - Kruger objected to Sunday labour and to the ways of turning corona, he once expelled five hundred prostitutes from Johannesbmg, he had memories of the Great Trek of hie bovho if, to escape British methods of government, he her) successfully reeinted one attempt at annexation, he objected to his people being outnumbered by the new mining population. Cecil Rhode. and Dr. Jameson determined to get him and his out id the way. Hostile feeling was provoked in England by the lying telegram saying that English women and children were iu danger at Johannesburg, aud the Raid Res 1110 for which the telegram had been hatched. This enlightening attack on the freedom and peace of the Transvaal was only in word dis- owned tit home. Cecil liluxles was exculpated in Parliament by Chamberlain,and the ft era then began their serious preparations fir defence ; for both English parties had white.warthed their eneniy. Then came the Milner-Chambei lain diplomacy and the war; —South Africa was devilated —tens of thousande were slain and 220 millions cf pfiunds were spent without glory. The yearly payments r f interest on the added debt,deprecialion in Cona-de.the stagnant reaction on trade at home,must be added to the total cost of this Imperialistic adoentore. The enemy this time was an unusual fine ; no we hive, instead of a crown colony, a free Parliamentary government, with the aer majority DPCO.3IIiIP in power, under the ministry of General Botha. Boer ide is rule. The nativea are excluded from the franchise in all the new states The resident British Indians are persacided and others are kept out, in spite of the principles held at borne' and really in contempt of the idea of a British Empire. The whale is a story of lying, of greed and of pride, on the pirt of the principals. of sorrow and suffering he the British public and by the Boer and British soldiers, and it is a ridiculous conclusion from the point of view of conquest, or of spread of enlightened British ilea., democratic and humanitarian. .</p>
        <p>Our behaviour in South Africa is the test of what we are equal to in dealing with lower cr simpler races. To obtain regular, abundant and cheap black labour to work the gold and diamond mines has been the single object of Europmns there. To that end we have separated the natives from their land in recent and well-known instances ; chiefly in order that, deprived of any other way of maint doing themselves, they may be compelled to work at the mines. So long as they had their kraals and their farms and their cattle, it was °Fen to them to like three months on the Rand when the pay suited them, and return with their wages to their families and their old habita They were thou in the name position as the English workman would be if he had access to land, and could at any time take to itecultiratirn when unemployelor onderpaid. Of all the ways of raising wage., here or eleewhere, this way is the safest, tl,e healthiest and the moat eff,„ctual. The English system of I Ind ownership is the worst obstacle in the way of raising English waive. So the policy of the Johannesburg mine owners is to get the natives from their homes and their farming, and to nettle them, without chief,or hit*, or land or cattle, in huts near the mines ; whither they rtn bring their families, and limed a cr ntiouous succession of mine labourers. They would live in the company'a huts at monopily rents, buy in the company's or come allied company's stores at m nopoly prices, and be p mid wages which would be decided by the allied employers, i.e , the Chamber of Mines, dominated already by a tingle concern, the Eckstein intermte. They could not reroute or ttrike ; they would ham no alternative. Pass-laws recall be used to prevent their migration, white courts would punish riots, and a legislature in wh;ch they are never to have votes would support whatever conduced to the prosperity of the owners of the mines, We have here the sort of hopelem serldom which results from an Imperialism with commercial aims, The benell s of Western civilisation, Christianity and education, do not aprear on the private minutes of the directors of the Mining syndicates. The mine owners in German West Africa confronted Ily a wages dinpute, cut off food and water fiom ,heir black labourers on strike it) October, 10111.</p>
        <p>The only way by which a European cumection would have benefited the natives would have been by improving their agiiculture, and setting, in the neighbourhoal of their kraals, an object le,. JD of decent civilised family life, lived on the find. We should do as the most enlightened missionaries do. Johannesburg Christianity, as exhibited by the habits of the fortune-honters of the Rand, themselves awav from their homes, and hoping urine time to leave the country with a share of their riches, is probably felt by the Bantus to be the faith of a 10,rribly drerylertt enrage tribe, out cursedly clever. In Jamaica, British Guiana, Trinidad, and other West Indian Islands, and in Manikins, cur Govern- ment works a system of black laimur or of coolie labour from India. which is probably as well managed and as satisfactory as toil in exile can ever lie. By compar,son with these pla^es we can estimate how costly to England and to Africa has been the curse of Rhodes.</p>
        <p>All hard and dangerous work in South Africa is done by the natives. If anyone lies to venture into a dangerous hole with perhats bad gas or perhaps a lion in it, the native is re...t. The whites are parasites on lab,ur and a steady tendency to languor and i- ilemoralitration due to parasitism s felt by those who R. nut there. But it would be very difficult to absorb South Africa by commercial conquest without becom- ing parasitic. Even an international control would be no better, for the mine owners are international already themselves. England is their adopted cottony in moist cases. A sordid empire, however European, would end in the came way. The Basutoland Protectorate, the condition of Kliama's country. and the work of Si • George Taubman Goldie in Nigeria and Sir Harry Johnston in Nyeasaland, are examples of what can be done in Africa on other than sordid linen. Here, at the back of the lairds of capable adminis.rators, was a sense of public wellbeing, not nrofits for speculators in g.. 14 and diamonds. In thrum regions there has been no legislation like the Glen Grey Art, imposed a money tax on all who did nut spend three months a year at the mines —a tax which they had n 3 money to pay except by hiring themselves out. They use no money and need none in their kraals. The stealing of native cattle. the use of prisoners as mine lab liners after a Ixogua rising in Bechuanaland, aid the whole Hut Tax policy are among the devices for practically forcing the native farmer to take to mining for the , profit of Europeon employers. But such cots as those of the Boer war are 'no adequate wrasure of what the sreculator owes to tl it taxpayer. Our army exists to be ready for wars for the waintenance and particularly far the extenniaa of ills profit-mongering Empire. NJ one really Antici- pate. that any European Power would attack Eigland g\ except on account of some foreign rivalry in ag erosion in far away lands. Our ;unfit and enormously 1 rest- ing military burden is therefore due t Impede sin r and is 'a be debited to th it party of unpat ; linanc:ers. And th&amp;r demands are not glutted. Now the army can only be voluntary increase raising heavily the rate of pay, sass to appeal, better paid class. This added expenee we Ai from Universal military service is therefore possi</p>
        <p>ttengivii alv Anti k Eigla gB(e .L si - I ro t iotic Increasey by Allo a ebiink )ssi le</p>
        <p>and with it a p or average of town bred material, unlois Ire can rheek Oar Imperio/iiits The only other plan is to fight by the help of negroea or Flasutos in India or America, Ghoorkas or Afghans in South Africa, and in general to maintain an Empire by arming and drilling and taking to foreign Janda as cheap mercenaries our subject dark races. We have already gone as far on this perilous and downhill path as is considered safe. Thus from a military point of view we are risking the stability of our Ernp:re by further following on this sordid way. JOHN W. GRAHAM.</p>
        <p>To be ronlitirted).</p>
        <p>loon Crow ottr. £6l.—Williern Jones, of no Axed I !abode, mu charged ith running away from the I I borough of Warrington and louting bin live children I chargeable to the Union. Prisoner retrerked I hat he had never been out of the borough. Mr. B. Shearer, who prosecuted mid that Bre children were tiger] I 10, 8,6, 4 end 21 years and were admitted to the Work- house about 12 months ago and had consequently cost I the town £64. Daring that time the prisoner had not I spent a tingle penny upon thorn. He had not be3n seen since last July. The Chief Constable (Mr. Martin Nicholls) said that the prisoner had been heron, the Court 13 times on various charge. including neglect of children. Prisoner Mon sent to god for thre3 months. Ttrestig v.—Before Councillor If. Roberts in the chair), Alderman Bolton, Colonel J. A. lidelsten, and Mr %V. Richardson. EXPINSINT: Tunis JOURNICr.—Fines of 406, and costs and 10s. and costs respectively were Imposed upon Walter Tallow, of 19, Adelsidgestreet, Bradford, Manchester, for travelling on the Lend N.-VV. Rat Iwny from Holyhead to Warrington without baying previously paid his fete and e ith having given e fal.o name and addle.,. Defendant did not appear. Mr. Tate, prose- cuted on behalf of the Railway Company.—The evidence showed that the defendant, when asked for bin ticket at Holyhead produced one with the date erased nod when asked about it said it most have been rubbed off by contact with his purse. Defendant gave his name and address an W. 1..11 rano of Welldon-etreet, Manchercer, and gave it at Chester oleo. While at Warrington the defendant changed his car and went into a first-class comrartment and whet, the porter c inte for him ticket beached bow much it wan to Manchester an he had Joel boarded the trim. The porter took the fare offered him and told the Inspector, who had been comraimicited with from Holyhead and the Inure-nor went to the defendant and mentioned that he had been informed that he bad travell d from Holyhead. This the defendant then admitted.</p>
        <p>looT Tter,4 r.—Flarry Egan, of no nettled abode, was charged on remand with haring etolen a pair of boots valued at tie. the property of Meaner. Briggi and Co., Market.gate.—Prisoner pleaded guilt) and the Chief Constable mentioned that he had been convicted three times preolouily at Wolverhampton.—lle was neat to Reid for 14 derv, FINDINGS NOT KNEDINGS !—William IVOI•ley, • newepaper boy, of fi, Fountibmstreet Wirrington, was charged with, on the 23rd March, felortionely stealing by finding end taking no steps touardeascermining the owner, a Indy a gold ring, the property of Miss Riche' Green of Sankey-RD( et. Primmer said be did not know that be was atealing.—The Cider Constable raid thnt the owner of the ring placed the ring in her pocket on the date in question and when she gut to the Picture- drools she found that it wits missing, al she gave : information to the police. Prisoner admitted hiving ' found the ring opposite tha prosecotrien shop and also I admitted having offered it to two men, for Va. These latter in their evidence mid that when the prisoner asked them to buy the ring he mid that he had found it, had been to the police station and left it there for a week and no one baring claimed it, it had been given to him. That, the Chief Constable elated, waa untrue. Prisoner, who had been before the court 13 timee before on various charges, including larceny, undertook tt go into a home and the ease was accordingly adj_ourned for three month.. FODEM AN Fc,ri AN Cu DIM'S MI-HERAVIOUR.— Ellen Dutton, 72, Chorley-street, Werrington, applied for a reparation order against her latebend, William Dutton, foremnn fustian cutter, on the ground that he was an hihitual drunkard. Mrs. Dutton said her hosnand was drunk three or four times every week and when he came home he knocked her about I eeidert damaging the fornitore.—A separation order was granted with 103. a week alimony to the wife. WZDNFADII,.—Megsra. Charles White (in the chair) J W. Hayward, Councillor C. Jackson Ilolmes and Alderman Splethuret. _</p>
        <p>A Corer Ilttonqsr, —lame. Moran, of 11, Back Crossley-street, Warrington, was charged with tumult- ing his wife on the I.Bth ult. Prisoner appeared the previous day in ancwer to a summon., but as he was not write sober he was remanded in custody until Wednesday. His wife stated at the hearing that he was frequently reaching home stoutl night and on the date in rpteation when be arrived home he knocked her down and beside, getting hold of her throst,kicketi hen—On promising to sign the pledge prisoner was noel 3m, sad costs or 14 data. P4W,M1101,... GRIEVANCE.—Mre. Ads Breilshsw, 47, Foretsr-atreet, and Mrs. Ellen Jackson, 123, Long. fordietrret, were summoned for wilfully airing false information ton p twobroker,ro wit,Mr.llirchall manager of Mr. Jacknon's nawnshop,Orford- lane. —M r. A. Browne who prorecuted fir the Pawnbrokers' Ass sci Alen, said that the matter in question was a very serious matter to the pawnbrokers of Warrington who every rear 10.1. VA by the practice. In this case Mrs. Bradshaw gave the false information and Mrs. Jackson aided and abetted her. It appeared that the former at 43a p.m. went to Mr. Jacks m's shop, of which she wars a regular customer. She took with her a cost nod •est and asked Mr. Birchedl to lend her Rs. 61 on there, b it on seeing the splendid condition of the goods he asked her if they belonged to her and she said that they belonged to Mrs Jackson. Mrs. Bradshaw then fetched Mrs. Jackson who stir' that the garments belonged to her eon. She stated that she bought them at a Manchester auction room. It was then aacertained by Mr. Birchen that the good, had been bought by • Mrs. NI orelaai, a late secondhand clothes dealer, from Nfr. Hickman. Such practioem were continually pro- ceeding, women buying goods and sending respectable women, who were regular customers, to the pawnshops, becalm° they colild get more money on them than if strangers went.—Mrs. Jackson said that Ike never saw the clothes and tie reason she said that they li-longed to her was In order to gst Mrs. Bradshaw out of the trouble —Mrs. Nlore'and, of 12 I, Longf ord-atrost.hav riving evidence ea to the rumbase of the goofs, Mr. Brooce said that he would not press the case as the matter had bean brought before the publics. The pawn- brokers of the district were determinei, he sail, that loch practices 'Mould cease.—Defendants were die. missed on payment of the cost of the summons.</p>
