        <p>REV. L. J. T. DARWALL, VICAR OF WALTON. The pretty village church of St. John the Evangelist, Walton, is roe f the most striking and picturesque features of a pleasant rural landscape. It is the centre of much useful and devoted</p>
        <p>work, and has also a great reputation for the beauty and musical (xcellenee of its services. The Rev. L. J.l'. Darwall, IID., has been Vicar of Walton since 15A5, in which year he was appointed by the late Sir Gilbert Greensll upon the completion :of the new Church. lie was scholar of Winchester and of New College, Oxford, and was ordained deacon in I'M' and priest in ISSI by Bishop kfackarneea of I Word. After a short time as assistant master at! Cowley Military College, he becione curate of All Saints, Oxford, and assistant chaplain of Lincoln College, holding also a minor ca,ponry (or " chaplaincy " as it is there called) at the Cathedral. Thence, after a short time spent 89 curate of the historic church of lflley, he came to Walton. Be is desee.ded from an ohl Cheshire Family, and numbers among his anceatets the composer of Dartvall's 118th " of which well-known titre lie pooseases the miginal manuscript. Keeping up the family tradition, he has himself taken much interest in church mus!c, and bas contributed several items to the " Bristol Psalter service, and Anthem Book " " Hymns for the children of the church" and to' The Anglican Organist" flji everting service in (7 is in regular use at Liverpool Cathedral and elsewhere. Mr. Darwall in held in great esteem and respect by his parishioners. Ilia sermons are always thooghtful, couched in perfect English, and full of gots' matter - in fact, he possesses the happy knack of being elan to compress the greatest amount of instruction within the narrowest compass. Ile takes a special interest in Church history and kindred subjects, and recently delivered a valuable series of lectures on the l'rayer Book, before the in. mob ers of the Warricgion Church Hooding Uni,</p>
        <p>Baden-PoweWs Boy Scouts. The Boy Scut" movement, 1., made considerable progress in Warrington ; and it is impossible not to feel considerable intersat in movement that is vo calculated to provide a healthy pursuit for young people, combining as it does recreation of the best kind with an education that at the same time develops the intelligence. The Vicar fe)ys Holy Trinity Pariah Maurice) would be very glad to have the movement taken up in Trinity parish, hut that can only be if some gentleman will come forward and act es ecout-rnester, sad make himself ahrog with others ruponsible for the conduct of the movement. St. Paul's Church. The current isn't,' of St. Pawl's Parish Mega/due con- tains the following letter :—My dear Priends,-1 are delighted to be borne and working amongst you agsin. lent deeply grateful to you all for the way you have rallied round my two coffeepots in my alreence ; it inn great joy to meter know the work has gone on no sat is- fsctority. May I ven'ttre to sppeal to you all through the medium of our Magazine, for a most generous support to the efforts we are tasking at our annual Sale of Work, on Tuesday, April *Si h. You all know howl we need your help, t'arr moat raise at least £3rlo, which , meane, if every reader will do his or her shareit can be done usily.—Thanking you, in noticipation, believe MP, ever yours sincerely, W. BRA, P.ANIP." The Bible Tercentenary. • Ibis hardly to be wondered at, sr) s "Holy Trinity Pariah Megesine," that the d'o Itli anniversary of the puhlication of the most ',velar version of the Scriptures in the English tongue, should rouse the Interest of Christians during thepresent year and cerise them to celebrate it as a National Festival. As in most parts of the kingdom there will be the opportunity for a great gathering in Warrington towards the end of the rear, nni it is to be hoped that it will haze the effect of bringing home to the minds of Chrietiana whet they owe to the Bible, so that interest in the study of it will he revived and the foolish jealousy which seems to have 'prong up with respect to it in some .plarters will be banished from our midst,</p>
        <p>tiv4:::: 1 r..:501t:,.... , : ~,,,, _ , I ~., , , . a .......)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>Confirmation at the Parish Church.</head>
        <p>Confirmation at the Parish Church.</p>
        <p>The Lord Bishop of Liverpool administered the rite of confirmation to 213 candidates at the Warrington Parish Church on Saturday afternoon and a large con- gregation usenabled for the very impressive service. After the laying on of handy, the Bishop in a short I address reminded the confirtnees, how they could keep the great gift which Gonl had given to them that day, namely, by faith, by c nnmulaion, and by nervier. lla had laid his hands on them to convince them that they were the children of God, and that It,, was their Father. Tbe laying on of hands also meant that (loada bands of goodness would always be over them to guide and atrengthen them. Although his the Bishop'e) Ihands had only rested on them for a moment, God's hands would e•er rest on them. If they trusted in .the ' • '' '</p>
        <p>—s, hive of God they would always be happy. The Bishop urged upon the young people the necemity for Bible reading end frequent I•lronounion and also asked them : to do some act of love sal kindness to somebody every dar. All the cendidates with the exception of trio from the Grammar Soho ,I, are members of the Parish :Church snd Mission congregai i on. no clergy in attendance were the Revs. Ilorace Gray, 'l'. A. J. Rid• patb, A. J. Brett, and T. Vickers.</p>
        <p>St. James Church Latchford. The Vicar on Current Events. in the course of his monthly letter to the parishioners, the Vicar of Si. James' (liew. H. B. Firth) says For msny years there have been attempts made by persons in high position, of authority to bring about a state of peace between the nations, and now it seems that a great step in the direction of peace is within measurable distance of being taken. Thnt Christian countries have to spend millions of pounds annually to build new Dreadnoughts—that the European powers should Bed it necessary to be armed to the teeth, is a sad and miserable state of things. An appal to law and not to force in the ideal which we should place before ourselvee as civilised Christian nations. TiLat God will bless the endeavours made to promote the peace of the world is a prayer which should constantly Le on our lips at the present time—a prayer which should be uttered with the deepest and most real conviction. ' Then there has token place the Ter-Centenary of the publication of the Aiiihorired Version of the English Bible. Sermons end speeches innumerable lines been made on the subject and uttered within the last week. It is said that in no other country are !tibias to be</p>
      </div>
