        <p>found so universally in the homm of the people no in Englend. But urn our Bibles rend very notch now -3- da3 a ? 'Much of our bowing about en " open Bible" is vain, for although en have the Hi ile in • Isneusge we ran understand we melte tut little use of it. If the Ter.Centenery of the Authorized Vertionstimulates the interest of people to reed the Scriptures more diligently for themselves, it will be well. One serse of the Bible read every day makes little difference at the end of this week ; it makes a wonderful difference however, et the end of s life. The roan who hea found out for himself the Treammen of the Word of God bee gained store of riches that can I lon obtained from no other source in the world. In shout • fortnight we ahall have come to Good Friday, and I appeal most earnestly to all our readers to keep the day holy, ltow any decent Churohnieu can rpend the day in pleseure •nd enjoyment peewee my understanding. I.set year our people responded splen- didly to the appeal made to them to come to the I morning servo. nn Good Fridey ; we repeat the sppeal snd beg of all our people—our non especially—to those whose side they ere on he attendance in the Rouse of God on the morning of the day ahen we cornmemorete the Crucillxion of our Lord and Master.</p>
