      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</head>
        <p>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</p>
        <p>By Philip Snowden, M.P.</p>
        <p>THE SRO* HOURS BILL</p>
        <p>The Bill which was read a second time ' last Friday afternoon is one which affects' very closely the interests of a very large and important section of the community. It proposes the regulation of the hours of labous of something like a million shop assistants, and less directly deals with the hours during which shops may be open. I have knownt," no measure introduced into Parliament during the last five years which is so uriversally approved in principle as this Shop Hours Bill. It does not divide, as measures dealing with the hours of labour usually do, employers and workmen into opposing classes. The shopkeepers appear to be as anxious to see something done to lessen the hours as do the assistants themselves. From no quarter does th( re come any hostility to the measure on principle, if we except the solitary figure of ,the honourable baronet who sits for the City of London, and who boasts that he is "neither Socialist, Radical, nor Democrat." The Bill was read a second time without a division; and even Mr. Bottomley, who moved the rejection of the Bill, but who did not press his motion to a division, expressed himself as being in favour of the restriction of shop hours by a universal statutory regulation. THE SIXTY HOURS WEEK.</p>
        <p>The shop assistants and the shopkeepers support the Bill, though both desire to see the measure made more thorough in some particulars. The Bill proposes the limitation of the hours that assistants may be employed to sixty per week, exclusive of meal times. The assistants would like to see the hours fixed at sixty per week, inclusive of meal times, but as was pointed out in the course of the debate, if that were done the legal hours of shop assistants would be much below those of the workpeople employed in factories and workshops, and it is difficult to argue a case in favour of shorter hours in shops than in factories and workshops. It should be remembered, too, that there is no legal limitation of the hours of male adult labour in factories and workshops. While one would very much like to see the Bill amended in the way the shop assistants propose, it I _must at the same time be recognised that the limitation to sixty per week exclusive of meal times will mean a reduction in the working hours for thousands of shop assist- ants of twenty or more hours a week. This Bill will not be the end of legislation dealing with shop hours, and after a short experience of the operation of the new Act it may be possible to make a further reduction, especi- ally if in the meantime, there has been some reduction of hours in other great industries. THE HALF-HOLIDAY QUESTION.</p>
        <p>The shopkeepers support the Bill, but they unanimously, so far as organised expression has been given to their views, desire to see the measure very much improved. When the Bill was originally introduced by the late Home Secretary, Mr. Herbert Gladstone, it proposed a compulsory Half-Day closing. This has been taken out of the Bill, and this deletion has, in the opinion of the shop- keepers, greatly weakened the usefulness of</p>
        <p>the measure. Some of the tradesmen's associations have gone so far as to express the opinion that unless the Compulsory Half-Holiday can be re-inserted in the Bill it is not worth while persevering with it. The shopkeeper, of course, is not bound by the limitation of hours which is imposed in regard to the employment of assistants, and the only way in which the shopkeeper can be assured of a half-holiday, or of a reason- able time for closing at nights, is by some legal fixing of the hours. It was made very evident during the debate last week, that there is very general sympathy with the shopkeepers in their desire to have the com- pulsory half-holiday, and the Home Secre- tary, though not giving a definite promise to leave this matter open in Committee, did say sufficient to indicate that he was open to be convinced that it was a practicable thing to do. It should not be difficult to convince the Home Secretary in Committee that it is not only the practically unanimous desire of the ' traders that the compulsory half-holiday should be made law, but that the difficulties which arise from the variety of local customs and from the variety of shops can be over- come.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>MINORITY INFLUENCE.</head>
        <p>THE MINORITY INFLUENCE.</p>
        <p>If the question of a weekly half-holiday is left to local option as is now provided in the Bill, the difficulty which has prevented the more general adoption of that practice in the past will operate in the future. In the mattt r of the regulation of shop hours, voluntaryism has broken down. There is no possibility of getting a satisfactory settle- ment of this question except by compulsion upon the minority which will not freely fall in with the general body of shopkeepers. It is proposed in the Bill that Home Office Commissioners should be sent down to the various localities, where there has been a desire expressed to have the weekly half. holiday, to assist in bringing about this reform, as well as earlier closing on other days. This cannot work very satisfactorily, and judging by the way in which a very small minority has been able to defy the great majority under the present Shop Hours law, there is good reason to fear that local option under the new Act will achieve no better results. But in view of the strength of the feeling In favour of compulsory half- day closing, it is very likely that the Bill will be amended in Committee to meet this demand. . _</p>
        <p>An equally thorny question raised by the Bill is that of Sunday trading. It is admitted that this subject is so difficult that it would not be possible to proceed to the extent of enforcing the closing of all shops on Sunday. But if universal Sunday closing is not possible, the case for a compulsory half- holiday on one other day of the week is made all the stronger. Very few shopkeepers who open on Sunday like the practice, and they would gladly welcome legislation which would enable them to get a day's rest on the Sabbath. But they are foroed to open because their competitors in the trade do so. The Bill makes Sunday closing universal, with certain scheduled exceptions. It is claimed that the provisions of the Bill as they stand will prevent an increase in Sunday trading, which has been on the increase in recent years. The trades which are exempted from tile operation of Sunday closing are those which supply liquor, refreshments, motor and cycle accessories, newspapers, and under certain regulations, bread, milk, and tobacco. Barbers' shops may be open until 2 p.m. on Sunday. The exclusion of Barbers' shops from complete Sunday closing is very much against the wishes of the vast majority of the trade, and a determined effort will be made in Committee to amend the Bill by taking out the programme for partial opening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <p>THE ADVANCE OF STATE ACTION. The Bill will probably be very drastically amended in Committee, and when it becomes ' law it will confer enormous benefit on some- thing like two millions of our working popu- lation. It is computed that there are about a million shop assistants, and something like half a million shops in the United Kingdom. There is no class in the commun- ity more entitled to consideration than those who are engaged in the distributive trade. Their hours of labour are so long that they have little opportunity of sharing in the , social pleasures which other people enjoy. ,Close confinement, often in unhealthy atmospheres, tells on the health of these people. The death rate from such diseases as consumption is very high among shop 'assistants and shopkeepers. No class in the community ought to be allowed to work such long hours that they have no time to ' give to their families and to their duties as citizens. This Shop Hours Bill makes another advance on the road which leads , away from the old position that the State had no right or business to interfere with , a grown up man. It is now recognised that the State has no right to let a man kill himself by overwork; nor have a minority the right to do as they wish when their action is unwillingly condemning others to do as they do not desire. The public have an interest in this Shop Hours Bill. But it may be safely assumed that the people will not grumble whatever Parliament may do in this matter. So far, as the public are responsible for unreasonable shop hours, their crime in indifference,rather than deliberate intention. If the hours of shops are shortened, and if earlier closing be adopted, the public will quietly fall in with the new arrangements. The public have no desire to corn pel shopkeepers and shop assistants to work long hours. The public will regulate their shopping according to the time the shops are open. There will be no less trade done if the shops are open only two-thirds of the hours they now open. It has been most gratifying to see such unanimity among the shopkeepers in favour of a strong measure for regulating their hours; and the small and unorganised shop- keepers need not fear that they will suffer from the legislation which is about to be enacted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>ICHILDREN'S CORNER, i Conducted by UACLE CALEB.</head>
        <p>ICHILDREN'S CORNER, i Conducted by UACLE CALEB.</p>
        <p>TALES OF AN INSPECTOR.</p>
        <p>When I was a boy et school we used to look forward to the coming of the 'napalm's with very great dieted, but since I have grown older I have found out that Inenectots (like newspaper Editors and other great ones whom I used to fear) are only human after all. I have just hewn lo king through a book which one of them loan written end it is full of all aorta of : amusing stories. The author was for a bac time an Inspector of schools in Cheshire and I feet he mutt be • dear old man and not at all dreadful to meet. I shall only have apace to copy rim of his tales die week though I may give you more by and by. This is how he talks about examining girls' necillework. As a rule the "garments" were laid out on a long i table. Alternately they were dealt out to the makers or to those who sere credited with makingsomething It was our duty to go on " visiting rounds" among the desks, to pick up the hateful things, to look wise, and, if possible, to stake an appropriate remark here and there. This wan a tarrible ordeal, for each girl regarded her work as the most important output of the whole year, and very few lad any sorption that we lacked knowledge. It happened one day that while I was thus engaged my driver got weary of waiting for me in the road with his dog cart and bailed a little maid coming out of school. "Be the gentleman a-coming? " " Yes," she replied, " he s juel a-lookin' at our sawin'." The driver was astoniehed. " What dew he know about raisin' then ?" " I expect," said the dear child, "he know a little about everytbink." It was a cruel judgment, though kindly meant if she had said " a very little" ebe could (within limits) have accurately described our peeler lions. After rne of these ordeals my assistant al il I , adjourned in an unusually exhausted state to " The Clown "for lunch. It was market day, and extra help had been hired to meet the demanda of the farmers ; no that we were waited upon by a stranget who began asking whether one of us bad not been at our school that morning. We pleaded guilty, jointly and severally. "My little gel," she went on to say, "is wond rful put out, she rime back cryin' till she is s!ek. 'The inspect 't; COMP: alio say, 'and pick up my weak, and he look at it. "Lewis," he ay, "-here's a piller- slip," and oh, mother, it was my chimera as I'd took such pains with,' and she did cry ever O." ,lily asoistata behaved shamefully ; he declared I was the culprit, and be laughed till the tears ran down his cheeks. THE BOY'S CHOICE. Suppose a fairy said to we, 'New, think who would you be If I could make yoo anyone?' I'd anew, r, ' Let me see! I'd like to have a lot of gold— You're sure there'll le no hitch?— So make nie Mr. Punk-book, 'cos He's rich! ' Oh, no; I wouldn't though! I'd rather Rule • lot of men : Have servant. in fine liveries, And castles nine or ten. I'd like the folk to shout and cheer As I rode out in state— SO make me King of England, cos He's groat! 'Bat stay; perhaps I'm hasty, though! I'll have another chola ; I'd like to bee soldier bold; I'm sure I should rejoice To fight and AV in great battles And my country's honour save— So make me General Fear-taught, 'we He's brave! 'No, no; I'd rather be a man Of learning and of wit, Who were he asked a question hard Could straightway answer it ; For I've been told that knowledge is A thing we all should seine— So make me like my teacher, 'cos He's wise!</p>
        <p>' Nay, do not leave me, fairy; let It. choose but one time MOM For I've just remembered something That I quite forgot before, No, I would not be one of these I've mentioned if I could— Please make me just like Father, 'cos Ile'e good 7 '</p>
