      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>What Children Like.</head>
        <p>What Children Like.</p>
        <p>It scams the most natural thing no the world that children should like sweetstuffs, puddings, tarts, currant cake, and so on—but has it ever occurred to you that there is a reason for this ? As we all know children are much more natural in their likes and dislikes than grown-ups ; and it is a scientific fact that the nearer we get to Nature the most truly do the tastes indicate the needs of the body. Children love currant cake or any other confection:ry contain- ing Currants, and there is nothing more wholeseine, more nutritious, or more suited to the children's natural apprtite than Currants in any form. The king's physician recommends them as an article of diet for young and old alike, and at all seasons of the year. Currants contain the right material for flesh forming, brain making, and stamina building, and the nutriment is easily assimilati.d. When we point out that this delicious little fruit called Currants is in reality • grape, and that it takes 31b. of berth grapes to make I lb. of Currants, the high food value will be understood. The number of ways in which currants can be prepared is shown in a little book on Currant Cookery, which your grocer mill glaillv hand you fire on request. The wise housewife should always have something on her table containing Cumuli.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>ANTIQUARIAN CHAPTERS.</head>
        <p>ANTIQUARIAN CHAPTERS.</p>
        <p>THE ROMANCE Or THE ENGLISH BIBLE.</p>
        <p>There is no story quite like that of Bible translation. From the days when Hebrew was turned into Greek to meet the needs of the newer Greek-speaking world, up to the present hour when the same writings are changed into the poor tongue of the Esquimaux or South Sea Islander the tale has gone on, unended and unending, each corner of the world offering its own bit of romance and adding its quota of interest.</p>
        <p>Those of us who have grown up in England with the music of a great version of the Bible in our ears do not always realize the slow degrees and many by which that book grew into being, and it cannot fail to be of advantage to us during this ter-centenary year of the King James version to read the numberless facts which the magazines and newspapers have gathered for us. Our own contribution to the great and interesting discussion may hest be made in a chapter of gleanings a little removed perhaps from the usual field, but sufficient to merit appreciation and remembrance. In what guise did the Bible story creep into the English language? As an ecclesiastical book and written in the Latin of Jerome it doubtless came with the first missionaries, and Bible stories would form the substance of the earliest sermons that fell upon Saxon ears. But who first turned this ancient Latin into the native speech, to make it, in the famous phrase, " understanded of the common people "I According to the Venerable Bede, our most , trustworthy historian of a dim, though</p>
        <p>This table and candlesticks are preserved in Lutterworth Church as the ones used by Wycliffe when translating the Bible.</p>
        <p>famous age, the first chapter of the Bible romance is the most romantic of all.</p>
        <p>yEDMON AND HIS SUPERNATURAL oirr.</p>
        <p>More than 1,200 years ago a poor Saxon cow-herd lay asleep with the cattle in a stable quite close to the Abbey of Whitby. He had spent the evening in company with his masters and companions, who, to beguile the tedium of long winter evenings had arranged to sup and sing together. The harp had been handed round and several had piped out doggerel verse. "Each agreed for glee sake to sing in turn,"says Bede,but when Ciedmon saw the harp coming to him his soul was in heaviness and he rose from the board and fl.d to his lodging among the cattle. And now in sleep a strange thing happened. The stable became like the stable of Bethlehem, lighted up with "the glory of the Lord." . „ .</p>
        <p>Sing, Ciedmon," he said, "sing some song to me." " I cannot sing—I fled from the place of songs." " Yet " said the shining one,"Thou shalt sing for me." "What shall I sing ? " "The beginning of created things." Then his soul was stirred and ho sang:</p>
        <p>Now mustyre wane The glory of Heaven's Kingdom, The Creator'. might And hi mind's thought; Glorious Father of men The Lord the Eternal, The vision passed but the power remained, and the cow keeper went out with the morn- ing to sing mighty songs, and men marvelled that the Lord had come again to the stable to work wonders in the earth. Hilda was Abbess of Whitby and when she heard the tale she translated to him from</p>
        <p>Latin parchments the stories written there. These he quickly changed to fine and flowing verse and both learned ones and ignorant stood amazed at his power. Fascinated, the Abbess bade him lay aside his dress and take up the monastic life. Henceforward, Ckedmon did no manual work but waited for the music from above as he wove the undying stories of the Bible into terse and stirring lines. Then as I understand, went The direipler together All to Galilee, Inspired by the Spirit, The Holy Son of God. Whom they saw Were the Lord's Son. This, of course, is not Bible translation as we understand it, it is paraphrase; but it placed in Saxon speech the facts of Scripture an 3 sang them into simple minds. How effectively he sang his way to the hearts of the people may be judged from the statements of William of Malmesbury, who declared centuries later that when copies of the songs were discovered the mere touch of them worked miracles of healing. But Bede speaks of greater thing than these in telling us that Caedmon's songs drew souls from worldliness and kindled many to the quest of the life celestial. The singing of the Scripture songs stirred others to an ambition for poetry, but, says Bede " none came near to him." Caedmon's work would inspire no marvel to-dap, but judged by their influence his words were things of power. When he died, early in the eighth century, Eadhelm, Bishop of Sherborne, was at work translating the Psalms into Anglo-Saxon. and Egbert, Bishop of Holy Island, was completing a version of the gospels. Then at Jarrow-on-Tyne the famous Bede took up the work and turned the Psalter and New Testament into the language of the day. With the death-film covering his eyes he dictated his version of the Gospel of John. "There remains but one chapter, Master, said he scribe "but it seems hard for you to speak." " Vey it is easy" the beloved scholar replii "write write quickly." With tears the scribe ,wrote on. The old lips(were quivering. "And now father, only onersentencc remain." Bede dictated it. "T* is fi</p>
        <p>fished</p>
        <p>le youi</p>
        <p>a continent has ever kix</p>
        <p>' • -or 41.1 LT CUM'S TABLE AND CANDLE,'"</p>
