        <p>Desire not thou thy neighbour's ance with unright. Work not thou thee golden gods a King Alfred's work as a ti deserves the highest praise. His homely and is written to be underst may smile at his " water-seoc-n describing the man with a dropsy, better for his day. And the die&lt; "learning youth" and the Sabt rests dae. ' Alfred did not live the Psalms but he translated mot Gospels,which parts of the Bible eeer been the favourites a thousand yea] In example of his style the Lord' may be copied here. We give, of co modern equivalent of his Saxon. Our father which art in heaven, thy name, Come thy kingdom' Be thy in heaven and in earth, Our loaf etanlial give us to-day. And forgive ne So as we I, rgive our debts, And nn into temptation hot deliver every one f WYCLIFFE'S BIBLE. After Alfred and his contemporar is a long pause in the story of Bible tion. The disturbance following tl invasion had hardly given place to, the Norman Conquest brought ch . . _</p>
