      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>i OUR SERIAL STORY. ,</head>
        <p>i OUR SERIAL STORY. ,</p>
        <p>[ALL U ris RESER \ En.'</p>
        <p>manned otttalde on toe pavement no as to pre- vent a surprise. Lawson trod firmly, he could have walked the path blindfolded. Not a throb of life, or a distant hum of traffic could be heard, they might have been a hundred miles away in the depths of the country, so still am the place. Simard remained stationary in the muld!e of the courtyard and carefully 'cannot] the windows of the mansion. :Stay still. Do not move," he ordered.</p>
        <p>He was gone some ten anxious minutes, but then, to his companions' relief, he returned saying: "No lights in any of the windows, not a soul to ho seen. Apparently, we hero the place all to ourselves." Tin detective uncovered a small electric lamp which he carried under his coat, twd critically exambed the steps in front of the several doors. They were thickly encrusted with moss and dirt, not a foot had disturbed the even layers which time had deposited on the once polished surface of the marble. El• rased said to Lawson: "No footprints, no jailer. 7 hererore, no prisoner." Lawson smiled, shrugged his shoulders, saying in the &amp;teethe s ear: "It is not al- ways Lecessary to enter a house by the door." "The.window?"</p>
        <p>" How then?" ...</p>
        <p>.Through the wall."</p>
        <p>I u— . him , muttering his doubts, followed Lie s'm under an exquisitely - sculptured piazza. which partly surrounded the lower portion of the mansion. At the extreme end, he passed his hand along the wall till his fingers distinguished n tiny fissure. Asking Simard to flash his lamp on bin hand, the light disclosed a thin crack or line in the masonary. ".We enter here," said Michael.</p>
        <p>, Simard spread a handkerchief on the tease- lated pavement and knelt, closely scrutinising the mosaic work. Then he sprang up again, a satisfied smile on his lips; "A man and a womb use this entrance.' By suddenly c Lwron's mouth he stlge'cr :nhicsjaheaurladtiomnif triumph. "Monsieur is imprudent—a 'retie shout—and under the turf We may go. Who knows what awaits us in this house of dark deed", and mystery?" The bells from the neighbouring church steeples clanged the hour of midnight, and the four men mentally counted the strokes together. To Lawson's touch the stone masonary slowly revolved on a pivot, and Si. mard walked into the breach, cautioning his friends to tread softly. "We have to deal with those accomplished in artifice," he said. "Be as stealthy no cats." A sensation, not exactly of fear, seized both upon Blandford and Chinnery; they were wrapped in a darkness that no eye could penetrate, and Heaven alone could tell what was awaiting them in the gloom. "Von lend us, Monsieur Lawson," whis- pered Simard.</p>
        <p>"Link hands," said Lawson. In Indian file they were conducted through a circular hall, in the middle of which wan a marble fountain. This they saw by the light thrown from Simard's electric bulb. Opening a door to the left, Lawson drew them into a small chamber fitted with shelves, on which reposed many rows of embroidered slippers. Encasing hi. boots in a pair of them, he ad- vised his friends to do the some. "It leaves no impression and makes no noise," he said. "Now come in the same order, please." Like ghosts they threaded their way under the Moorish arches of carved wood, ascended the broad staircase, and crept along a gal- lery, and here Simard flashed his light, but there was not a sign or mark of occupation. nor could anything -he heard except the sound of their own softly-treading feet. The unnatu- ral abse.nce of ncise oppressed Chinnery and Blandford, who were, of course, only super- nunneries inn tho adventure. but Lawson, who had e‘crything to gain, was eager and impul- sive. while the -Frenchman regarded the nisht's work a a part of his professional ' duty. Nuctural and unauthorised entries into mansions were mere episodes not requiring , consid,rytion, lie r,nnained perfectly cool, ' and ready for any emergency that miyht con- front th, party, who now found themselves in !n rcom decorated in the early Arabesque style. _</p>
        <p>Simard, wishing to survey the arehitec- lure. asked Lawson to stop for a minute. " Mon Dieu! Cent maguifique !" he ex- ; claimed in a w hisper. He was filled with ad- miration at the noble proportions of the 1 apartment, but his delight did not came him to forget to look behind the crimson hang- ings. " This Immo is not deserted," fie said. ..See the palms are freshly watered. The mould is moist." Drawing from his pocket a hank of string, he selected three ivory-carved seatu. which he carried into the corridor and arranged an entanglement. Barely was this completed, when the men were startled by a distant cry. "Ps at,' and Blandford pressed his throb- bing hand on Chinnery's arm. As soon as Monsieur Simard heard the sound he covered the electrical bulb and the four men stood motionless, endeavouring to pierce the gloom which surrounded them on every side. The situation wits trying even to the strongest. nerves. The detective whispered: " What do you make of that?' • '</p>
        <p>"It was the voiee of a creature in anfinish of mind or pain of body, it was a despairing cry," said Chinnery, moistening his dry lips with the tip of his tongue. A distant beat- ing on wood vibrated from afar.</p>
        <p>" Someone beating against a door with the palms of the hands." "Bet where?" asked Blandford anxiously. "Mon dieu, I cannot say; the maison seems haunted, and we must lay the ghost." Lawson then spoke. I"You see, I am right. There is a prisoner here."</p>
        <p>--filen there came a creaking sound far from where they were standing, followed by a weird moaning, but though each strained his hearing to the utmost none of them could even express an opinion a. to whether the cry came from above, below, to the left, or to the right of them. " Listen !" whispered Lawson. They gripped hands preparing for an attack, but though they were enveloped in total darkness, they could distinctly hear a rustling and a shlitlling of alippered feet passing by them and onwards.</p>
        <p>Hearts stayed their beating, then thumped against their ribs, almost betraying their pre- sence. They were collected now the appre- hended danger wee near. Whence had COM,. the cry? Whither had gone the ghostly glid- ing feet no longer audible? The men listened in tense expectation of a repetition of their weird expenence. For a while not a man spoke, they were clustering closer together for protection. Earn ached acutely, straining to catch a repetition of the cries which, still lingered in their memorien. The cavernous depths caused by the electric rays wore more fearsome than the inky bleckness.</p>
        <p>A shattering moan rouried Simard from his reflections. "Come," said he in a tone of de- cision. "We will search the maison from roof to cellar."</p>
        <p>Room after room was investigated, but the search proved useless, they could find wither the Temple of the Golden Sun, or the room from whence had come the despairing soks,. At the foot of a flight of polished marble steps, an iron door, shrouded by a rich velvet curtain. barred the way. No keyhole, or handle was visible. Simard, Blandford. Chin. nery, and LIIWROII took it in turns to discover the secret spring, bet each one failed. A muffled thud and a distant shout sno-ded throogli the deserted corridors, thrilling the explorers with an intense desire to furthet prosecute their search. " Ah, my little traps has caught a whispered Simard. Throwing aside all thought of co' 'grshoent they rushed pell-mell to the spot, the chairs were upset, the string broken, but the ,corri- dor WWI silent and empty. Simard searched systematically, but nothing was discovered. Then the detective asked Lawson if th late owner of the maison had kept a monkey " Yes," said he—" Barbary Ape." "Just on the brink of a secret recall it self, and we are beaten," said Simard, HD. tying the string and replacing the chair pre. eisely as he had found them. "Let de- part,'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>A. MYSTERY OP MAYFAIR,</head>
        <p>A. MYSTERY OP MAYFAIR,</p>
        <p>JOHN STRANGE WINTER.</p>
        <p>MANTA XVII.</p>
        <p>TEI RIARCR FOR Tnic Ismrcs or TUE GOLDEN SUN AND A DISCOVERY.</p>
        <p>The dining - room of the Sappho was crowded. Each table had its full compli- ment of mcinbers, and all were flushed, eager, and excited. The reserve which usu- ally reigned in the club was given over to political discussion. Panty feeling ran high, for dissolution was imminent, and vari- ous partisans were busily advocating the claims of their particular candidates. Of course, the peppery, antediluvian politician twat there declaring emphatically that the country wan going to the dogs, but none lis- tened to this threadbare assertion, and so the statement died unopposed. The angry gentleman subsided into silence and retired !tom the room. At a table in front of the middle window sat Chinnery, Blandford, Simard, and Lawson. The two former were facing the light, the two latter men had their backs to the win- dow. Simard listened gravely to the opinions expressed on the policy of the recent government and the prospects in store for the new. Such a scene was a novelty to the Frenchman who was under the impression that Englishmen were cold blooded both es traders and politicians. The other three men were pre-occupied, their spirits per- turbed, and were only putting on the time till night should fall. Though lips were doted, eyes and ears were Open.. . . . . . .</p>
        <p>r6t.innery and his guests had had a fair amount of knocking about the world, but their scrapes had never no far led them into the hazardous adventure of housebreaking. Burglary! Yet none of them gave a thought to retreat. Indeed, they were obstinately re- solved to see the finish of the affair.</p>
        <p>Coffee and liqueurs were placed on the table, and then Chinnery ventured a re- mark. "Not before eleven-thirty, eh" "No, not till then," answered Simard, adding to Lawson: "You have a key to the aide wall%"</p>
        <p>Lawson responded by a nod. " Three hours exactly," said Blandford, looking at his watch. "To while away the intervening time, will you three come to the Colineum' And to this proposal the others agreed gladly enough. Chinnery and Blandford were consumed by ennui. Simard was stern and expectant. Lawson nervous, and full of conjecture. Con- versation hanging fire, 13Iandford and Chin- nery tried to amuse themselves by taking 'took of the pedestrians. A man attired in evening dress, his light overcoat thrown open, stopped before the Window to light his cigar. "There's • fine specimen of a man," said Blandford, caeually. "Handsome face of a bed type," sold Chinnerv, lazily, wishing that his companions would be a little more lively. " '</p>
        <p>" I should not care to 'have him for an enemy," remarked Bland ford, gazing in- tently at the man with a glance of recognition in his eyes. _"_lt can't be," he muttered.</p>
        <p>Simard and Lawson turned and looked into the street as the man on the pavement looked at them through the open window. The Frenchman's face underwent a rapid change, from an after-dinner geniality, it as- sumed the ferocious expression of a veritable demon. Then he smiled, a smile which trans. fixed the man who clung to the lamp post, his cigar rolling in the gutter. Lawson, leaning from the window, cried to those belfnd: "Dorinus, my murderous chauffeur."</p>
        <p>"Alias Signor Victor Paratti," ejaculated Simard. distinctly enough for them seated at the table to hear.</p>
        <p>Then I am right. Tied is the supprsed owner of the ear destroyed in Jertnyn- street," said Blandford.</p>
        <p>While this conversation was ring on, Leon Rodriguez recovered from his momentary panic, hailed s hansom, jumped into it, and was driven quickly in the direction. of Water- loo-place. Lawson rose from his chair with a wild Ides of going in pursuit, but Simard caught him by , the arm' ... . .</p>
        <p>"He' is gone, you'll not catch him. Pouf," and Simard blew out his cheeks. "Be con- tent, terror has struck him. Ile see me, he trembled, he was pale as ashes, 'ix eyes bulged. The hour of suspense 'are begun. Ile will dread the night, dread the waking morn. Alphonse is racing after the cab. Rafael Simeon, of the Secret Police, will make him feel like a hunted hare. The chase has be- gun, we'll have the boots yet, Monsieur Law- son, den the chart. Ah, mon ami, that vil- lain fears, he thinks of the coming blow, he throws up his hands and cries 'Mercy,' but he will find none here," he cried ; then ended solemnly laying his hand on his heart. "Mon- sieur Lawson, leave that man to me, for his punishment is mine." The last item of the entertainment was in progress, the auditorium was gradually thin- ning, and our four adventurers, taking their ease, went with the tide drifting from the Coliseum towards Charing-cross-road on St. Martin's Church clock struck the hour of eleven. Simard suggested a few oysters and a bottle of Chablis Moutonne. The proposal was eagerly accepted, and they adjourned to Scott's.</p>
        <p>---Stinird and Lawson chatted freely, but Chinnery and Blandford were very quiet. Supper brought to an end, Simard took the leadership, the othlrs submitting to his ex- perience. He forbade the use of cabs a 'bus to Victoria would enable him to detect whether they were being followed or not. Inside the grdes of the station Simard was keenly on the lookout, his eyes were everywhere, and he examined closely the faces of those hurrying by. To make assur- ance doubly sure lie sent his three compan- ions into the buffet, disappearing himself be- hind Smith's newspaper stall to watch travellers coming from the booking offices. Satisfied that there was nothing to excite sus- picion, he joined his friends at the counter and indulged in a brandy and soda. Ile ar- ranged the order in which they were to go. Lawson in front, lie a few yards in the rear. Chinnery and Blandford most walk on the opposite side of the road, but he cautioned them not to lose eight of their guide. To the chagrin of Lawson. when close to Trafford House he saw a laheurer loafing against the very door of which he had the key. Chinnery and Blandford had crossed the street, and were but a few feet behind Simard, who trod on Lswaon's heels, when he abruptly stopped in his calls. Simard was equal to the occa- sion, raised his but. apologising loudly. Lower he demanded sharply: "Why don't 'you go on?" "A spy," whispered Lawson. "Is that the wall of the house? Then you thee° separate, shake hands, we part. See? Walk round the square, then come back, one by one. See?" Bidding "Good-night" audibly, each went his separate way. Simard strolled by the man leaning against the door, he coughed three times.</p>
        <p>"Om. Wsieur." What have you to report?" "Nothing at all, M'sieur." "Bon! Where did the man in the cab To Paddington; he took a ticket for Marlow," Chinnery, Blendford, and Lawson came into view from three different points and at a signal given by the detective, jOil:ed him at • corner of the wall. Interpreting their looks, he said: "It is Alphonse &amp;wined. Admit us, no delay, the police may put in an inconvenient appearance." Lawson unlocked the door, holding it open for his companions to pass into the tarp, neglected courtyard where grass was growing Wale between the uSones. Alphonse re-</p>
        <p>. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Dangerous Dregs of Influenza.</head>
