        <p>•• too as we came. No. n 0,.. cried Lawson in tones of deepest dejection. " There will be other nights," said Simard. " To.night we are baffled ; next time We shalt be more fortunate. Have patience and wait. Como, Messieurs, daylight will he upon us; There were none except Lawson who did not rejoice at leaving the darkness behind and going back to the world again. When however, they had gained the main entrance, and were half-way across the court-yard, the detective raised his hand.</p>
        <p>" Listen !" said he imperatively, and as he spoke there came to their cars, • wail, ,tud- lble but faint, yet so terrible in its agony that the three were for renewing the search, but Simard over-ruled them.</p>
        <p>"Whence comes that cry, Lawson?" panted Blandford and Chinnery. " From the interior of that dome," an- swered Simard, pointing to the summit of the melee!). "There is the man we seek. We come again soon." The street was totally deserted when they quitted the courtyard. Lawson closed the door and Simard whistled softly. Where N. Alphonse, that he did not respond? Simard whistled again—then tho four men walked to- gether towards the corner of the square ; but before they reached it they came across a man lying huddled in a heap under tho shadow of the high wall. It was Alphonse-- stone dead. .be Continued).</p>
        <p>vain and that they spend cot their strength for nought. Above let, all those Rho profess religiol practise whit they preach. Them is still force As well as justice in Leigh Hunt's patodos that 'the paucity of Christians is astonishing considering the number of them !" But there ! I'm at it again ! " MACBETH."</p>
        <p>Bo our local amateurs have el or n this great Shakespearean tragedy for their commemorative and charitable effrt. 'Tis a wise as well as a bald choice, and artattredly much good will reatult every way. I wonder if any of my readers have heard Mr. James Ft theringham's series of Shakespeare lecturer', in which " Macbeth," comes last. In this, be told his audience when I heard hire, they had one of the central tragedy group, and cue of Shakespeare's greatest tragedies went with" Othello" as one of the most tragic of the great tragedies, and with • character of its own ameog those tenet' es. It had much of the intensity, speed, plosion, and terror that was found in " Othello." How full of meshing it wan, that between 1603 and 1616, in those three or four yearn, Shakespeare made, at the very height of hitt power, four of hisgreatere tragedies ; one after the other, in steady and rapid succession. The first record of the performance of the play nosy particularly in question was in the diary of Simon Forman, an astrologer. Ile told them that he raw the play at the Globe in 1610. That was pretty well all they knew. It could not peraibly he •• 'truly as 1603, nor could it bean late as 1610, for in verse, mood, colour, theme, and ethic it wae of that tragic group in which he had already pl Iced it. The s'oty was obtained, as was King Lear, from Holinshed's Chronicle of England anti Scotland, termed and re- published in 1377. Shakespeare drew spot the story of Delman and Macbeth, and he used also part of the story of King Duff'', an anaestor of [Juneau's. The slot), was considerably legend, and Shakespeare took even the legend for his purpose. It did not agree with hide"), but history wan nothing to Shakespeare. It was the Nomination of human passion, of human life, of human motives, and the posaibilities of trendy that Shakespeare saw in the two 'gorier', that drew hitt. lies' It cied, combined, added, re-touched, and fi ted to his purpose in order to get the marvellous figures '1 Macbeth and his wife in connection with one of his great tiagic thane.. Tragedy was ide I and was only teal in so far as it gave a true picture of the human soul under the straits and strain of life's hattle.They saw here, the motive of the dramatist He simply took from facts and ledend what suited his puree.. Ile shaped and developed them in ord, that he might get bin tragic theme The workings of a cert tin great paerion in a certain immure ; in a certain age and emit ontnent ; that was his purpose, and the material was only of use to him in relation to that puree-ter. Alluding to the ideas in the play Mr. Fortheringham obaerred that there was the witchery if evil That Shakeapeare meant, explain it as they would Their wine dratnat'at said the witchery and fascination of evil was a part el ' their experience. Did Shakeepeare believe in witches? Very likely he did. But tine evil that was in Macbeth was in Litn ',dote the wi•ches put it there. But that was not all it meant Fr Shakes- peare. There were evil powers and force, in life whisk might get theta into their grips arid lead them they knew not whither. Once nine in that play Shaker, pare wrote for them that lesson at least. That evil was transitory ; good wad firm and divine. There were times when God seemed to do nothing in certain crises or human tattle, bet Be did not forget the good order of the world. The tyrants' day would come and righteouttness and humanity would prevail. Why die they read these tragedies ? Some had mid they could not hear to read them That meant they could not bear to understind life as the masters had understood it. They could not bear to put their hearts into the heart of Shakespeare in order that they !night get what he learnt of life e wisdom Let them not take that point of view.lf there Ivens nly paitt,he would not ask them to read, but because pain was purged, purified, and ennobled, through such work, as these they were led on to the divine ground where they may all stand through the great heart of man. It is in all respect+ good, then, that Or. Law and his company ehould give "Macbeth " iu Warrington, with promise of other of Shakespeare's plays to follow in due courae. ERRATA.</p>
        <p>My anything but impeccable caliaraphy (in extenuation of which I recently put in a fairly reason- able plea) in conjunction with the fact that existing circumstances make it impossible fxr me to revise the proofs, goes far to explain the errors which sometimes creep into this column; but when, as in last week's contribution, compositor and proof reader conspire to represent me as writing "theoretical" sud "revere" where I had respectively net dbwn "heretical" and "reverse "—oven, to fall back upon a deltaic, I deny the allegations and I defy the alliga- tors. Yet, halfa-m,. A glance at the head of the page front which my grievance stands out so prominently reminds ins that these "fantastic tricks" at my expense were perpetuated on the fateful First. Ah, ab! yes, yes ! Now, of course, I see the joke ; and, as by Olin time the reader perceives it also, and no longer ha. reason to suspect that the anonymous writer of these notes is not what in the headline ie professed, we will shake hands all round, and resolve that no far as in us lies we will play no more pranks of the kind uotil next All Ftors day.</p>
        <p>EASTER HOLIDAY EXCURSIONS. " The great Central Railway Compaq through their agents Meninx Dean and Dawson Ltd., have sent as an attractive list of eacorsions which are being run from , Warrington Cent'sl for the forthcoming holidays. Commencing on Thursday April 13th excursions will he dispatched throughout the holidays to London, Nottingham, Leioester, Oxford, Stratfori-on.Avon, Rugby, Grantham, Linc Ola Yarmouth, Lowestoft, Cromer and the Eastern Crionties. The North East Coeat has not been overlooked, and thorn, who intend travelling to York,Scarboro', Middleshortr',Darl.ngton, Ac., may reach these placer; at cheap fare.. On Good Friday April 14th, and Easter Monday April 17th, excundoes will be run to Sheffield, Gainsboro, Brigg, Grintaby Dock. and Cleethorpes. There will be an , extsosion of Saturday to Monday tickets. Tickets and all information respecting these excursions may be obtairaid in advance at the Station, and from Mr. U. Isherwood, 37, Bridgrestreet, Warrington."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>19 YEARS' ECZEMA TORTURE.</head>
        <p>19 YEARS' ECZEMA TORTURE.</p>
        <p>Raw, Itching Disease that Defied Every Treatment But Zam Buk</p>
        <p>, Zmn-Bult's remarkable triumph over eczema of 19 years standing was the subject of a Itirtningbain reporter's conversation with Mr. 11. A. Rowlands, a !young paint mixer, living at 61, Alfred-street, Sparkbrook. " Nineteen years ago, when 1 was nine years old," said Mr. Rowlands, an attack of scarlet fever left me with a rash all round my mouth, nose and chin. Eruptions followed, makieg my skin smart and itch. The sores were always worst in Spring and Winter, when (ho cold, sharp winds made my akin raw and tender. It was agony to wan), nty fare. I often coeldn't Lew: to even touch it, and lwaa obliged to go k long time without shaving. It would be impossible to tell you of all the ointments and salves I tried, only to be disappointed agsin,tmd.again. . . _</p>
        <p>- "Seven yeare ago I underwent treatment at Birmingham skin hospital, where they told me I had running resema. Ihe treatment bad no lastingeffect, however. Just after the summer of 1009 my face was in a ahocking atate, my lips being graatly swollen and sore, and the intense irritation seemed more than I wild bear. .</p>
        <p>" Having 'maid a great deal about Zana-Buk's special power over ecv.eme, I decided to t.y this balm, though I confess somewhat half-heartedly. Zast43ith greatly surprised me, however, and I was so pleased with its soothing effect that I kept up the treatment. All the burning sensation began to go, and after Zam.Buk had thoroughly cleansed the some a raw skin formed over them. The swelling went : down, too. All the eczema was cleared array before ' the Winter of 1909, and though I cycled many miles every day during the worst weather I had not another breaking out. Alllast year, too, and thcmgh the past Winter rny skin kept m splendid health, and lam now convinced that Zam-Bak has permanently cured me.</p>
        <p>"I always carry a box of Zara-Buk in my pocket, now, and find it heals sores and wounds quickly and cleanly. "My wife was troubled with eczema, especially when she caught cold. Zam-Buk, however, cured her as well, and she declares she will never be without a box of this magic balm in the house."</p>
