      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>PALM SUNDAY.</p>
        <p>As all nty rei;ders are aware, the Sunday beforel Easter is commonly called Palm Sunday. It used to be, and here and there still is, customary on that day to decorate churchyards with wreaths of evergreens; but in most cases nowadays choice spring flowers are placed on gram; n Easter morn instead, aid that is a decided improvement upon the older practice. Last Sunday, some of our young folk, boys especially, were to be seen with slips of willow, the chief English substitute for the palm. However, in these modern times, the anniversary in cot observed as once it was even in this locality. Formerly, according to my reading of history, the festival was elaborately celebrated throughout the greater part of Europe. In the absence of palm, the branches of yew, willow, box, or other trees would be blessed by the priests after mass, and distributed among the people, who carried them in procession in memory of the Saviour's triumphant entry into Jerusalem. After the Reforma- tion in 1530 Henry VIII. declared Palm Sunday among those festivals not to be condemned, and the custom was kept up by the clergy until the reign of Edward VI. when it was left to the voluntary observ- ance of the people. GOOD FRIDAY.</p>
        <p>_ Our modes of observi,g this most solemn of all church festivals Ste wide as the poles asunder. A few, comparatively, devote the boon entirely to con- templation of the tremendous, epoch-making event for the commemoration of which it was originally pat upon the calendar ; multitudes hail it as the neat great holiday of tho year, and either rush at once to spend it amid like-minded crowds at popular pleasure escorts, or go fishing or gardening, or playing at or witnessing football, or taking part in pigeon-shooting niatchee, Ac. While not for a moment finding fault with toilers for reeking the nod of the respite and opportunity for relaxation afforded at this season, I em old-fashioned enough in view of thews extremes to prefer a via media—the hippy mean (as it seems to me) of certain serious-minded neighbours of mine, who by attendance at public service or in some other way give the opening hours to • orship, and then turn to such rational enjoyment of the holiday as shall not jar with their own earnest, deep convictions, nor rouse the susceptibilities of any other chi-s or individual. HOW IT FELL ON A TUESDAY. I have ventured to express an opinion, tut, lest I be suspected of preaching, I hasten to change the line if not the subject exactly. It is recorded that a young lady asked if any member of the company in which she happened to be remembered the year when Good Friday fell on Pancake Tuesday. " No," was the general tesponce. " Well, I do," said she. " I was not very old, but still I remember it distinctly." " I don't are how that can be," said another lady present; " I'm nearly 40 years of age, and I can't remember such a thing as that." " But it was the case," persisted the younger lady. " And I repeat that it is imp:saible," wee the rather excited answer, and the place seemed to be getting warm. " When I was a little phi," slid the lust speaker, "my father went in for dog racing a great deal and kept a dog called Good Friday. It was • good runner, and ho had entered it for a race winch was run on Shrove Tuesday • but Good Friday broke his Icg. So, you see, Good Friday fell on Pancake Tuesdiy.' CHURCH-GOING ON THE DECLINE. - - - ••</p>
        <p>I have read the report of the Free Church Council Conference at Warrington, and that and other refer- ences in the same and previous issues of the 'Examiner" compel agreement as to the fact that a spirit of restlessness and enquiry is every% here manifesting ihelf. The churches are evidently arriving at the conviction that empty pews,diminished income, lack of candidates for the ministry, and other signs of weakniss, can no longer be ignored. They are also •sare that Christisnity is developing and extending apart from the churches. Surely, after all, it were better that society should be saturated with Christiaa principles and that all should live and move and have their being in a tinily Christian atmosphere than that men should merely subscribe to a particular creed or be zeckoned among nominal adherents to some peculiar form of doctrine or discipline. What I feel as to these things has been admirably stated by Mr. Harold flegbie in a eeriee of papers written for the " Christian World," of the encouragement of those Christian workers in Warrington who are apparently inclined to pessimism because of the seeming indifference of many who remain outside the churches, I sill reproduce a few sentencee. Mr llegbie fearlessly asserts that a great torrent of Christianity is pouring through the whole earth and watering all the fielda of human action. Men are conscious, as never before in the history of the world, of moral responsibility. The attitude of the employer to the employed is unthink- ably different from the days before Christ and from the conditions which even now exist in lands where the Church has not yet carried the lamp of Hie illumination. All these modern schemes of profit- sharing, healthy cooditions of labour, shorter hoots, fairer wages and increased opportunities for rational happiness, have their rise in Christianity, and are the moot faithfully developed where the Christian spirit is strongtst. The _great fortames devoted by single individual. to public benefits are signu of the progreas of the Chrictian idea. The loving-kindoess which now marks our administration of the poor-law witnesses to a deeper religious C 011.6011100.8 than was known to the framers of that law. The existence of inaurance offices teatifies to a religious self-sacrifice on the part of parents for the sake of their children. Our anxiety concerning national education, even if we omit to consider the vast and extendmg work of our Sunday schools, is testimony to the Christ idea of moral responsibility. Recent reform, of our prison system witness almost more eloquently than anything else to the dominion and increasing dominion of the Christian spirit. After much more to the like effect—of course I can only give a bare summary—the writer roncludee—The old callousness to suffering, the old peseimism regarding the bad, the old indiffer- ence to the needs of others, the old eellishneas which made struggle for existence the law of life and the excuse for every form of brutality— these ideas, elid- ing still in the individual mind, are dead and power- less as regrode the national ccnecience. let despondent Warringtonians who have engaged in voluntary service in Sunday school or other form of church work be assured that their labour is not in</p>
