        <p>Mr. R. Fl. Ilklnell, M.1.C.H., an respeotor Of Ma Local Government Board, held an bigotry at the War. ringtoo Town TIM, on Tuesday, into the application of the Cerporattou for cane ion to the borrowing of £1,401 for the provision of bowling greens In Victoria Perk, St. Elphin's Perk, end the Liverpool-road trecwatio• gronnd. No poblio interest wart masifested In Ow pro- ceedings, not or single ratepayer, .part from members of the Council, heiog prement. The following members end officials of the Corporrei or were in attendance -- Alderman Bolton (chairmen of the Town 11,11, Perks and Bathe Committee), Cermetller Pemberton, Messrs T Longlin (borough romeynr), A M Ker (wetweet surveyor), J Fairhurst (borough treasurer), J floughtort (Ate( clerk in the brrough treworeee depertment), A T Holloway (depoty town clerk), and T ii Spinks (assistant town clerk). The Deputy Town Clerk expielnei that at thepresent time there wagons municipal b cling green—ins rands Pork—and it wee being used to such an extent that a wood green in tin park bed lecome absolutely cecencnrY, the object being to work the two in conjuoo- tion nod occasionally give the imbuing green • rear. Astor as Len° ehire and Warrington were concereed bowling was regarded as n national inetitution. There were eighteen bowling greens in Werringmn, but only one was a public green, the others being consorted with licenead houses and private oinks -end either of totr.,y. pptple did. green eitrovktto.orir p.to,k was constructed in 1904, pertly out of the reies and pertly with n lon The cost to the Wee in the first throb (ma ens £283 In and with the loan the total ..tl.ll in that period war 2439 13s 31. In rho movvyn 1903-6 the receipt, wee' '4 41 Id and the coot of maintenance was Ell _ , together with £IS loan chergas. There wee, therefore, a profit of EU 9. Id. In the (Atoning yearn caretnker wee appointed sod the cost of meh tenant-el ems increased to ES3 IS. 3d, the income being £5l 10g. After payment, of loan charge% there sax a net, low toll. retro of ill 9e Id. In 1901-11 the lose to the wets was CA ns 4d and in 194)89 £2B 154 It. During the past warren the income was £3314. and the net lora to the retest was Eta It, lid. The, Inspector asked if it would not be ponible to increase by a smell shade the chirp mode for the on of the amen, no as tr get rid of the lon to the rater. Mr. Ilellsway t It worr'd Ic p ruble hit the Cm- poratio.n do not wish to do it.</p>
        <p>The Inspector : Is it not a fact th tt whataver loge there is, falls on the goner 'ate? Mr. Hallawey : There in no doubt ab rut th it. The Inspect or And people who do not get any bentfit or desire any benefit from Vile green, been to pay heoanse the poop's who play do not pay enough money to kw p the peened in order. Mr. flalleway : Toot is no. Of course It Is Impos- sible for me to disarms the polio, of the Corporation. In roomer to the Inspector, Mr. Hellaway said the charges for use of the green were 21 Intone hour (with bon Is) and Id for each stthesinent hour and ZI for two hoors when the player brought his own bowls. The Inspector thought It was likely that when three additional greens were prod i lei the imyone of the existing groan would decrema cal the ova t the 'v., IwYer+, with loan., &amp;o , would be heavier. Would it not be possible to twe the effeot of tvo new greens no the one•in Victoria Perk, before providing another there?</p>
        <p>Alderman Bolton pointcd not that • footbridge was to be constructed across tlw Mersey from Howley and he thought that would teed to increase the use of the greens in Victoria Park by bringing the two districts into elm., touch.</p>
        <p>--doWn-clllo7ile—rOberton. a, • representstive of Latch• ford Ward, pointed out that the demand upon the existing green was so great that many people welted in vain for the opportunity of playing s gams. The popu- lation of people coming from other vele of the town to the Latchford green WM inlloitesimtl and he did not think the provision of new green, in other neighbour- hood, would 101 l moue the takings at the Viotori• Park by one penny. r_e damned for the greens wse unanimous in the Council and among the ratepayers</p>
        <p>Mr. Bioknell.—Do sou think the greens should be eelfenpport ins or should the retepayers hear the loss? Conuoillor Pemberton. —The working of is... get very little hick for the rates they ply, sad it is • good thing to encoursge them by providingsuch reoreatioes. Mr. Bicknell—Do you consider it is quite just end proper for people who don't play bowls end who are the vest mejority to pay the entre coet for Lb. Wasik of those who do ploy? Councillor Peml.rton I do. The same this I appliee to baths ; all people do not swim but all contri. bete to the cost. It would be better if the greens were seiteupporting, but the lose to infinitesimal. I do not think there would be any difficulty in making op the deficit. The bowlers would willingly pay it. The inquiry then closed.</p>
