        <p>WIN %V ICK FREN Cu l'R(ll.—ThO sixth anniversary of this church was celebrated on Sunday when Pastor W. Arnold, of Golborne, wench.' at the morning and evening service,. Special hymns were sung and the choir gave an excellent rendering of the anthem Tabo , a Foam." Mr. O. Floyd rang the aolo " The song and I the sinner," and the retrain" Jesus, Lover of my soul," was taken I y six of the Sunday school scholars. On Monday evening a public tea was followed by a meeting at seven, prenided over by the Rev. 11. Whll. The teachers and scholars gave a programme of songs and recitations, Ste., after whirl, Paator W. Arnold die- tributed the prizes to the Sunday school scholars. A pleasing (Intone was the presentation in recognition of his services to the church of a beautiful timepiece suitably inccribed to Mr. George Wainwright, who is leaving the village. Orford.</p>
        <p>CONFIRMATIoN.-11. Lord Bishop of Liverpool held the flint confirmation service in Orford Church on &amp;Birder, for the parishes of Orford, Parigste, end Flollinfare. Seven ?our persons were coLlirmed, 211 from Padgate, 13 from Hollinfare, and 35 from Orford. In the course of his sidewise, the Bishop Rave come sound advice to the conflrmers, and ached them not to come to 11017 Communion once or twice, and then fell away, hut to come regular and as often Bs rossible. Lymm.</p>
        <p>- -- LANTERN SERVICE IN THY. PRIMITIVE METHODIST SCHOOL—nit service am again well attended on Sunday evening. The story which were illustrated on the screen wes entitled "Due Deivreon'e Harvest," and was appreciated hr all. A cola wee creditably sung by Mr. Holt. Miss I'. Elliott presided. A collection was taken at the close of the meeting. FOOTRALLERS' tortes SOT ENPERIENCIL—Lymm Amateurs who visited limbo's° will have cause to remember the tat of April, for on returning to the dresaing.room after thr ir match with the above club they fctind that the pockets of their trousers had bran emptied and money to the amount cf over 1.4 taken. Our of the players having .C 5 in gold jou before leaving the dressing.room for the match put the money in he beg and thus saved it from the pilferers.</p>
        <p>CoNGRI.GATIONAI, CIIIII,TIAN Itesn.—A good number of young ponole were present st this meeting on Monday. Miss Fenny Sutton presided, rllli Mass Mary A. Tailor rendered s solo " Jesus hop me near the cross" very effectively. A stirring address ass given hr Mr. John Perrin on The Church end amusements." The meeting concluded with short prayers by various members.</p>
        <p>Coxunno STION.cJ, 5,13.4 Y 5€ 1100 t..—On Thursday evening several of the young people/connected with thin school pre a very good entertainment in the schoolroom. A good audience eras present and thoroughly enjoyed the programme which consisted of songs by J Rose, /Hem Turner, James Hewitt, Eliza Moores, Douglaa I lalt, Harry Rushton, Mr Moser, and Annie Erse, and a duet by Mieses Eliza Moores and Annie linen. Two sketches were given during the evening entitled "Slightly mined "and "Henpecked " were played very effectively, and thoroughly amused rill. The proceeds see for the decoration cf a room of the school which will be used for social purpoaes.</p>
        <p>FOorli 11.1. —1.1,1 M ATIII.F.TIC V. MOIIIII,IILEV. Theme clubs met on Saturday on Latchford gronntl to decide be Association Football semidlnal. Lymm from the start 1..1 far more of the game, the goal being completely bombarded until hall-time came. Lymna could do nn 3 thing but score, the Alobbarley goalkeeper playing r, capital gain.. After the turn over Lymut seemed to go completely ort, Wileon letting Mobberley in oh min-kicks and Mobberley taking advantage of the weakneas of the Lymna backs, gained a victory of 3 gnals to nil. The Lantm sup- port°. went away quite disappointed with the display of Lymm, who are the head of the Altrincham Leagne.</p>
