        <p>Tin: (~RONATION.—The parishioners of Higher and Lower Whitley met in the Church Inhtitote on Thurs- day evening of last week, to make arrangements for a local celebration at the King's Coronation. It was decided that the festival should be held on Thursday, June 2nd, in Grimsditch Hall Park, and eight collectors were appointed to obtain .'he funds necessary to provide for the sports, music, &amp;o. The following gentlemen constitute the committee :—Chairman, Mr. C. E. Romney ; Messrs. Acton, Antwis, Barber, Bred. ford, Cartwright. Clarke, Dees, Fairhurst, Fletcher, Frodsham, (hough, Hayward, Ilowsrd, Kay, Leach, Tfounfleld, Moore, Renshaw, Taylor, Walrnsley, Wilkin- son, West 6, oh, and Wright ; hon. treasurer. Mr. F. G. Fairhurst , non. secretary. Re, J. C. Trbmpleaeme. Moore.</p>
        <p>WESLEy GUILD.—The devotional meeting of the Wesley Guild was held on Wednesday evening last in the schoolroom. Mr.Meldrurn presided (in the absence of Mr. In over sorry Rood attendance. rhe Her. J. Gunnell, of Penketh, panes most interesting sad educative lecture on: The Stos yof the English Bible. In the short epee° of an hour he related in a wonderfully concise and interesting form the whole story, Gum the manuscript copy front the pen of Wycliffe down to the present authorised versh a! published in Fill. The cost of the that M.S. copies translated by Wycliffe ass £O, as money in now. Ito told of the perils (to the producers) under which it was first produced end how on several oco.siens tie circalation was almost extinguiehed by flee. Several of the early translators had to pity for it with the, lives. At the oboe of the address Mice Dutton very tactfully moved the lust thanks of the meeting to Me. Gunnell, this wet seconded by Mr. J. Jackson.</p>
