        <p>The Gotland Committee Appointed at the recent parish meeting to make arrangements for the celebra- tion of the coming Coronet ion held a meet inn on Mon- day evening in the Hall Cafe. Mr. 11. J. Westbrook (chairmen of the Psrish Council) presided over a good attendance and was itipported by Mr. Fred Pearson Ovum treasurer), and Mr. Elwin Bradshaw (clerk of the Conceit), as hon. reeretery. It was decided that the fest ivities shou'd commence on Thursday, June 22.1 d, and include a short public meeting in the Square and the peramtailation rf the principal rondo of the perish in procession ; followed by tea for the children whom, age. rat go from three to 15, with sports afterwards. The need folk, are to be entertained on the evening of the following day— Friday, the '..3rd. Several special suh-committees were appointed, viz., entering, sports, enter tainn.ent, decora- tions, finence and executive. The Eccleiiristk al Parish AVIS lined as Chevron of operations plus the remaining portion of Lower Walton. The area was mapped out into district., endeollector, were appointed for eneh diet, mt. The proceedings will commence r n the first day at 1.15 pm. end on the second day at 5 p.m. It were sloe sugnetited that Ole Chairman should invite the member. qi the Corrention Comn.ittee and the pane bodies rod fir-prarith and the inhabitants generally to join a prneres4W If, Divine service at the Parish Church on the Thesdsy, areinning that sod, service is held in the mornieg et such a time as will not interfere with the other •rr1.11....t.• GARDEN SOCIETY'S BULB SHOW.</p>
        <p>The Stockton Death Garden Society held it sixth annual bulb show in the Church lentituton Sattirday afternoon and although the entries were son ewhat disappointing, some story fine bloome were e.g.. A re-awaktning of interest in this side of the society's work ito, however, crratly to I e desired. fir Gilbert Cireenatl, Boort , the pre ilent, sent I special exhihit of beautiful flowers from the Walton Dell gardens which was much admired. During the oho moon the show was visited by Lady Brno., Mr. Roger C. Parr, nod the Rev. Jam. and Mi.. Collins. So leo ions were played et intervals by the aociety'a orchestra. Mr. A. Fawcett capably discharged the duties of hon. secretor, .......... _</p>
        <p>PRIZE II 02. Six hyacinths in pots I, E Pollard ;2, It Mocbrie; 3,J Colley. Tbr• e hyacinths in pots 1, J Colley ;2, E Pollard ; 3, t/ Williams. One hyseenth in a pot : 1, It Mochrie ; 2, J Gleam, ; 3, Mrs Cleave. Three hyscintbs is glsres : 1, E Pollard. One hyacinth in •glass I, It Mortals ; 2,J Gleave : 3, Mrs Glenve. Three pots of doublo tulips ; 1, It Mochrie ; 2 J Colley. Three its of single tulips : I, E Pollsrrl ;2, J Colley. One pot of double tulips: IF Langley ;2, E Vernon; 2.0 Williams. Ono pot of single tulips: 1, R Mochrie; 2, Langley 3, J Colley. Three Pots of single nnrcibvi : 1, II Mochrie ; 2, E Pollard. Otte pot of single nsrcissi : 1, F Langley ; 2, Mrs Hargreaves ; 3,0 William.. One pot of polyanthus nnrcissi : I, Williams ; 2. C Wilcockson ; 3, J Oleave. Double daffodils : 1, J Colley ; 2, E Vernon. Miscellaneous collection of both,: I. Mrs (Heave ; 2. J Gleams ; 3,0 Williams. Best armor :1 rase of Iprins flowers and foliage : I. Mrs Hargreaves ; 2, II Parton ; 3, E Vernon. One hyncinth in spot : I. Berths Langley ; 2,T Colley ; 3, Jessie Falahsw ; I, Jack Glenne. One hyacinth in a glass : I, Bertha Lergley ; 2, Doris Pollard ; 3, Jack tumor ; 1, Jessie Falshaw. Ono pot of narcissi 1, Bertha Langley • 2, Jessie Falthaw. Collection of rpriug flowers, 2, E'Vernon.</p>
        <p>Winwlck. FARMER'S SUCCESSFUL ACIION. JUSTICE GRANTHAM CRITICISES THE ASYLUM AUTHORITIES.</p>
        <p>In the Bing'. Bench Di visirn on Monday Mr. Justice Grantham gave judgment in the action of Longton v. the Committee of Vikitors for Winwick Asylum, which was tried before him et the recent Liverpool Assize.. Mr. Rigby Swift, M. P., 'Torqued for the plaintiff, and Mr. Sotto. for the defendants. _</p>
        <p>Ills lordship mid this saes an emir n by Mr. l'eter Longton. a farmer living at %Nine ink, where he and his father Ind been tenant s of a farm for many years against defendants as managers of ft vast lunatic asylum at Winwick, Lancashire. Plaintiff claimed dart ages for injury which, he alleged, had been cavael to his crops by cannon of the defendant ft having Idled up an old stream called klill Brook, and negligently and improperly substituting therefor a drainpipe ehich was faulty in design and construction, and incapable of receiving the water from plaintifra writer c iurses, the result bring tt at his laorl was flooded. lie alleged wrong by defendants, wiling that the drainpipes were improperly laid, so that they were higher in one rvirt— as much as one foot In places—than the old Mill Brook, and the con.quence was that they would not take the water that had been acelistomed to flow from plsintilia drains into the old Mill Brook, and ea was got rid of from his lend. Defendants denied generally any wrong to the plaintiff, and denied Isis right to make any claim for compensation, even supponing injury had bran dove. They also said if any injury was dune they sacra not responsible. It wean cacr of considerable impottanee and diffl- catty, SU I the facts end inferences to be drawn from the facts were so much in dispute that he thought it desirable to vis:t the locus in quo in ordn, the better to 110 justice betwten the parties. Ile had a view on Monday, and althongh he was on the spot from eleven to tier. he did not complete all that he seated to do. Ile did not go to the save., of the Bin, pipe, to ate where that came from tted to had curt the various faults alleged. lie also hoped to have gone lathe end nf the otitle' :f the klill Brook, but mould not do an, as his was obiljed In Inane by the Rix o'cleJk learn. Lla•ing recapitulated the facts, his Lordahip Enid he must hold that the defendants had acted Meg illy in Interfering with lil3 watercottee without !Irak com- municating with the local authorities, having tilled up a public watercourse without any effort to gat in fcrda. Don as to what drains they might be interfering with. They, 'boracite, roust not complain if doubtful cases were decided agiinat them when damage had been proved to be &amp;we, lie, thetofote, hold that plaintiff was entitled to reggae, not to the full extent claimed, but to the extent of .Clti end costa. The defendants raised a point of law oaths conveyance of the land, which he held they could not maintain. lie did not award plaintiff anything for general damages, but gave him judgmi at for £lOll and c.a. lis led not the xlighteat ohjertion to the defendants exerciaing their ight to appeal on the usual terme hut, at the relationship of the parties to wish other, the pleintiff. a poor men, and the defendants, having the wealth of the ratepayers at their bock, he hoped some more amicable way of settling their differeacrs might be found than in apend ion the ratepayers' money tO ruin somall farmer by relying on legal techni.lities to prevent his tieing his farm a ith the .nle advantage he enjoyed before the defendants lieught his land, and for the deprivation of which they paid him nothing.</p>
