        <p>IMPORTANT PROPOSALS BY LANCASHIRE COUNTY COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A propoeal of an important character to lehth Lanca- shire and Cheshite came before the members of the Bucklow Rural C dincil end Board of Guardians on Wednesday on a letter read from Nlr. Potts, clerk to the Cheshire County Council. The letter stated that a rat.. I had been made to l'hethire by the Lancashire County Council that a portion of the parish of Lyrom, north of the Manchester .i4ltip Canal, and the shots of the parte of Warburton, Partington and Carrington re,pectively. aituated on the north side of the canal, should be transferred to Lancnshirw In the event of their objecting to the proposal, Mr. Potts inquired whether they would be prepared to waive such oh jecrion if the Lancashire County Council were willing to pay corapenention to Cheshire in respect of the prospective increase in ratable value of the part of I hoshire aitasted north of the canal and proposed to be transferred to Lencalhire. At the Rural Council meeting, Mr. Price said there was great Industrial developnaerit in the parishes named, anrdit hehoved.them to be careful.</p>
        <p>'l'be Clerk (Mr. lisigh) mid a aimilar propostl watt before them some years ago, and linclilowr was dead against IL 'I here was a big indostrisl development, pnd it ars a mstter which ongbt to e discussed in all iln details in open Council. . .</p>
        <p>The same vie;-.a %Ili expressed by the guardians, and the communication liar referred tja special joint committee. - -</p>
        <p>Penketh. SCDDIN DEATII.—it is with mgret we report the death of Mr. Janos Denn, Warrington•roail Penketh, who had acted as coachmen for Or, Murray, for about It year., end no Inter than Saturday %SS in Werrington with the doctor's carriage. Deceased was taken ill on arriving home and pasted awry on Sunday afternoon. The dammed leaven a wife end two little girle. the funeral took place on Wedneeilly afternocn nt the Sankey Cemetery at three o'clock, the Rev. J. H. Jones, M.A. officiating. Dr. Murray's carriage followed behind the hearee nith the widow, the two little girls and Or. Niue, . TI ere were also a number of members of the l'enketh. United Order of Druids, with which he deceased had long been associated, including Bro. 1). Houghton, past grand master of the Order. A great number of wrmithe were received from relatives and friends and one from the members of the Lodge. PARISH 'MEETING. . . _</p>
        <p>A parish meeting ens held in the Council school, Strick•lane, on Wednesday evening, when a good number of the parishioners were in attendance. The following members of the Count it were also present r—Dr. Murray (chairman), Messrs. J. 6. Withinshaw, J•rtee Woodwerd, James Ditchfield, R. ltoutledge, W. Davie., and E. Foster )clerk).—A discussion took place as to the age limit for the school children, it being contended by several present that the school children ought to be admitted under 15 —On the proposition of Mr. 11. Maintey, it was rarried;that the Parish lorimicil approach the Education Committee with a •iew of opening the question of the school age.—The question of 'snips was also discussed, end Mr. E. Marshall moved that three more lamps he pot up, one at Hall Nook, Band 0 irdens, and 003 by Mr. WhiTisid's farm.-Ihrs was carried.</p>
        <p>CO.I.IONAIIOIXELEIIIIA.TIONS.</p>
        <p>After a long discussion Mr. klainley rose and moved " That we do publicly celebrate the Coronation of the King." This was carried unanimously. In what way this (Mould li. done was then dis- cussed. It,. Murgav explained that last time there were sports and a baby show. (Laughter.) Ile. Murray : er, , a habg show, also a bon.llre. It was decieed, on the motion of Ms. J. .1. Foden, that the pariah council form a part of the cuurnittee to take the arrangements in hand. Mr. Itoutledge then moved that the friendly societies in the village be requested to send two or three representatives, as were also chairman of other public bodies and superinten• dent. or the Sond ty pchools of the church and chapels. This was agreed to a ith power given to the committee to add to their number.. Mr. Cloy then moved that the expense should be met by public subacription. the resolution CM carried. 'I he questions chic', hid had been received were then reed nix. Mr. A. Gouldin re pehlM footpaths. Mr. Marshall naked what power, the Town's Planning Act had ci nferred on the parish Council (dui when were the hurdles ; that had been ordered, going to be erected round the recreatirds ground. Mr. Faulkner brought forward the qoestion of allotments. Mr. Clay moved that a urinal be erscted on he recreation ground also that a public telephone be appliel for at Penketh ; this question was also larked by Mr. P. C. Garnett.</p>
