      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>REVOLUTIONARY BUDGET CARRIED.</p>
        <p>The last meeting of the session of the Warrington Parliamentary Debar ins Society was held on Tuesday evening In the Cairo-street Church parlour. There was a fair number of members present. The subject under diFcussion was Mr. F. G. Taylor's Budget, which arm introduced last week, the main pro•isiona of which were the abolition of food and land taxes and the institution of a rcherne of graduated income tan on all person. except minors. The PRESIDENT OF THE HOAR, Or AOlth Miroit (Mr. C. Plimton) opened the debate and ea d be saw no inconsistency in the (jovernment's attitude towards Tariff Reform and the Budget last week, because, Although they proposed one tax which they telieved was equitahle,still if goods produced by sweated labour in other countries sere sent over here and competed with honest Britiah labour, they would not hesitate to Ming in a measure to protect the latter. 'they of the Conservative l'arty considered the working classes had been paying Indirectly far and above their fair share of he taxation of the country, and they had not hesitated to my that. (Hear, hear.) If the Budget propotals I sw, re examined it would he aren the am in it piid upon weges would be ccnsiderahly fairer to the workman tlAn at the present time. Hear, hear.) A more equitable proposal had never boon brought before the Home. Continuinr,Mr. Plinston said that the mention of lend seemed to be to the member of Limehonse ' tqrl • red rag to atoll and he seemed to have the en that everybody who owned land moat bares crook in his character. )) _</p>
        <p>Mr. NEW (L Lintehouse) : I repudiate that, I never said it.</p>
        <p>Mr.-l'hrrisvoyr. : I know you never said such a thing, but lam perfectly jostilled in drawing my own conclu- sions as to the remarks that fall from members on the other Aram (hear, hear and laughter). In conclusion he said that under the Budget the landowner would have to pee in the same proportion as everybody else, which he considered wen equitable. (Opposition cheer.). Mr. MARK Mow Al,ll I, Falmouth) raid the position of the Government was that while retaining Tariff Reform they were advocating a Free Trade Bridget. (Laughter.) Judging from the Chancellor's frequent quotations from one of Mr. Chi... Money's booklet. during his Budget speech, he (the speaker) thought he most have received a great amount of Inspiration from it. The CHANCRLI.OI, or Tut Esa IttitUt:lt (Nfr. F. G. Taylorh—The book I quoted from I did not see until the day before the pre•robs sitting. Mr. How alarm replied that he made good use of it nt any rate. Contlnuieg, he said that he thought the Government were wrong in making all the sweeping changes proposed in one year. It would have been better to have proceeded gradually thin year elirninat• irig some of the present tertian and bringing insole. of the new hue.. (Ministerial cheers). Ile was sorry to are the land taxes were to Ire swept away by the Budget The Government did not meal to grasp the fact that the had later were not put on merely for revenue pur- polies only. There Was an ulterior motive and he never heard anyone adequately defend the proposition that land which has increased in value by the progress of the community shoold not pay something for it. (Ilear, hear). On the whole he supported the Budget,althongh be did not regard it as a perfect measure, iloan, hem).</p>
        <p>PIERnn WILLI knfn (Sec., Anglesey) mid that though he was not unconscious of the good things involved in the Budget there were many things open to criticism. At the previous slitting the Opposition presented a strange spectacle. Not only did the Opposition forget that it was the duty of an Opposition to oppose, lot they eeernod to be eufferiug from , political aberration. (I.sughter The hon. member for Burton seemed to he fnebriated—(lattahter)—by the adoption of the Tore Chancellor of the Exchever of what he deemed was hi. one pullout. policy. Ile thought the hon, member ran away from his position, and no did other members of the Opposition who spoke at that sitting. IlAughter.) lie could only attribute that to confusion of thought. The two great et lie of the Budget were the exemption of all minors and the taxation of ell wages irrespective of any minimum whatsoever. In his view Close fnolln were eullicient to condemn the whole, and ho was prepared to vote against tee a whole unless the Chancellor 0: the Excheyter dropped those propoaitions. They were told without reservation on the part of the Chancellor of the Exchequer the, weges of even ss. n week would bee sub. ject to a ten, hut he contended Owl in wages of 2ns. or even 400. a week there was no taxable margin. Beside., the man elm worked for 204. e week non,. nine who was robbed, and to take something essay from [lot war truillly es bid. '1 he mere propotel to tax the labourer'. wage showed a lack of knowledge of the workingman, Wear, hear).</p>
        <p>The PRIME MINI,I,II(Mr. W. if. Stonsfleid" supported the Budget because it wade all taxation direct. Ile elan thetught that the Budget era, a step in the direc- tion of Tariff Reform end a very large atop, herniae in the past it had hire found that when the revenue of this country wits a hit short recoorse had been mode to increase tale. on ten, coffee, tabecco, tea and beer.: Such an action would be impossilile in the future. Then the workingmen would taken greater interest in the country, because they would be called upon to pay direetly and they would realise that it hen they hoppened to be short of ravenon that would probably advance the programme of 'Florid Reform. The Budget stimulated patriotism, inasnoich as it' allowed a men who served hie count ry in the Army or Navy to eacape Legation on his wages drawn es a ofildiiir or sell.e. Further, it encouraged men to j in the Territorial Forces through the remission of 50 per cent, of their taxes while members of that body. The Budget leesened the borders of the poor sod placed there on the rich. Thu Government were in sympathy with the poor man, and they had proved it by putting into practice a principle to help hint. The hon. member for Anglesey had mu there RCS no Margin to tan in the working man's income. At the prefent time the worker paid a Reenter proportion of teXation then he would pry tinder the Budget. The bon. member was inconsistent in that respect. (Gear, hesr.) Mr. Pit:Rile : Nly point in that I arc against ell taxation direct end indirect on the worker. 'the Prtisig Illixfodcdt t this is a principle which you are quite justified in taking up, but if Oman does not pay any taxes he shall not have the not,. (Govern- ment cheered_</p>
        <p>Captain 11-.0. Mammy. (L., afontgemory), stid that now theme° with an income of £l,OOO paid in direct anl indirect taxation £53 per annum and on the basis of ! the Budget proposals be would hare to pay over E 07,1 which was en increase of about tin per cent. Ile mnin• tamed that that amount was outofproportion to what ha should pay. Regarding the proposal to exempt front tans., !lien those in the Army end Navy he acid that 50 per ' cent of the officers in the Army were not there because of patriotism but for social purposes and they were ' therefore, not entitled to receive the coneideration they I did under the Budget. The Territorial xfficers joined ! that force out pure patriotism end they were justly entitled to a. much considelation att those in the Belittler Army. (Opposition cheers.) ! Mr. W. limper,' (N. Dublin Harbour) said that had that Budget been brought into the Imperial House of Commons by the Conservative party some years ago they wonld not have been in the terrible poeit ion they were at the prenent time. in regard to the Home of Lords, becnuse the latter would have palmed it. If the Liberal Government had ht., ught it in the cry of the Conservatives would have been •• Socialism." (Lnughter.) Mr. llsavillOßN (C., Butt !Wrest . said that the Budget would do mote foe" Illateliforcrt diatom Dog' than any other Budget had ever done. (Laughter and applause. 1,Y.146rt of. THY 01.1.0sITION (Mr. F.. Wynne Jones) disagreed will, the Chancellor of the Exchequer in exempting land and he might say that when the Liberals were again in power he would try nod persuade hillside to give the lend to those to whom it belonged, (Ministerixl cries of' Socialism.") I said I would try. he replied, amidst laughter. That Iluldet was one of ! the most revolutionary propos de with regard to the question of revenue raising that had ever been intro- ' diced and further it MINS revolutioniry proposal which erns rot made by gentlemen opposite because they believed in it, but it was a complete surrenier to public opinion. (Opposition applause.) He would have likod to have been at the, Cabinet meeting when the proposal, were frarni 1. (Laughter.) Ile cosld finite trnag ne the Piime Minister saying " Let's dish Chem and in'tivduce a Radi.il Illoiget,"—(lisoghter)— he could quite imagine the Colonial Sreretsry Alderman lienaett) baying "another dream of mine P.lle maalt.--(losd laultter) In could imagine there all walking hp end down the room singing "Oh where ia T. R. to-fight," (Laughter. Ilowescr, he believed that if there Wes a direct taxatiou the people would take a greater interest in p iblic affairs and control MO/0 fully both local and national Affairs. (Applause.) lie regretted that more progressive taxa- tion bad not lees introduced in the higher income., for it *es only fair that the larger income, should bear a larg. r percentage than was proposed in that liudget. (Opposition cheers). ' The CHAN, ELLOII OF ills Exi Heart:a said he failed Ire see where the bon. member for Anglieey was sr or sistent. Ile flrat said the workers were too poor to be taxed, yet he had stated be woold not vote for the Budget, which would considerably lessen the burden of taxation already borne by the pour. Tae hon, member would he the first to claim that the people should rule, yet by exempt ills them from taketion they would be tatting the power to rule frorn them for those who did at per taxes would not be allowed to vote. That *as the reason minors were not to be taxed—because they were not to have the Vut• until they attained their majority. The Government had not been converted to Free Trade. It was not so. They c add introduce • Tariff Reform Bodget after the present Budget rise-sally aa they ciiil.l have di liP before th a MeaStITP was intro. ducat,. (Applause'. If in the Budget they had dna not)rirg else they hail at least exposed the fallacy of the Idea that our prtsent fiscal system air is Free Trade. (Apple., The Budget was carried without. d)vision, there being very few who opposed it.</p>
