      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Rectory, Warritigton. April sth, 11.</head>
        <p>The Rectory, Warritigton. April sth, 11.</p>
        <p>Sir,—May I again call upon your courtesy and space in reply to " Sautella's " letter in your issue of Saturday last? At the commencement, my friend would like to know the cenoection between persecutions and Catho- licity. Catholicity, I take it, means liberality of view (or spirit), and, on I attempted to demonstrate, I fail to see when or where the Roman Church has shown nra she over possessed this adjunct. Instead, however, he turns to Dr. Smyth (a Protestant) for an exaroplwl While agreeing with my friend when he shows on we have nothing to boast about in our past history, I most say the vilest deeds ever perpetrated under the Protestant banner pale into insignificance when c er- pared with three if the "only Universal Church." Who lies not beard of the noble order of burnt Dominic Inc Black Friars) whote policy of persecuting Clue Albigenses W. very similar to that ad, ptcd by the later but more noted Inquisition? His statement "The maj city of the negroes of the Southern United States are Catholics" requires the proverbial I.inch of salt. How he arrives at this remarkable assertion, I fail to see, as I have always been lid to know that an oterwhelming majority of the negroes are adherents to the Protestant cause—a like mojstily, in fact, to that found in the whole country, among any colour. Hence the enlightenment and freedom of the nation. I entirely agree with my friend when lie states that the Rumen Church is still alive and active. Recently all eyes have been tarried upon her, so much attention has she drawn in her " activi,y." At last year's Garibaldi celebrations, the Vatican was thrown in a went, state of activity and procouuced itself alive by hurling condemnation at the head of the unfortunate Mayor of Rome for his patriotic speech or rather " infringement of the Sovereign. Rights of the Holy See." Needless to say, the Maver had the nation behind him, for the Pope ia not ' laved in Italy if elsewhere, and is regarded by ail lovers of their King and nountry as a stumbling- block to progress. 'I ben ore turn to Portugal, where tire" worm turned." Here, quite ricently, a greater activity existed. We learn low the nuns, who are supposed to fill the the very air with their goodness, were racked out bag and baggage, with orders never to return. 1113 pries's, oleo, were made to look "alive," and that by their nwu "children." And still more recently, we come to the Oath against Modernist Doctrines. When this was issued a storm of "activity " greeted it. l'riests stood in open revolt. The modems in the Seminary of San Monaco rn fused or blue to the the Oath. Throrgliout Enrore wholesale excommunications wire effnckil on unfortunate priests and professors who dared to question the orders of the Holy See, or who refused to commit moral suicide. In Prussia, the " activity " was great, the Vatican evidm thy not knowing the nature of these ion ud Teutons, among whom .611 moves the spirit of Martin Luther. We witnessed, however, the humiliating spectacle of the Pope withdrawing the Oath from certain priests. " Rome bends where she cannot break." His last few sentences are as digestible as can be expected. I sin uld like my worthy friend to publi,h the names and particulars of twenty ministers of arr Protestant denomination in England, who, during the last three years, have joined this Roman Church. By restricting the period, you sill see my friend wid unable "to qm te such mimes as Newman and kfanning."—Yours &amp;c. JEUNESSE DE WESLEY.</p>
        <p>Sir,— Though cot wishing to intrude myself between the Rector and Froher Whittle on the dogma of Transubstantiation I cannot help nothig the sentence in which the Reverend Father raters to Holy Scripture and its interpretation. I infer by his remark that his Church still considers it sigh: to with- hold the Bible from the unrestricted use of the laity and place an inte.pretation on its pages contrary to the ordinary sense of its meaning. I think the worthy Father and those who think with him make • great mistake, for the most glorious period in the history if our nati, n has been the last three hundred years durieg which we have had the open Bible before us. Our own krciliiehop of Canterbury addressing the King a few days sgo bore excellent testimony to its value when he said . " The growth and rdlergth of the Empire owe much to the English Bilde. It bassweetened fume life ; it has set a standard of pure spec!), it has permeated literature and net; it hm helped to remove social wrongs and t ameliorate condi- tions of labour ; it has on dified the laws of the realm end shaped the national character, and it has fostered international comity and wort wdi among men. Above all, the English Venion of the Bible has made accessible to us the revelation of God our Father in His Son Jesus Chi ist our Lord. . . . We prairie God, not only for the benefits of the Bible to oast generations, hut also because is truths, as long es they are made the standard of life, will preserve the glory of our Empire thr ugh generaticna to come." • Turning to your correspondent " Santini "I must deny his claim Plat the Roman Church is either Catholic or universal as a religious organisstion. The great Founder of Christianity had no nrcesaity to appoint a visible head to His Church on earth, for he bad promised the Iloly Spirit to guide his people, and if we turn to studs, the lives of the Popes we find very little of holy guidance hut a great deal of world- liness wired with sensuality and lust. Proof of this is given in the pai,es of history, for we find as the Papacy rose in power, religion and moral rectdude declined, in fact its aim very early ceased to he religions, the Bible was suppreised, and it became is political organisation. If Ronianists advanced the claim to be the greatest political organisation in the world I would willingly grant their claim, but to be a universal Church--never. I could go over a wide field of history showing the state of our courtly is pre-reformation days,if it were necessary during the reigns rf Henry VII. and VIII. and Mary I. whom Pope Paul IV. culled that "Blessed Daughter " But I will come to more modern days. Let us look over Europe during the first decade of the present century alid .(11110 France firbt. What is the cause of religious strife in France at the present day? Is it not the persistence of Rome to hold a firm grip on the religious comcience while France is equally determined to be mistress in her own house ? Is it not a fact that an organization founded by M. Marc Sanquier during the rontificate of Leo XIII. has been condemned by the present Pope Pius N. yet we are</p>
        <p>It's just like Best Better." This is wbet everyone 86y. 81.1011 t " GOLDIN FLELCIt " MARGARINL.—Adrt.</p>
        <p>t. Id the fkace is infallible? If eon want to see Papal intolerance and the burden- it imprees on a nation you most go to Spain. Article Xi. of the sparest Constitution runs as foil ws The Ile man, Catholic, Apostolic religion is the religion of the Stare. 'I lie lotion takes the obligation to m.intain the cult and its ministers. No one shall be hindered by reason . his religious inions nor in the exercise of his cult, except regmel to tl.e respect due to Christian moral.. Nevertheless nu other mini feetatione or public ceremonies will be tern.ided titan those of the telipion of the State." I Clad, over f this amide Xi. there are ealablished lin Spain Info k after its 10,000,000 inhabitants 58 I dioceses with GI Riede pit or Archbiehops and some 21,1100 roan, s and mitt r digniteries costing the nation £1,G00.000 out of a national budget if little more than .elo,ooltolo and 1h s does not recleVe other large mums paid in fee.. In 1501 available notation gave the nitather of nuns as being 40,034 living in 2,1;511 cone( ate, and 10,630 rennks or brothers in 59; establieltments ; these not only pay I a taxes of any kind. but have en Ablations, c spot et hitiestowards the State, but still they rater the labour market slid cotepete again t the workineelas.es. The Clacch also oaaa ii lat gp quantity of pr toy which pays neither rates lor taxes. 1 Liu, you La, e some idea of the state of ensile is a mutiny dominated by Romanism. The charge of intolerance levelled against Protestants I utterly deny. We as a nation have always endeavoured to tolerate those who differed from us and rue forefathers purchased civil and religious liberty by their blood and sweat and tears. The intolerance of Rome compelled them t enact in eelf-defence those real laws to which "Seminal' objects and as the opportunity came round thee laws have been abolished and cur former enemies have been granted the Fame freedom as ourselves. In regard to the horrors of the stake in the four years reign of Thiry I. two hunched and eighty-eight persons were burnt to death for their teligious con- victims and when Vl.O recall the memories of the loquisition iii Spain and Italy, the revocation of the edict of Nantes, and the massacre of St. Itartholemew's 1•:ve in France, is it to be wondered at tl.at some slight exeesses occurred among Prote-tanta ? Rebid. cur It ly faith, widt its Sabbath., its saectuaries, place in cur land, sirlue and love at our hearth., hope at our death beds, and the blessed words of inspiration dispelling the gloom above oar graves—besides all thia—and how much all this is worth, eternity only can tell—se owe to the Refor- math n every gine:Tee art width distinguishes our nation. If law now reigns where violence formerly prevailed- if the lamp of learning now burns where darkness futmetly blended, if noble cities nw rue where hovels aforetime stood, if tLe ocean is etrewit with cur ships, and the land is covert ii with our factories and our e orkshops— if tut Sag flies on elm at every promontory of the earth and every island of the mea, and if our p-pulation ii Ins ing outwards in mighty waves, and per pling the ample regions of Canada and Audralia —these are but devel- optmnts of the Reformation- the working of that mighty and still unspent impube communicated to cur natio.° three centuries ago —I am Ac., PHIL SQUOD.</p>
        <p>'How are the Mighty Fallen " ! : was one of a "parcel of women " who attended the Town Council meetng on Tuesday hist i for the purp se of witnessing the passing—or ether- wise--of a resolution sent in by the local Wotneu's Suffrage Society in support of the Conciliation Bi.l. From my childhood upwards I have I evened our " City Fathers," and looked up to them with some- thing akin to awe, becaud, of their superior (?) intelligence. supposed good manners, and oft-reiterated business aid it,. litich terms as chief citizens, chief magistrate de. of the " good old town" are as familiar to me as my bread and butter. I bare also for year a back occasionally attended the important meetings of the Women's Liberal Federation and tthers, and if I we!, to t II you what my impressions ere of the difference in the conduct of these influential gather- ings, it would not be in the least degree flattering to the local one. We women are charged ith " talking our heads off," being "na•ty," with knowing nothing about pro- cedure, finance, or the capacity to disown the pros and rolls of a case. But for muddle and make-be here Tuesday night's meeting beats tie record. It was the most personal, snapping, snarling, " you're another" sort of meeting! have ever attended, and to my mind one of the bent arguments in favorer of the election of women to serve on corporate bodice, if only ass restraining influence. Humour said there were Kane plain-clothes men preserd to keep the women in order—if need be—they might more listingly bare turned their attentnns in another direction. At times fuony ? Oh yes! Very. What with mixed metaphors, had grammar and gesticulations, and the pathetic appeals to the press to "keep it oat" (w ilk the exception of two more daring spirits rho got so heated as not to "care a ' hang," and demander! that it should go in "eve'', word ") it proved to be a most amusing entertaio- ', emit _ _ .</p>
        <p>I think I shall go agsin. At the fag end de mote than three hours' Ling-- when they were in a condition to pass anything —they passed our resolution—the remaining members did and we we very grateful to them. But, knowing that tine were a few opponents, we would have liked to hear acme discussion on it.</p>
        <p>Yours, " MORE IN SORROW THAN IN ANGER."</p>
