      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Women Ratepayers and the Suffrage.</head>
        <p>Women Ratepayers and the Suffrage.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln reference- to the resolution on N01110D.3 Suffrage tanned "heartily and unanimously " by the Town Council on Tuesday. I should like to give you particulars of a canvas mode among the Women Itatepaye:s if the Borough. This was undertaken bi members of the Warrington Women's Suffrage Society to try to show the Council that the subject was really praising, and we only had three weeks in which t do it. We asked each woman whether she was in favour of the Conciliation Bill or against it, and those who weir undecided We entered aa neutral. As the ri ault we were able to send the Mayor a petition signed by 618 of the women heuseholdera. -This number is ro per cent, of the total and 71 per cent, of those actually seen and shows clearly that the women of Warrington, who a!ready posse. the Municipal vote, think that they ought also to have the Parlia- mentary vote. This answers the objrction, as far as Warrington is concerned, that" women do not want the vote." . . . ,</p>
        <p>I might also remind you that a canvas of the Parliamentary voters of Warrington made last year resulted in 3175 signatures to a petition asking that the vide might be given to women on the same terms es it is given tir wen. This seems to show that the men of Warrington are also in favour of Women's Salt rage. Ihe particulars of the canvas mly he of interest, so I enclose thenl.</p>
