        <p>- - Tole! 6lB 57 157 92 23 155 41 951228 *Not finished. Yours truly, • HELEN M. RINSOME.</p>
        <p>National Service as the Worker's Charter. Sir,—As I have not argued that the N.S.L. endorses the policy of the aristocratic members of the league in regard to the queativm of the House of Lords, Mn. Poyntz' analogy of Lord H Cecil and Mr. Blatchford is quite beside the point. At the same time, Mr. Poyntz has not producssl a single argument to show how the N.S.L. proposals are going to end privilege. Therefore, we may safely assume that the crnamental titles will still grace the head of the league's list of member.. Mr. Payola points ,no j,ke intended) too Labour Government in Australia adopting compulsory sewice, but this is no evidence that its adoption by us wcyld mean the workers' charter.</p>
        <p>He quote. Swifterland as an example of compulsory scribe carrying with it manhood suffrage, yrt the German people have had compulsory service for years . and still the democratic element in the Reichstag is wholly inadequate in voicing tbeclaime of the workers As regards the question of security my words were " the securi,y from want" for the worker, hut Mr., Poynts rides , if on the question of the security from invasion which has nothing to do with the pre,ent</p>
        <p>Ile refers to the French Railway Strike as " that I ancient bogey," but this strike was an actual fact and is not ancient hut very recent. He declares that the workers will he protected in such contingencies by the Territorial pruv'sion, yet this provision did not prevent Tonypandy being flooded with the roililary during the recent coal all ike. Mr. Poyntv winds up with the quite unworthy gibe ahont •' a little knowledge being a dangerous thing." I will finish by gm ting the words of one whose know- ledge of the condition of the workers of the world is at least a good second to th.it of Mr. Poyntz, viz., Mr.' J. Ramsay Macilonald,the leader of the Labour Party. Speaking on March 19th he aaid It was not the national safety and security That was behind the propa- ganda of the National Service League it was the knowledge that once rro-e the people would I.e put back into their old nos Hon if hewers of wood and , drawers of water "—Yours the ,</p>
        <p>" WORKER."</p>
        <p>The Church of England and Prayers, for the Dead. Sir,— I agree with the Bev, M,'. 'l' Jaye as t r the I desirability of keeping to the point io controverey, but fail to appreciate the relevancy of his dissertation on Protestantism to tie snlijiret of our discussion. I submit that he has entirely failed to prove that the Church of England condemns provers for the departed or that it was the intention of the English Reformer, to do no. Let me remind your readers that the Church of England has never withdrawn the , pallet ion which she freely arid deliberately gave to such prayers in 151:1 and 1549, as eh yen in my last letter. On the only occasion on which it might be thought that she did withdraw it—on the publication of the Second Prayer Book of Edword VI in sire soon was careful to place ou record in the most explicit terms a protest that that was not her inten- tion. Mr Jaye gives no a quotation, in which there is no mention of prayers for the dead, from Cranmer's dying speech. Even if it does bear the construc- t ion Mr. Jute places upon it, I believe that for the purpese of ascertaiuing the true position of the Church of England and of the Reformers, the, open-minded inquirer would attach fur more me ightl lotion caiefully considered judgment of a great c 113- mission of bishops and other drvinee, over which Crammer presided by virtue of his high sffice, than to the isolated stat intent, under peculiarly trying circum- stances, of inn individual. Dint judgment, as those who have followed this correspondence will know, west unmistakably in favour of prayers for the departed. Mr. Jaye thinks that prayere for the dead are opposed to Holy Scripture Such a view finda in sniper t from the Enelish Refortocre of the time of Ede ard VI., who held that they were " agreeable to the Word i f God and the Primitive Church "—than description applied in the Act of Uniformity to the whole of the contents of the First Prayer Book, includ- ing numerous prayera for the dead. I fail to see why Mr. Jaye /should exult in my frank smtement "that there is no direct command in Scripture concern- leg prayers for the departed." The fact that a practice is not specifically commanded in Holy Writ dais not necessarily mean that it is anti-Scriptural or pernicious. For instance, Mr. Jaye is, I presume, a believer in Sunday observance, hut be would be unable to point to a single text in the Bible which teachen us in no many words that the seveuth day— the Jewish Sabbath instiatted by Divine authority —was to be superseded by the first. In justifying the change We appeal to the Primitive Church, believing that the riders and Bishops of the Church w,,uld never have sanctioned it if they had iii t been con- vinced that their action would have commended itself to our Lord. And so it is with regard to prayers for the departed. It surely cannot be unreasonable to suggest that what is the rule of authority in the one rase most lee made the mule in the other. To quote the words of the scholarly and saintly Canon Liddon, "It is strange indeed that people w ill go on con-, stantly appealing to the authority of the Primitive Church and yet ignore, if they do nix deny, the fact that players for the faithful dead ' were as much a part of the Church's life —politic as well as private--as even the worship of our Blessed Lord, much more no than our recogni- tion of certain north,nv of the Canon of the New , Testament." As I emphssi,ed in my last letter, there 1 is strong indirect evidence in the Bible that prayer for the deported was habitual to the Chrietians of the Apostolic age. Notwithatanding Mr. Jaye's authorities. I still hold— and, on I have already said, low backed up by many learned commentators that the whole tone of the passage from St. Paul's 2.nd epistle to Timothy indicates that Onesiphorus was dead when the Apostle offered the petition for him for mercy in the Day cf Judgment With regard to the Homilies, Mr. Jaye doss Iligh ChurAnien— or Ritualists as he prefers torahl them an injustice in stating that they " would repudiate the dr ctrines height in them." He may hardly credit it, but I have actuallt, heard the Homilies quoted with approval iii a' High Church pulpit, when, enjoining such a Scriptural arid Prayer Book custom as fasting or abstinence ! I hope they receive the Berne thorough-going acceptance from Mr. Jaye in one ease as in the other. But Mr. Jaye, I maintain, fails to are things in their true proportion% and because a Homily happens to accord with hi, n:d'ons. he coils it to ur justified preeminence. After all,l the Homilies are only a rollection of sermons con- sidered necessary for the age in which they were composed. According to the Reformation settlement, as I understand it, die Prayer Book set the standard , of doctrine end worship, the decisions of the Undivided Church based on the teaching of Scripture foiraerl the ultimate test of heresy, the articles safe- guarded certain points of doctrine from medieval and Pith century exaggerationand the Homilies suggested to preachers the line of teaching winch the Binhops of that day desired them to follow.Therefore,l am jostitied in saving that when the Homilies nccord with the teaching of the Pray, r Boon we need have no hesita- tion in accepting them But the Prayer Book, far from containing a single word in condemnatii n of prayers for the departed, includes them in one public services. wlrPe the doctrine of the Intermediate Stele, which Mr. Jaye's favourite Homily attacks, in Scrip- tural, Catholic and in accordance with the teachitur of the Church of England. Mr. Jaye rejoices in the fact I that Queen Elizabeth ordered the Homilies to be read in the churches. Iler Majesty, however, appears to , have had misgivings as to whether the Secrnd Book of Homilies in which this discouragement of prayers for the departed is found, did in all respects ienresent the mind of the Church of England, for she withheld her approval of them for twelve mullin and thin only allowed them to be circulated on the urgent entreaties of the Archbishop who was about to under- take his visitation of the pal islice. Si, reover, Queen Elizabeth's l'rimer, published by authority in 1559, contains peelers for the dead! In my last letter I gave two examples of prayers for the departed in the present services of the Church of England. Mr. Jaye queetions the correctness of my interpretation of the Burial Collect but, with all deference, I contend that he has not graaped the meaning of the sentence a hint lie &amp;vele is so simple. The words "we whir all those " must be equivalent to" we and all those," for if not the order of words must have been changed and would have run thus That we may have our perfect consummation and bliss with all those" Ac. My •revr that the burial service applies not merely to the bystanders but to the departed receives important corroboration from the fact that the Presbyterians at the Savoy Conference in MI requested the insertion of the following rubric in the Burial Service : "The exhortation in the Office is intended for the comfort and edification of the living and not for the dead "—a request that was not granted. And did not the Putitans think tint " hero is prayer for the dead arowed and practised in the Church of England" ? It may interest Mr. Jaye to know that I am not a member of the English Church Union. I am. how- ever, a convinced Catholic layman—ti use the word Catholic in loyalty to the l'rayer Book which knows no such modern designation as "Protestant')— anxious that those who faithfully preach doctrines and practices, which, rotwithstanding the prejudices and misconceptions of these days, are unquestionably part of the great heritage of the Church of England shall not be accused of misrepresenting the Anglican posi- tion. As a final word, may I call attention to the fact that the great John Wesley—a priest of the Church of England—in one of his manuscripts to which we may ascribe the date, approximately, of 1741, declared that he considered it his duty "to pray for the faithful departed," maintaining that the practice was ancient, orthodox, justifiable, of " the earliest antiquity, amid the Church of England."—Yours, eta, LAYMAN, Warrit glen, April :eh, UHL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Osborne Judgment.</head>
        <p>The Osborne Judgment.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Will you kindly allow me space to again reply to your correspondent Mr. S. Kenyon re the abtive question. It is only natural that people should like to spend their money as best they think fit, ne matter for what • e-pore, but the point is this : Do they spend tie money fir the good of themselves and the community generally Dues the drunkard or gambler use his money in the way he considers best for the amtry I. No, be does not ; lie has never stopped to consider. That is what we as trade unionists want. We want them to understand their thEy to the State, and the Staten duty to them in return. We want them t r think the question out for themselves, and when the workers of this county ard of all the world do commence to think and rea i.e.e their power, their emancipation will be near at band. I think both your correspondent and myself have strayed away fr nt the print a little. I toot believe Mr. Kenyon has aoy feundatton for saying the Itrento Catholics wruld coerce the Prot stunt minority into suppoi ling their religion in the event of Ireland net- ting Home Rule, and I don't see the two questions bear any analo.Jy—namely the Osborne Judgment versus Roman Catholicism and Protestantism. What we object to as Trade Enimists are Judge- made laws. The Act relating to the power of trade unions empowers us to amp tort our own represen'a- tires on municipal government+, in fact, all politic bodies, yet the judges twist and mould it to their liking, after ite being on the Statute Book for 30 years. We are not allowed to Ilse our funds fir labour representation on our own town councils. What is the result ? A few of the most eelf-sacrificing have to contribute to an indepen dent fund to do this; whereas the Osbornites get oil free, and come in for the benefits which are derived from lab or representation, jest as the non-soeiety man benefits from the sacrifice of the society man who pays into his trade union no that they may tight in an arbitrary may, if possible, for any concession he feels justified in asking for. Yet the non-soeiety man at the name trade reaps the same reward. It is self-interest mik-applied which is the trouble. , The employer who gets elected to the town councils, commences to apply It, but unfortunately, it is for himself and his class. The same thing applies when Ire is elected to the Impel ial Parliament. That is why I believe in payment of members. The workers would be rep•eseated better. As reg trds the figures which Mr. Kenyon votes, if it were adopted, it would only be direct taxation, whereas tinter the present system, we have to ply indirect, due to class legislation.—Yours, J. If. PEMBERTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>LIBERAL ACTIVITY.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL ACTIVITY.</p>
        <p>EXECUTIVE MEETING.</p>
        <p>The Liberal Genersl Executive met et the Lihersl Club on Monday evening. Councillor J. Wilkinson presided over n good nttendance, among whom we re 1— Mr. P. Peacock (depno -chat inv.), Councillor Laker (hon. treasurer), Alderman li:wine, Captain 11. G. Roberts, Messrs. W. Bibby and IV. Clegg (vice- presidents). Apologies for non-attend ince were received from Ale/ire:F. O'Brien, G. S. Millington, S.</p>
        <p>Dankest and John Dodds. telegram was also received (rote Mr. A. IL Cros• field consuming congratillstions on the progress of Liberalism in the Borough. Mr. Prstimierme submitted the Bnancial report which was arm&amp; mous! y adopted. Mr. Jolts lino rigs, of the Gladstone Lengde, gave an interesting address on Liberalism nod mule no isppeal for canvasser., urging upon all present the necessity of becoming subscribers and of devoting their energies to whole-hearted propagation of Libaral principles. tie mentioned that ths ladies intended doing a good work in fact they alrealy had 100 canvassers and 100 more had promised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>LATCHFORD WARD.</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD WARD.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Dodds presided over a meeting of the Latchford Literal Executive end General Committee, held at the Liberal Club on Wedneaday evening. There was a good attendtmce amongst whom were : —Messrs ' W J Walker, .1 Mather, J Ii Waring, W Sohnackenberg, O Wright, l' Broadhuret, II Cox, S Dutton, E H Jones, W J Seregg, 0 Duntestibin, W Monks, E Wynne Jonee, J Lowniles, W llonshell. J Adams, J Distill (Orford Ward, H New and E A Sanders. Mr. Jurists Adams hiving intimuted his intention of going to Vancouver in the near future remarks of apprtiviation for past services and hopes of success were made by the Chairman, Mr E Wynne Jones, and Mr 0 Dunbobbin. Mr Marne suitably replied. Mr. H. New delivered en address on the Tory amend- ments to the land taxes and dealt at some length with some of the main stiegest ions. Questions were asked concerning royaltion and way leaves, and also Into whether the undeveloped land tax and increment duties would not result in the burden falling upon the tenants. In reply Mr. New gave several instances which refuted that standpoint. A very interesting discussion was taken part in by Metiers. E. Wynne Jones, Jas. Ada us, W. J. Walker, Robert Jones and other, 012 l'0121) WARD.</p>
        <p>A well attended meeting of the executive and general committee of Orford Ward Liberals um hold at the Liberal Club on Thursday evening. Councillor John Wilkinson presided and among those present were Alderman Tinnion, PounciPor Ilalliwell. Messrs P Peacock, It Fogg, .1 Bissell, T Levin, .1 E Buckley, A Hoyle, T Connor, J II Archer, .1 T Massey, John Nudge, G Henning, H New and E. A. Senders. Mr. PE eCOCK submitted the rimrt of the Finance Committee, which was adopted. Mr. NEW pointed out in a subsequent discoteion on municipal government that in regard to the increase in the local rates Councillor Wilkinson had rendered the workers a real a .reiCe by a, plainly shoal ig how Pm increese would effect house rent. WOMEN'S TEA .M.EETING.</p>
        <p>The merntere of the Women's Liberal Assccixtion in the Tone Hall end Orford Wards met in Bold-street Wesleynn schoolroom end quite 400 of them enjoyed an excellently served teethe committee ladies presided at , the urns and wise ably ar sliced by auxiliirise from both wards. Afterwards a capital concert was held presided over by Mrs. James Dodds, Among those present WOrek Mr. J.Hushee (of the (Antigone League,London), Mr P Peecock,Councillor Halliwell,Mr Harry New,Mesdarne• Lindley and 'l' Locker (captsine of the respective wards), Pemberton, Osborne, Walker, sod Miss M siker. Apologias for non-attendance were received from Coen- cube Wilkinson, Messrs P 0 Brien, F Lindley and T Looker. The progremixe included the follbeing Items: Song, Miss E. Wilkinson', song and donor, Misses Halliwell and Mosier Harry Dodd ; recitation, Miss Corney ; dance (is character, Misses Howard and threat ; humorist, Master Frirrington ; song, Miss Hewitt ; song, Miss C. Wilkinson ;nosg, Miss Conning- ton; song, Mies Peacock. Mr. Wilke noted as lyceum-</p>
        <p>papist. During tbe evening Mr. IiCG 11E9 delivered an Interesting address on organisation work, and Mrs Ita‘sostr spoke on Women's Suffrage. Votes of thanks to NI rs. Dodds, speakers, aitistes. ward captains and helpers, were passed on the proposition of Mr. Pt..,0, seconded by Counc Ilor II tun, ithr. Mrs. DOT., Tl,poodod.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>Appointment of a Resident Engineer. I Mr. J. J Webster. the engineer for the new War- rington Bridge, had a long interview will the members of the Bridges Committee n Wednesday morning Vf ith regard lo the preliminaty arrangements for the construction of the bridge. The committee approvtd the appointment of Mr. J Mansfisld Cobb, of Louth n. as resident engineer. We understand that Mr. Cubb is a former pupil of Mr. Wehwer and is strongly recommended by hint as a man having had a very aide experience in the con- struction of reinforced concrete sr orbs. . .</p>
        <p>Alderman Smethurst was appointed chairman and the Mayor vice-chairman of the committee. The ccittractor will be in Warrington immediately after the Easter holidays to begin the cmstructiun of the bridge. It was decided that the chairman and vice-chairman of the committee should each be invited to drive a pile to inaugurate the bridge woke. The date for this ceremony ha, not been lined, but the pile_ are already being made.</p>
        <p>• Gaslitter's— Bankruptcy. Charles flees, gasfitter, if 20, Hewitt-street. appeared for his public examination in bankruptcy yesterday morning before Registrar Ridgway. In his evidence he stated that he was for several year, storekeeper to the gas works of the Corporation and some time ago he started business on hi, own account a a gasfitter. During the past six months he had had a utalt in the market. Ile attributed his loadkruptcy to lack of capital, insufficient businerie and law costs. Ilis liabilities were .ti. 177 79. 2d,. and assets 161 65.4 d. The examination was closed.</p>
        <p>A Warrington Centenary Approaching. We would remind our renders that the Centenary of the Warrington Auxiliary of the British and Foreign Bible Society occurs thin year. The celebra- tion will take place in October, and the '22nd of the month is suggested an" Bible Sunday." Mr. Seger Owes is treasurer and Mr. Jr C. Holland the secretary of the Centenary Committee. On the t 9.411 October there will be a big Parr Hall meeting - Loss</p>
