      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>IRISH AFFAIRS. NOTES FOR IRISH READERS.</head>
        <p>IRISH AFFAIRS. NOTES FOR IRISH READERS.</p>
        <p>IRELAND AS AN EXPORTER.</p>
        <p>Dealing with statistics and controlling argument by figures is not, generally speaking, a process which appeals to the popular mind and that we suppose is one reism why the Irish Trade Belot-an, in common with the Irish Finance problem briefly touched on last week, get such scant attention. Vet a great deal of interest attaches to th, se seemingly dry-as-dust com- pilations. Who for example would imagine that after • century's strife and economic chaos torch a, we have I had in Ireland the foreign trade from Irish ports 1, could equal a total of .1.'fi1,000,000. That surmising figure repreeems the minimum value of Ireland's , present export trade. Of this four-fifths is sent to Great Britain and that fact alone is enough to kill lany idea of separation if such an idea existed. A small portion of the remaining fifth, about rue and a quarter million sterling is carried on direct from I Irish to foreign ports It is a possible development ,of the future, waiting only the band of the man with the money, that this direct trade should be increased and that efforts should be made to have as much of that trade shipped in Irish huts, no that Ireland might be the gaimr by the cost and time SR.C,I in avoiding transhipment as practised to-day. Mr. J. I'. Boland, M.P.. Mr. Hugh Law, M.P. and several other members of the Irish Party have busied themselves of recent years in the movement for the organisation and advancement of Irish industries, and a difficulty which they have one Anatole(' has been the lack Gf reliable data on the impart and export trade returns. But steps have been taken, at Mr. Boland's invitation, to comple statintics which shall hoof a wholly reliable eharacter and the figure we have just quoted in taken from a return issural under the new system upon 1 investigation of English Consular Reports and other testa I s mrces. These statistics I show am mast other paints, the interesting fact that nub two PSCMpt..OIIO Ireland now exports I mere is r head of papolation than any other country, In France se,d Germany the ammat Is put at £5 per head, in the routed States it was only .;:.3 17e., whereas lin Ireland the figure in all,. as £l3 Ills. Of course I many tremors play in the consideration of that fact, land we do not of course regard it as •ny safe tent of a 1, people's prosperity. The value of the frisk Trade figures lies in the suggestion that if this degree of development has gone on tinnier crippling conditions, I bow lunch 111010 may ressonably be looked for in the fitter business mditions shich waled certainly be one of the first conserpiencen of the establish next of an Irish Parliameet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>AFTER ROME RULE?</head>
        <p>AFTER ROME RULE?</p>
        <p>One of di, aspects of the Irish queition that is sure to he to the front in coming discassions is that of Ir sh Representation in the lame. ial Parliament at Westminster after a Parliament has been net up in Dahlia. The earlier attempts at Home Rule legpla- tion, it will be horse in mind, had two si lotions of this point. In the one Mr. Gladstone excluded the bish members from Westminster, flail in the later Home Rule Bill provided for Irish representation in the Imperial Parliament. What altitude Mr Redinond will take up alter conaultation with his party on this [natter depends entirely upon what provisions are contained in the BON. Government introduced. Mr. Redmond has steed that the ch ice in felt with English statesmen whether our men come to West- minster as well as woik in Dublin and Mr. Cteil Rhodes it may be recalled sent Mr. Parnell a chip. f £liboo9 in support of the Nationalist movement at the same time begging the Irish Leader to in- jet on Irish Representation at Westminster a beginning of a great Federal Par'isment of Empire. We seep are we must wait and see, hut meanwhile there are one or two con- aide, ations that man be at do an here. Should Ireland get full c intral of all purely Irish affairs the question cin scarcely arise, for 00 one would dream of making Fincli an obviously unilemo- cratic demand as that. Ireland should secure such control, and at the same time retain a Yoko and vote in deciding matters exclusively English and Scotch If on the other hand the peer to levy Customs and ' Excise duties, or any ithee part of Irish taxation is • withheld from the Irish Parliament, then we cannet see how any opposition can be effered to Ireland demanding I continued representation at We,oriiiiister. That representation could only of Course he maintained for the purpose of di-cuseing and voting on taxation preposals such affected Ireland. IRELAND AND TUE EMPIRE.</p>
        <p>On the lager question if Ireland's representation at Westminster for counsels of Imperial import, a view point strennoudy maintained by Mr. Carl Rhodes and that great Irish democrat Michael 'Witt, it is perhaps premitnre as yet to do anything more than conjecture and look forward to the Horne Rule All Round idea translated into legislative act. But even as a speculdion it pISSF sses a peculiar fascination to those Irish minds that ambition for their country a place in tho E upire's Parliament where her tallest influence rosy he exerted for the developnieat of trite Imperialism. To an Englishmin MI6 word, may have a s'r Inge sound coming from an Irishman, but we iv rid point oat that there are eller wit higher definition, of the Imperial idea than that of the Chamberlain.Miluer school. The Itnieri diem, so-called, which engineered the South Afr.can wa,, the Imperialism which. fail ng to defeat the granting of II me Rule t i the Transv.ral, spat venom on the project, the Imperialism that denied eel-government to Irela..d is the tytanity of Jingoism ausiti-t which Ireland hse ever hurled every ounce of National .nergy. But the Imperiali..m we said just now Irishmen were ergot to support in that which is manifested in the truer statesmanship and the nobler conception of freedom whirr in the past few tears has . written cleaner pug-s in thr first Ty of British Imperial policy. And in these worthier and finer I desigt.s. the ceaer Ireland hopes 9‘l. may be permitted , to add the quota of her succour and support.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-04-08_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>GUILD OF HELP.</head>
        <p>THE GUILD OF HELP.</p>
        <p>Deputation to the Health Committee</p>
        <p>It will be remembered that a deputation from the Guild of help waited upon the Health Coinnsittea about I sin months ego.whers certain proposals were myths for I reducing the infantile death-rate. Since that turns two ldy Health V Biters bare been appointed in aldition to Miss Niter. who torn long time bad been doing health visiting. On Wednesday everting further deputation met the Health Comniittwe by appointment and suggestions were made for closer co-operation. After hearing the speekers from the Guild of Help, be Health Committee agreed to appoint several of their number to meet three members of the Guild to discus. in detail the vatiots suggestions which bad been made.</p>
        <p>LECTURE BY MR. W. J. ROWLAND. On Monday. evening Mr. Wilfrid J. Rowland. secre- t try to the OMId of Help, lectured before the War- rington Sdciety members in the Old Academy on " irate sod Organised Cuarity." The paper was followed by an interesting awed useful diacussion. Mr. E. J. Hell presided. Mr. Rots LAND, in the course of a thoughtful addresa, argued that haphazard alma gi•ing was wrung. As citizens we bad set up machinery for dealing with drat it token and it was not good citizenship to net tip mechinary to do a definite service and then without adequate knowledge to attempt to do the same work as individual.. Yet this is happening eve-, day, because we refuse to leave. well-defined sphere in which the Poor I.OIM may work. Ile believed that the man who gives 2d. to a street beggar scarcely ever thought that he was doing his small part to hinder proper public administratiou. Both majority and minority commissionern nelv..cated no exchange of information between the Poor IlAvr autbority and the organised charitior, and this emphasised the need for the organisation of charities. Mr. Rowland costended that the soundest, friendliest, and most scientific method was to be found in the Guild of Help movement, which did not supplant the Poor Law, but co-operated si ith it and with other existing agencies. A complete record of case* would be obtained, and the fullest inveatigation made, while the help given was of • continued and practical uplifting character. Their aim woo to orgaoise voluntary effort, and he culled for help to bridge the great gulf between Disco and Lazarus. The discussion, which wart aintest wholly fa•curalls to the Guild and its methods, was taken part in by the Chairman, Miss Broadbent. Mr Hill, Mr .1 A Itansoine, Mr Madeley, the J 11.11, Alderman Pierpoint sad the Townroe. Mr Rowlsod was heartily thanked for bin paper.</p>
        <p>Why pay 14. Id. and upwerda for Witter, when GOLDEN FLU., " MLR,4OINE can be bought for 81. per pound or I? pools for a la.—Ad•t.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH SWIMMING. ANGLING AND BOATING ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>A very succeasful ession's work was ur..l, rs•iew at the annual meeting of the Stockton Heath and District Swimming, Angling and Boating Association which was I held at the Hall We, Stockton Heath, last evening. In his annual report the secretary (thy. J. It. Sutton) , recalled the openinx of Stoc:.ton Heath Lske by Mrs. Lyon on June Ith. He alas paid tribute to the work of the worthy President, Dr Parker, the originator of the scheme. The gratifying succees of the as•in ming 'department was referred to and it can auggentei that , during the coming serene the itedie• shout I form separate committee *et suggeetions I for the considettation of the general committee. It was pr ipos.l that before :An commenceinent of the Coming no non the Into should hoe irpLieil and cleaned, due roger being paid to ths presArvation of th s fish which were now of anion suitable for the breakfast table. With reference to the a...dings of the lake the banks Pere in excellent order, the trees and plants adding to its beauty. The erection of per msnent costa along the bank would be :completed before the Op.n.ng of next Itertlion ' During the season the dub had 200 menibers including the lidles. Uround season tickets were issued et a price of la each, and the committee Mate' they would be again appreciated. Toe ttsuas of the associstion were due to Messrs. arsenal', Whitley •nd Co., Ltd., and Mr. Piers Egerton Warbur on for the prtctical assistance they hat rendered to the scheme in genernl and to Mr. Arthur Down for Ws very valuable assist- ance. It was hopcd to establish • Swimming Club early in the coming season. Ths report of the Treasurer (ND. F. J. Grocott), showed that there was a balance in lb. bank of £Ol. ye., but Mot this was for a debt which would become di;, on May Ist. The folios; ing officer. were Isle : —President, Dr Parker; ~cruter,, Mr James Melt muse : tressurer, Mr P J Grooott ; committee, Messrs Bedell,Fl /Henley, Cot. Pletche-, Dixon, John Melbouran, C IC B into., A War maws, F Waratert sn. tlcloty, J K Sutton, 'Caldwell, A. Down, J. klyn•kyn, and W Da•eop in.</p>
